\id ISA - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.23
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Isaiah
\toc1 Isaiah
\toc2 Isaiah
\mt1 Isaiah
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \add This is\add* the vision of Isaiah son of Amots), which he saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem in the days of Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah, kings of Judah.
\s1 Yahweh scolds Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 2 Hear, heavens, and listen, earth, because Yahweh has spoken:
\q1 “I’ve raised sons and brought them up,
\q1 ^but they’ve rebelled against me.
\q1
\v 3 The bull knows his owner,
\q1 and the donkey knows its master’s feeding trough,
\q1 ^but Israel doesn’t know—
\q1 my people don’t understand.”
\b
\q1
\v 4 This sinful nation won’t come to a good end.
\q1 these people who excel at disobeying.
\q1 Descendants of those in the habit of doing evil things.
\q1 Children who act corruptly.
\q1 They have abandoned Yahweh.
\q1 They have despised Israel’s holy one.
\q1 They have estranged themselves from him.
\q1
\v 5 Why do you want more beatings?
\q1 Why do you keep rebelling?
\q1 Your whole head is sick,
\q1 and your motivation is weak.
\q1
\v 6 From the sole of the foot up to the head nothing is healthy.
\q1 Bruises and sores and fresh wounds haven’t been cleaned out,
\q1 and they haven’t been bandaged or treated with \add olive\add* oil.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Your country is totally ruined with your cities \add all\add* burnt down.
\q1 Right in front of you, strangers are taking all the harvest from your field—
\q1 leaving devastation and destruction behind.
\q1
\v 8 Zion’s daughter is left \add isolated\add*
\q1 like a hut in a vineyard,
\q1 like a shed in a cucumber field,
\q1 like a besieged city.
\q1
\v 9 If it wasn’t for the fact that Commander-in-chief Yahweh left a few survivors \add behind\add*,
\q1 we would have been \add wiped out\add* like \add both\add* Sodom and Gomorrah.
\b
\q1
\v 10 Hear what Yahweh has to say, \add you who are like\add* Sodom’s rulers.
\q1 Listen to God’s instructions, \add you who are like\add* Gomorrah’s people:
\q1
\v 11 “What do your many sacrifices mean to me?” asks Yahweh.
\q1 “I’ve had enough of the burnt offerings of rams,
\q1 and the fat of fattened animals,
\q1 and I’m not pleased by the blood of bulls or lambs or goats.
\q1
\v 12 Who told you all that you had to appear in front of me?
\q1 Who said that you all should come and trample through my courtyards?
\q1
\v 13 Don’t keep bringing me worthless offerings.
\q1 They smell terribly bad to me.
\q1 New moon \add holidays\add* and sabbaths, calling of assemblies—
\q1 I can’t stand your disobedience mixed with your festive celebrations.
\q1
\v 14 My spirit hates your religious holidays and your feasts on the calendar.
\q1 They’ve become a burden to me that I’m tired of carrying.
\q1
\v 15 So when you spread out your hands, I \add just\add* hide my eyes from you all.
\q1 Even though you multiply prayers, I don’t listen
\q1 \add because\add* your hands are full of blood.
\q1
\v 16 Wash yourselves \add and\add* get cleaned up.
\q1 Remove the evil of your actions from in front of me.
\q1 Stop doing evil things.
\q2
\v 17 Learn to do good.
\q1 Strive for justice.
\q1 Correct the oppressor.
\q1 Serve justice for the fatherless.
\q1 Stand up for the widow \add in court\add*.”
\b
\q1
\v 18 Yahweh says, “Come, now, and let’s work things out together.
\q1 Even though your sins are like scarlet, they’ll become as white like snow.
\q1 Even though they’re red like crimson, they’ll become like wool.
\q1
\v 19 If you’re willing and \add ready\add* to obey,
\q1 you’ll all eat the best \add food\add* from the land,
\q1
\v 20 but if you refuse and rebel,
\q1 war will be what eats you all.
\q1 That’s what Yahweh is telling you.”
\s1 Disobedient Jerusalem
\p
\v 21 How did the faithful city become a prostitute?
\q1 She who was full of justice—
\q1 with righteousness embedded in her,
\q1 ^but now \add ended up as\add* murderers.
\q1
\v 22 Your \add city’s\add* silver has turned into junk metal.
\q1 You wine is \add now\add* mixed with water.
\q1
\v 23 Your leaders are rebels and companions of thieves.
\q1 Everyone loves bribes and kick-backs.
\q1 They don’t defend the fatherless,
\q1 and don’t even think about standing up for the widow.
\b
\q1
\v 24 Here’s the declaration of Israel’s powerful master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh:
\q1 “Oh well, I’ll get relief from my opponents,
\q1 and avenge myself on my enemies;
\q1
\v 25 I’ll turn my actions against you,
\q1 and refine your junk metal with chemicals
\q1 to remove all your impurity.
\q1
\v 26 Then I’ll restore your judges like they were before,
\q1 and your counsellors like they were at the start.
\q1 Afterwards you’ll be a faithful city called ‘The city of integrity’.”
\b
\q1
\v 27 Zion will be set free by justice,
\q1 and those who repent, set free by doing what is right.
\q1
\v 28 But those who rebel or disobey will be shattered,
\q1 and those who abandon Yahweh will come to their end,
\q1
\v 29 because they’ll be ashamed of \add the shrines\add* that you liked at the \add sacred\add* oak trees,
\q1 and you’ll all be disgraced by the gardens that you chose \add to worship idols in\add*.
\q1
\v 30 You’ll all be like an oak tree with withered leaves,
\q1 and like a garden that has no \add access to\add* water.
\q1
\v 31 The strong \add man\add* will be like kindling and his work like a spark,
\q1 and both of them will burn together \add in a fire\add* that no one can put out.
\c 2
\s1 Yahweh’s mountain is central
\r (Mic. 4:1-3)
\p
\v 1 This is the message Amots’s son Isaiah saw concerning Judah and Jerusalem:
\q1
\v 2 Near the end of time, the mountain where Yahweh’s house is
\q1 will be established as the most important mountain.
\q1 It will become more famous than any other hill,
\q1 and all the nations will flow to \add visit\add* it.
\q1
\v 3 Many \add different\add* peoples will come and say,
\q1 “Come on, let’s go up to Yahweh’s mountain—
\q1 to the temple of Jacob’s God,
\q1 →so he can teach us his ways and we can walk in his paths.”
\q1 Because God’s instructions will emanate from Zion,
\q1 and Yahweh’s message will spread out starting from Jerusalem.
\q1
\v 4 He’ll judge between the nations,
\q1 and will settle cases for many \add different\add* peoples.
\q1 They’ll hammer their swords into plough blades,
\q1 and their spears into pruning hooks.
\q1 The nations will stop attacking each other,
\q1 and they won’t go to war again.
\b
\q1
\v 5 You descendants of Jacob,
\q1 come and walk in Yahweh’s light.
\s1 The day of Yahweh’s judging
\q1
\v 6 Yes, you’ve abandoned your people, Jacob’s descendants,
\q1 because they’re too influenced by eastern ideas
\q1 and they practice divination like the Philistines,
\q1 and they make deals with sons of foreigners.
\q1
\v 7 Their land is full of silver and gold,
\q1 and there’s no limit to its wealth.
\q1 The land also is full of horses,
\q1 and there’s no limit to its chariots,
\q1
\v 8 Their land is full of idols.
\q1 They worship the craftsmanship of their \add own\add* hands—
\q1 what their fingers have made.
\q1
\v 9 Then a person will be bowed down,
\q1 and a man will be humbled,
\q1 ^but not forgiven.
\q1
\v 10 Go into \add a cave\add* in the rocks,
\q1 or hide in \add a hole\add* in the ground
\q1 from the terror from Yahweh
\q1 and from the blinding light of his majesty.
\q1
\v 11 Proud people will be humbled,
\q1 and the arrogant will be take a lesson,
\q1 and Yahweh alone will be honoured on that day.
\q1
\v 12 because Commander-in-chief Yahweh \add has chosen\add* a day
\q1 \add for judgement against\add* those who are proud and aloof,
\q1 and against every arrogant person—\add they’ll all\add* be humbled.
\q1
\v 13 \add It’s also against\add* all the high and mighty cedars in Lebanon,
\q1 and all the oak trees in Bashan,
\q1
\v 14 against all the massive mountains
\q1 and all the high hills,
\q1
\v 15 against every high tower
\q1 and fortified wall,
\q1
\v 16 against all the ships from Tarshish
\q1 and all those beautiful sailing ships.
\q1
\v 17 Humankind’s pride will be humbled
\q1 and the arrogance of people will be cut off,
\q1 and only Yahweh will be honoured at that time.
\q1
\v 18 All the idols will be completely eliminated.
\q1
\v 19 Yes, men will go into the caves in the rocks
\q1 and into holes in the ground,
\q1 \add to escape\add* from the terror of Yahweh,
\q1 and from the blinding light of his majesty,
\q1 when he takes action to terrify the earth.
\q1
\v 20 On that day, people will throw away
\q1 their gold and silver idols which they made to worship.
\q1 They’ll throw them into rabbit holes and bat caves.
\q1
\v 21 \add Then they’ll crawl\add* into the crevices in the rocks
\q1 and into the openings in the ragged cliffs,
\q1 \add to escape\add* from the terror of Yahweh,
\q1 and from the blinding light of his majesty,
\q1 when he takes action to terrify the earth.
\q1
\v 22 Don’t put your faith in humankind who need air to breathe,
\q1 because what can they do about anything?
\c 3
\s1 Judgement on Jerusalem and Judah
\q1
\v 1 Yes, listen, the master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh will take supply and support
\q1 away from Jerusalem and from Judah—
\q1 all the supply of bread and of water,
\q1
\v 2 the top warriors and regular soldiers,
\q1 the judges and prophets,
\q1 the diviners and elders,
\q1
\v 3 the unit captains and respected officials,
\q1 the counsellors and expert craftsman,
\q1 and any skillful enchanters.
\q1
\v 4 “Then I’ll put young people in place as their leaders,
\q1 and children will rule over them.
\q1
\v 5 After that, the people will be oppressed—
\q1 one person \add fighting\add* another,
\q1 and a man \add being taken advantage of\add* by his neighbour.
\q1 The lad will insult the elderly,
\q1 and the dishonourable insult the honourable.
\b
\q1
\v 6 Indeed, a man will seize his brother in his \add own\add* father’s house:
\q1 ‘You have a coat \add so\add* be our ruler,
\q1 and this ruin will be under your control.’
\q1
\v 7 That will cause him to shout out,
\q1 ‘I have no intention of helping. I don’t even have enough food and clothes for my own household.
\q1 and in my house there is no bread and there is no clothing.
\q1 Don’t try to make me the ruler of the people.’ ”
\b
\q1
\v 8 Yes, Jerusalem has stumbled,
\q1 and Judah has fallen,
\q1 because their words and actions are against Yahweh,
\q1 defying his royal authority.
\q1
\v 9 You can tell their intentions by the look on their faces,
\q1 and they talk about their disobedience like the people in Sodom did.
\q1 They don’t \add try to\add* hide it.
\q1 Their lives won’t end well because they’ve brought suffering on themselves.
\b
\q1
\v 10 Tell those who do what’s right
\q1 that they’ll be rewarded for their good deeds.
\q1
\v 11 \add But\add* the wicked won’t end well.
\q1 They’ll be repaid \add appropriately\add* for their actions.
\b
\q1
\v 12 Even children will oppress my people,
\q1 and women rule over them.
\q1 Those who guide you lead my people astray
\q1 and confuse your progress to make you follow the wrong path.
\s1 Yahweh judges his people
\q1
\v 13 Yahweh is getting ready to make his case
\q1 and is standing \add ready\add* to judge the peoples.
\q1
\v 14 He will come \add to pronounce\add* judgement on the elders of his people and their leaders:
\q1 “Yes, all of you, you’ve taken all the fruit from the vineyard—
\q1 the plunder from the poor is in your houses.
\q1
\v 15 Why do you crush my people
\q1 and rub the poor people’s faces \add into the ground\add*?”
\q1 \add That’s\add* the declaration of my master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\s1 A warning for Jerusalem’s women
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh said:
\q1 “Because Zion’s daughters are proud,
\q1 and they walk with their chins stuck out and with flirting eyes—
\q1 walking and mincing their steps and tinkling their feet,
\q1
\v 17 Then my master will shave the heads of Zion’s daughters,
\q1 and Yahweh will make their foreheads bald.”
\p
\v 18 At that time my master will remove their fancy ankle bracelets and headbands, crescent \add necklaces\add*
\q1
\v 19 and earrings, bracelets and veils,
\q1
\v 20 scarves, armlets, and sashes, perfume bottles and charms,
\q1
\v 21 rings on their fingers and in their noses,
\q1
\v 22 festive robes and capes, veils and purses,
\q1
\v 23 fine clothes and underwear, and their turbans and shawls.
\p
\v 24 When that happens, there’ll be a stink instead of fragrance,
\q1 a rope instead of a \add nice\add* sash,
\q1 baldness instead of a styled hairdo,
\q1 \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth instead of fancy clothes,
\q1 brands \add on the skin\add* instead of beauty,
\q1
\v 25 Your men will die from the sword
\q1 and your warriors in the battle.
\q1
\v 26 The \add city\add* gates will mourn and wail,
\q1 and she’ll end up \add just\add* sitting their on the ground, alone.
\c 4
\p
\v 1 When that happens, seven women will take hold of one man, saying,
\q1 ‘We’ll eat our food and wear our clothes.
\q1 Just \add let us marry you so\add* we can take your name
\q1 to remove our shame.’
\s1 Yahweh blesses Jerusalem again
\p
\v 2 At that time, Yahweh’s branch will become beautiful and glorious,
\q1 and the produce of the land will be \add a source of\add* pride and delight for what remains of the Israelites.
\v 3 All who remain in Zion, i.e., the remnant in Jerusalem, will then be his people who are destined \add to continue\add* to live.
\v 4 Surely my master will wash away the filth of Zion’s daughters, and clean up the blood stains from the middle of Jerusalem using the spirit of judgement and the spirit of fire.
\v 5 Then over all of Mt. Zion and her assemblies there, Yahweh will place a canopy over all the splendour—creating cloud during the day, and smoke and the brightness of a flaming fire during the night.
\v 6 During the day, it’ll be a shelter that gives shade from the heat, as well as being a cover and place to get out of the storm and rain.
\c 5
\s1 The song about Yahweh’s vineyard
\q1
\v 1 Let me sing, please, to my dearest one,
\q1 a song for my darling about his vineyard.
\q1 My dearest had a vineyard on very fertile hillside.
\q1
\v 2 He dug it over and cleared it
\q1 and planted it with choice vines.
\q1 He built a watchtower in the middle of it
\q1 and also hewed a wine-pressing \add pit\add* in a rocky portion.
\q1 Then he waited for it to produce grapes,
\q1 ^but it produced \add sour\add*, wild grapes.
\b
\p
\v 3 So now, you who lives in Jerusalem
\q1 and man from Judah,
\q1 judge, please, between me and between my vineyard.
\q1
\v 4 What more could have been done for my vineyard,
\q1 that I haven’t \add already\add* done for it?
\q1 I waited for it to produce grapes,
\q1 ^but why did it produce wild grapes?
\b
\q1
\v 5 So now, \add listen\add* please,
\q1 \add because\add* I’ll tell you what I’m going to do to my vineyard:
\q1 I’ll remove the hedge \add around it\add* and burn it in a fire.
\q1 I’ll tear its wall down so people can trample over it.
\q1
\v 6 Then I’ll \add allow it\add* to become overgrown.
\q1 It won’t be pruned or hoed, and thistles and thorns will grow through it.
\q1 Then I’ll command the clouds not to rain on it \add any more\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Yes, Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s vineyard is Israel,
\q1 and the people from Judah are his nice plants.
\q1 Then he waited for justice, but look, slaughter \add came\add*.
\q1 He waited for people to do what’s right, but listen, screaming \add came\add*.
\s1 People’s bad activities
\q1
\v 8 Those who acquire houses won’t end well,
\q1 or those who add to their fields until there’s no room left.
\q1 →Then \add the poor\add* are made to remain alone in the middle of the country.
\q1
\v 9 I heard Commander-in-chief Yahweh say:
\q1 ‘Surely many houses will \add end up\add* empty—
\q1 \add even\add* large and nice ones won’t have anyone living in them.
\q1
\v 10 A large vineyard will produce a measly amount \add of juice\add*,
\q1 and ten baskets of seed will only give a harvest of one basket \add of grain\add*.
\q1
\v 11 Those who get up early in the morning to get a strong drink won’t end well,
\q1 \add yes,\add* those who loiter into the night as wine inflames them.
\q1
\v 12 They celebrate with harp and lyre,
\q2 tambourine and flute and wine,
\q1 ^but they don’t care about anything that Yahweh does,
\q1 in fact, they don’t \add even\add* know what he’s done for them.
\q1
\v 13 Therefore, my people will be exiled because of \add their\add* for lack of understanding,
\q1 and its honourable people are hungry,
\q1 and the parched crowds are thirsty.
\q1
\v 14 Therefore, the grave has enlarged its appetite
\q1 and opened its mouth without limit.
\q1 and its elite and its crowd will descend \add into it\add*,
\q1 along with its noisy crowd and those celebrating.
\b
\q1
\v 15 Everyone will be brought into submission and humbled.
\q1 All the proud people will be humiliated,
\q1
\v 16 and Commander-in-chief Yahweh will be honoured for his justice.
\q1 God will demonstrate his holiness by doing what is right.
\q1
\v 17 Then the lambs will feed in their pastures,
\q1 and fattened \add calves\add* will graze around the houses.
\b
\q1
\v 18 Those who pull in wickedness with cords of lies won’t end well,
\q1 and nor will those who drag disobedience along \add behind them\add* with cart ropes.
\q1
\v 19 They say, ‘Let him hurry,
\q1 let him speed up his work so we can see what he’s doing,
\q1 and let the counsel of the holy one of Israel approach,
\q1 and let it come and let us know.’
\q1
\v 20 Those who call evil good, and good evil, won’t end well.
\q1 Nor will those who make darkness for light and light for darkness,
\q1 or who set bitter as sweet and sweet as bitter!
\b
\q1
\v 21 Those who are wise in their own eyes won’t end well,
\q1 nor those who think they’re clever.
\b
\q1
\v 22 Those who are experts at drinking wine won’t end well,
\q1 nor those whose strength is mixing strong drinks,
\q1
\v 23 or those who take a bribe then say a wicked person is innocent,
\q1 or those that deny justice to innocent people.
\q1
\v 24 Therefore, the roots \add of their families\add* will rot,
\q1 and their flowers will flower away like dust
\q1 just like how a tongue of fire devours stubble \add in a field\add*
\q1 and the dry grass goes up in flame,
\q1 because they’ve rejected Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s instructions,
\q1 and they’ve despised the message from the holy one of Israel.
\q1
\v 25 That’s why Yahweh got very angry at his people,
\q1 and he’s taken action against them and has struck them.
\q1 The mountains tremble and their corpses become like garbage in the streets.
\q1 In all these things, his anger doesn’t subside,
\q1 and he doesn’t withdraw the actions he’s started.
\b
\q1
\v 26 He’ll signal to faraway nations
\q1 and will whistle to those at the ends of the earth.
\q1 →Then wow, how quickly they’ll show up.
\q1
\v 27 Those enemy armies won’t get tired or stumble.
\q1 They won’t stop for a rest or to sleep.
\q1 They never take their weapons off,
\q1 or loosen the straps on their sandals.
\q1
\v 28 Their arrows are sharp,
\q1 and all their bows are strung.
\q1 Their horses have hooves as hard as flint,
\q1 and their chariot wheels are like a windstorm.
\q1
\v 29 Their roar sounds like a lion,
\q1 and they growl like young lions.
\q1 They seize their prey and carry it off,
\q1 →and there’s no rescuer \add in sight\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 30 They will roar above their prey on that day,
\q1 like the roaring of the ocean.
\q1 If \add someone\add* looks across the land they’d only see darkness \add and\add* distress.
\q1 The light has been darkened by the clouds.
\c 6
\s1 The showing of God to Isaiah
\p
\v 1 In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw my master sitting on a tall, elevated throne, and his long robe covered the temple floor.
\v 2 There were ‘serafs’ standing above him. They each had six wings—they used two to cover their faces, two to cover their feet, and two to fly with.
\v 3 They were calling out to each other,
\q1 “Commander-in-chief Yahweh is holy, yes, fully holy.
\q1 The whole earth is filled with his splendour.”
\p
\v 4 Whenever one of them spoke, the sound of their voices make the doorways shake, and the temple was filled with smoke,
\v 5 and I said,
\q1 “This isn’t going to end well for me. Yes, I’m doomed because I am a man with unclean lips,
\q1 and I live among a people with unclean lips,
\q1 and yet my eyes have seen the king: Commander-in-chief Yahweh.”
\p
\v 6 Then one of the ‘serafs’ flew to me. He was holding a hot coal that he’d taken off the altar with \add a pair of\add* tongs.
\v 7 He touched my mouth with that coal and said,
\q1 “Look, this has touched your lips,
\q1 and your guilt has been taken away
\q1 and the price has been paid for your sin.”
\p
\v 8 Then I heard my master ask, “Who will I send? Who will go on our behalf?”
\p “I’m here,” I answered. “Send me.”
\p
\v 9 Then he said,
\q1 “Go and tell that nation:
\q1 ‘Listen closely, but don’t understand,
\q1 and see clearly, but don’t perceive.’
\q1
\v 10 Fatten the heart of this people,
\q1 and dull its ears and dim its eyes.
\q1 Otherwise it might see with its eyes
\q1 and hear with its ears
\q1 and understand with its heart,
\q1 →and turn and be healed.”
\p
\v 11 So I asked, “Until when, master?”
\p And he replied:
\q1 “Until cities are desolate—without inhabitants,
\q1 and the houses are without people,
\q1 →and the land is a ruined wasteland,
\q1
\v 12 and Yahweh has driven the people far away,
\q1 →and the middle of the country is almost totally deserted.
\q1
\v 13 If there’s still a tenth \add of the people\add* in it,
\q1 →then disaster will turn back and burn everything.
\q1 Like an elm or an oak tree that’s been cut down \add but\add* still has a stump—
\q1 →\add so too\add* the sacred remnant is the nation’s stump.”
\c 7
\s1 A message for Ahaz
\p
\v 1 During the reign of King Ahaz (son of Jotham, son of Uzziah) in Judah, King Retsin from Syria and King Pekah (son of Remalyah) from Israel, went up to Jerusalem attack it, but they weren’t able to conquer it.
\v 2 It was reported to David’s descendants that Aram stands by with Israel, and the king and the people trembled just like the trees in the forest shaking in the wind.
\p
\v 3 Then Yahweh told Isaiah, “Please go out with your son She’ar-Yashuv to meet Ahaz at the end of the channel from the upper pool, near the road to the field where they wash clothes,
\v 4 and tell him, ‘Be careful and calm \add and\add* don’t be afraid. Don’t let your courage melt away because of these two tails of smoldering firebrands, i.e., by the anger of Retsin and Aram and Remalyah’s son.
\v 5 Yes, they’ve plotted together against you, saying,
\v 6 “Let’s go up against Judah \add together\add* and terrify her. Let’s break into her and crown Teve’el’s son as king \add right there\add* in the middle of her.” ’
\p
\v 7 However, my master Yahweh says:
\q1 ‘That’s not going to happen.
\q1 They won’t succeed
\q1
\v 8 because Aram’s capital is Damascus
\q1 and Damascus’ leader is Retsin.
\q1 Nevertheless, with sixty-five years Israel will no longer exist as a nation.
\q1
\v 9 Israel’s capital is Samaria.
\q1 and Samaria’s leader is Remalyah’s son.
\q1 If you don’t \add keep\add* believing,
\q1 →then you’ll \add end up\add* not standing.’ ”
\s1 Immanuel will precede the thorns
\p
\v 10 Later, Yahweh spoke again to Ahaz,
\v 11 “Ask your god Yahweh for a sign \add of confirmation\add* for yourself. Request an incredible miracle.”
\p
\v 12 “\add No,\add* I won’t ask,” Ahaz replied. “I refuse to test Yahweh.”
\p
\v 13 Then Isaiah said, “You descendants of David need to listen. It’s bad enough that you’re wearing out the people, but why are you also annoying my god?
\v 14 Because of that, my master will take the initiative himself to choose a sign: listen, a young, virgin woman\f + \fr 7:14 \ft See https://textandcanon.org/does-isaiah-predict-the-virgin-birth.\f* will become pregnant and give birth to a son, and will name him ‘Immanuel’ \add (which means ‘God is with us’.)\add*.
\v 15 He’ll know how to refuse evil and do good by the time that he can eat yoghurt and honey.
\v 16 However, before the child is old enough to do that, the countries of the two kings that you’re afraid of, will become deserted.
\p
\v 17 \add But instead,\add* Yahweh will bring the Assyrian king to \add attack\add* you and your people, and David’s dynasty will be under its greatest stress since Israel broke away from Judah.”
\p
\v 18 When that happens, Yahweh will whistle for a fly that’s in Egypt’s distant streams, and for a bee that’s in Assyria.
\v 19 They’ll all come and settle down in deserted valleys and cracks in the rocks, and in all the thornbushes and watering holes.
\p
\v 20 At that time, my master will use a hired razor from beyond the Euphrates to shave the head and legs of the Assyrian king, and to remove his beard.
\p
\v 21 In those days, a farmer will preserve a \add single,\add* young cow alive, and a couple of sheep.
\v 22 \add However,\add* because they’ll produce so much milk, they’ll produce yoghurt, because everyone left in the country will eat yoghurt and honey.
\p
\v 23 During that period, every block of land with a thousand grapevines that would have been worth a thousand silver coins, will \add just be full\add* of thistles and thorns.
\v 24 Because it’ll be so overgrown with briers, men will hunt there with their bows and arrows.
\v 25 All the hills that had been cultivated, will now be off limits because of all the thistle and thorns, and sheep and cattle will be left there to tread it down.
\c 8
\s1 Isaiah’s son is born
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told me, “Get yourself a large, \add stone\add* tablet and inscribe \add the name\add* ‘Maher-Shalal-Hash-Baz’ \add (which means ‘Quickly plunder and grab the loot’)\add* on it,
\v 2 and I’ll get my reliable witnesses to attest for me: the priest Uriah, and Yeverekyah’s son Zechariah.
\p
\v 3 Then I slept with \add my wife\add*, the prophetess, and she got pregnant and \add in due course,\add* gave birth to a son. Yahweh told me, “Name him Maher-Shalal-Hash-Baz,
\v 4 because before he’s old enough to call out ‘papa’ or ‘mama’, the riches from Damascus and the plunder from Samaria will \add all\add* be taken to be presented to the Assyrian king.
\s1 Invasion by the Assyrians
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh continued to speak to me again, saying,
\v 6 “Judah has refused the gentle waters of Shiloah and \add instead\add* requests help from \add King\add* Retsin and Remalyah’s son \add King Pekah\add*,
\v 7 so therefore my master will send the waters of a large, powerful river onto them: the Assyrian king and all his majestic power. It’ll come like a flood filling all the channels then overflowing all its banks.
\v 8 It will sweep across Judah, flooding yet moving on until it reaches your neck. Its edges will cover your entire country, Immanuel.”
\q1
\v 9 Raise the war cry, peoples, and be terrified,
\q1 and listen, all you distant places in the world.
\q1 Prepare yourselves for war and to be shattered.
\q1 Prepare yourselves for war and to be shattered.
\q1
\v 10 Make whatever plans you want, but they’ll come to nothing.
\q1 Give a presentation but it won’t stand, because God is with us.
\s1 Yahweh warns Isaiah
\p
\v 11 Yes, Yahweh spoke strongly to me, and warned me not to behave like other people, saying,
\v 12 “Don’t say ‘conspiracy’ everyone time that other people do, and don’t be afraid when they’re afraid, and don’t live in dread.
\v 13 Only honour Commander-in-chief Yahweh, and be concerned about him and fear him.
\v 14 He’ll be like your safe retreat. He’ll be \add both\add* the stone that strikes and the rock that makes both Israel and Judah stumble, and like a trap for the inhabitants of Jerusalem.
\v 15 Many people will stumble over that stone and that rock and fall and be broken, or be snared and captured.”
\s1 Warning about consulting mediums
\p
\v 16 Tie up this report and seal up these instructions for my apprentices.
\v 17 I’ll wait for Yahweh \add to act\add*. He’s distanced himself from Jacob’s descendants, but I’ll continue to expect his help.
\p
\v 18 \add Both\add* I \add myself,\add* and the children that Yahweh has given me, are like directions and warning signs for Israel, that come from Commander-in-chief Yahweh who lives on Mt. Zion.
\p
\v 19 When they tell you all to consult mediums and fortunetellers who chirp and mutter, shouldn’t the people consult their god rather than the dead guiding the living?
\s1 The time of sadness
\p
\v 20 Shouldn’t it be the written instructions that they talk about? If they’re not \add following\add* this message, dawn will never arrive for them,
\v 21 and they’ll be distressed and hungry as they pass it by. Then because they’re hungry and angry, they’ll curse their king and their god as they look upwards.
\v 22 They’ll look \add back\add* to the ground, and wow, \add only\add* see trouble and darkness—gloom, distress, and thick darkness thrusting out \add at them\add*.
\c 9
\p
\v 1 However, it won’t not cover over those who are distressed. Previously he humiliated the Zebulun and Naphtali regions, but later, he’ll honour the way of the sea—beyond the Jordan \add river\add*—Galilee of the nations.
\s1 The coming king
\q1
\v 2 The people who walked in darkness have seen a powerful light.
\q1 The light has shone on those who’ve lived in a land of darkness.
\q1
\v 3 You have multiplied the nation itself.
\q1 You’ve increased its happiness.
\q1 They celebrate in front of you
\q1 like the happiness at harvest time.
\q1 Like their excitement when dividing the plunder,
\q1
\v 4 because you’ve shattered the chains on its neck,
\q1 and the stick \add whacking\add* its shoulders.
\q1 You’ve broken the rod of his oppressor
\q1 like when you defeated Midian.
\q1
\v 5 Yes, every \add warrior’s\add* boot treading loudly will be burnt,
\q1 along with every piece of clothing with blood on it,
\q1
\v 6 because a child is born for us.
\q1 A son is given to us
\q1 and the government will be on his shoulder.
\q1 He’ll be called ‘Amazing adviser’,
\q1 ‘Greatest God’, ‘Forever father’, ‘Prince of peace’.
\q1
\v 7 His government will be without borders—
\q1 with peace that’s never broken.
\q1 He’ll rule on David’s throne, and over his kingdom,
\q1 to establish it and sustain it with justice and with fairness from now onwards.
\q1 The zeal of Commander-in-chief Yahweh will do that.
\s1 Yahweh’s anger at Israel
\q1
\v 8 My master sent a message against Jacob,
\q1 and it dealt with Israel,
\q1
\v 9 and all the people will know it—
\q1 Israel and those who live in Samaria.
\q1 With a proud and arrogant heart they’ll say,
\q1
\v 10 “The bricks have fallen, but we’ll rebuild with \add beautiful\add* chiselled stone.
\q1 The sycamores are felled, but we’ll replace them with \add expensive\add* cedar.”
\q1
\v 11 But Yahweh will send \add King\add* Retsin against them as an adversary,
\q1 and will stir up their enemies—
\q1
\v 12 the Arameans on the east and the Philistines on the west,
\q1 and they’ll be a big mouth that’ll gobble Israel up.
\q1 Even then, Yahweh’s anger won’t subside.
\q1 He’ll still be taking action against them.
\b
\q1
\v 13 The people still won’t return to the one who struck them,
\q1 and they won’t strive to know Commander-in-chief Yahweh
\q1
\v 14 so he’ll cut off Israel’s head and tail—
\q1 \add high\add* palm branches and \add low\add* reeds, all on the same day.
\q1
\v 15 Israel’s leaders are that head,
\q1 and the lying prophets are that tail.
\q1
\v 16 These people’s guides have led them the wrong way,
\q1 so the ones being guided were destroyed.
\q1
\v 17 Therefore, my master won’t be happy about their young men,
\q1 nor will he have compassion for their fatherless and their widows,
\q1 because everyone one of them is godless and does evil things,
\q1 and every mouth speaks \add out\add* foolish things.
\q1 With all that going on, his anger never goes away,
\q1 and he continues to take action \add against them\add*
\q1
\v 18 because wickedness burns like fire.
\q1 It burns up the thistles and thorns,
\q1 and it spreads to the trees in the forest
\q1 as smoke swirls up into the air.
\q1
\v 19 Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s anger causes the entire land to be scorched,
\q1 →and then the people become the fuel for the fire
\q1 as \add each\add* man doesn’t \add even\add* spare his own brother.
\q1
\v 20 They devoured on the right but were still hungry.
\q1 They ate on the left, but it didn’t fill them up,
\q1 →\add so\add* everyone ate the flesh on their own arms.
\q1
\v 21 Manasseh \add fought\add* Ephraim, then Ephraim found Manasseh,
\q1 then they fought together against Judah.
\q1 Even through all that, his anger didn’t subside,
\q1 and he continues to take action \add against them\add*.
\c 10
\q1
\v 1 Those who make wicked laws won’t end well,
\q1 nor will administrators who write troublesome edicts,
\q1
\v 2 suppressing the rights of the needy,
\q1 and robbing the poor among my people of justice,
\q1 making widows become plunder,
\q1 and preying on the fatherless.
\q1
\v 3 Then what will you all do for the judgement day,
\q1 and for the destruction coming from far away?
\q1 Who will you all flee to for help \add then\add*,
\q1 and where will you leave your wealth?
\q1
\v 4 All they can do is to crouch among the prisoners,
\q1 or fall among the killed.
\q1 In all these things, Yahweh’s anger doesn’t subside,
\q1 and he continues to take action \add against them\add*.
\s1 Yahweh uses but judges Assyria
\p
\v 5 Assyria won’t end well.
\q1 The rod of my anger and the staff of my rage is in their hands,
\q1
\v 6 I’ve sent their king against an arrogant nation
\q1 and against the people that I’m angry with.
\q1 I order him to plunder the plunder and to prey on the prey,
\q1 and to trample them—trodden down like the mud in the streets.
\b
\q1
\v 7 However, that’s not what’s in his mind,
\q1 and he doesn’t even think about those things,
\q1 because he’s bent on destruction
\q1 and the elimination of not \add just\add* a few nations.
\q1
\v 8 In fact, he says,
\q1 “Aren’t all my commanders \add like\add* kings?
\q1
\v 9 \add We destroyed\add* Kalno liked \add we did at\add* Karkemish,
\q1 Hamat like Arpad, and Samaria like Damascus.
\q1
\v 10 I’ve already punished idolatrous kingdoms,
\q1 and their idols and images were more impressive that those in Jerusalem and Samaria,
\q1
\v 11 so just like I did to Samaria and its idols,
\q1 I’ll will \add also\add* do to Jerusalem and its idols.”
\p
\v 12 In due course, when my master has finished all his work against Mt. Zion and against Jerusalem, I’ll deal with the result of the Assyrian king’s proud plans and the arrogant attitude that he displays,
\v 13 because he says,
\q1 “It was my own strength and initiatives that got me this far,
\q1 and by using my wisdom, because I was discerning.
\q1 Now I’ve removed the boundaries of the peoples,
\q1 and I’ve stolen their treasures,
\q1 and like a powerful animal, I’ve brought down the inhabitants.
\q1
\v 14 My hand has seized the wealth of the nations
\q1 just like someone taking the eggs out of a nest.
\q1 I gathered it from all over the world
\q1 but no one fluttered a wing, or opened a mouth and chirped.
\p
\v 15 Will the axe say it’s greater than the person who swings it?
\q1 Will the saw praise itself more than the one who uses it?
\q1 As if a rod could move those who are lifting it up,
\q1 or a staff could lift up what’s not made from wood.
\b
\q1
\v 16 Therefore, my master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh
\q1 will make his muscular warriors waste away,
\q1 and instead of the Assyrian king getting glory,
\q1 he’ll get his fingers burnt like with a burning flame.
\q1
\v 17 Then Israel’s light will become a fire,
\q1 and its holy one a flame,
\q1 →and it will burn and devour his thorns and his briers in one day.
\q1
\v 18 His beautiful forest and his fruitful land
\q1 will be steadily destroyed,
\q1 like a sick person who wastes away.
\q1
\v 19 There’ll be so few trees left in his forest that \add even\add* a child could count them.
\s1 Only a remnant will return
\p
\v 20 At that time, the remnant of Israel, i.e., the survivors of Jacob’s descendants, will no longer rely on that \add king\add* who makes them suffer, but instead will rely on Yahweh, Israel’s holy one.
\v 21 A remnant of Israel will return to their powerful god.
\v 22 Although the Israelites are \add currently\add* very numerous, \add only\add* a remnant will return. Destruction has been decreed because justice must prevail
\v 23 Yes, complete destruction has been decreed by my master Yahweh and he’s about to apply it throughout the country.
\s1 Yahweh will punish Assyria
\p
\v 24 Because of that, my master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, “My people living in Zion, don’t be afraid of the Assyrians. They’ll beat you with a rod and raise their staff against you like the Egyptians did
\v 25 for a little while longer, and then I’ll be upset with the Assyrians and my anger will lead to their destruction.
\v 26 Commander-in-chief Yahweh will wave a whip against them, like when the Midianites were struck at Orev rock, and wave his staff over the sea like they did leaving Egypt.
\v 27 Then at that time, he’ll lift his load off your shoulders and his yoke from off your neck, and that yoke will be broken as you begin to thrive again.
\s1 Enemy invasion
\q1
\v 28 The enemy has come to Ay, passed through Migron, storing their supplies \add north of Jerusalem\add* at Mikmas.
\v 29 They’ve crossed over the pass. They camped at Geva. Ramah is trembling, and Giveah of Saul has fled.
\q1
\v 30 Cry out for help daughter of Galim,
\q1 pay attention Layshah,
\q1 you’ll suffer Anatot.
\q1
\v 31 Madmenah flees,
\q1 the residents of Gevim are taken to safety.
\q1
\v 32 Yet today he will stand at Nov,
\q1 shaking his fist at the daughter of Zion—
\q1 the hill of Jerusalem.
\b
\q1
\v 33 Listen, my master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh will cut off the branches with a crash,
\q1 and the tallest of the tall will be cut down,
\q1 and the high and mighty will be brought low.
\q1
\v 34 He’ll chop down the thickets in the forest with an axe,
\q1 →and Lebanon will be felled by the mighty one.
\c 11
\s1 The future, peaceful kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then a shoot will come out from the stump of Jesse,
\q1 and a branch from his roots will \add grow to\add* produce fruit.
\q1
\v 2 Yahweh’s spirit will be with him—
\q1 a spirit of wisdom and understanding,
\q1 a spirit of good advice and strength,
\q1 a spirit of knowledge and striving to obey Yahweh.
\q1
\v 3 He’ll be happy to respect and honour Yahweh.
\q1 He won’t judge \add just\add* by what can be seen,
\q1 or make decisions \add just\add* from what can be heard,
\q2
\v 4 but he’ll judge the ordinary people with justice
\q1 and decide fairly for the poor in the world.
\q1 He will strike the earth with the rod of his mouth,
\q1 and with the breath of his lips, he’ll execute the wicked.
\q1
\v 5 His waistbelt will be doing what’s right,
\q1 and the strap for his hips will be faithfulness.
\b
\q1
\v 6 At that time, the wolf will live with the lamb,
\q1 the leopard will lie down with the young goat,
\q1 and the cow and the young lion and the calf,
\q1 →all \add there\add* together, and a little child will lead them.
\q1
\v 7 The cow and the bear will graze together.
\q1 Their young will lie down together,
\q1 and the lion will eat straw like the ox.
\q1
\v 8 A child will play over the cobra’s hole,
\q1 and the toddler will stretch his hand over the viper’s nest.
\q1
\v 9 There’ll be no more pain or destruction on all my sacred mountain,
\q1 because the world will be full of knowledge about Yahweh,
\q1 \add just\add* as the waters cover the sea.
\s1 The return of a remnant
\p
\v 10 Then on that day, Jesse’s root will stand as a signal to the peoples. Nations will search for him, and his residence will be wonderful.
\v 11 At that time, my master will again \add exercise\add* his power to recover a remnant of his people who remain in Assyria, Egypt, Patros, Ethiopia, Eylam, Shin’ar, Hamat, and from \add various\add* islands.
\q1
\v 12 He’ll raise a banner for the nations,
\q1 and gather the Israelites who were banished—
\q1 assembling the Jews who were scattered right around the world.
\q1
\v 13 Israel’s jealousy will go, and Judah’s enemies will be eliminated.
\q1 Israel will no longer be jealous of Judah, and Judah will no longer treat Israel as enemies.
\q1
\v 14 They will swoop down the slopes to attack the Philistines on the west.
\q1 Together they’ll plunder the peoples in the east.
\q1 They’ll take over Edom and Moab,
\q1 and the Ammonites will become their subjects.
\q1
\v 15 Then Yahweh will completely destroy the gulf from the Egyptian sea,
\q1 and wave his hand with his scorching wind over the \add Euphrates\add* river
\q1 →and divide it into seven streams that can be crossed in sandals.
\q1
\v 16 There’ll be a highway for the remnant of his people coming from Assyria,
\q1 just like when Israel came out of Egypt.
\c 12
\s1 Song of praise
\p
\v 1 Then on that day you’ll say,
\q1 “I’ll praise you, Yahweh, because although you were angry at me,
\q1 your anger subsided and you’ve comforted me.
\q1
\v 2 Yes, God is the one who saves me.
\q1 I will trust him and not be afraid
\q1 because my strength and the one I sing about is Yah—Yahweh,
\q1 and he’s rescued me \add from my enemies\add*.
\p
\v 3 Then you’ll cheerfully get water
\q1 from the springs of salvation.
\q1
\v 4 “Give thanks to Yahweh,” you’ll say at that time.
\q1 “Ask him for help.
\q1 Tell all the people groups what he’s done.
\q1 Tell everyone how wonderful he is.
\q1
\v 5 Sing to Yahweh because he’s done amazing things.
\q1 Make sure everyone in the world knows about them.
\q1
\v 6 Yell and shout happily, you inhabitants of Zion,
\q1 because Israel’s holy one is powerful among you.”
\c 13
\s1 Yahweh’s punishment of Babylon
\p
\v 1 \add This is\add* the heavy message about Babylon, which Amots’ son Isaiah saw:
\q1
\v 2 Raise a banner on the bare mountain.
\q1 Call out to them \add and\add* wave your hand,
\q1 and enter the gates of the princes.
\q1
\v 3 I have commanded my sanctified ones
\q1 as well as angrily summoning warriors—
\q1 my boasting, arrogant ones.
\q1
\v 4 There’s a loud noise from the mountains,
\q1 like that from many people.
\q1 It’s the sound of uproar from the kingdoms—
\q1 nations that are gathering.
\q1 Commander-in-chief Yahweh is mustering an army for battle.
\q1
\v 5 They’re coming from a far country,
\q1 from the end of the heavens.
\q1 They’re the instruments of Yahweh’s judgement,
\q1 coming to destroy the entire country.
\q1
\v 6 \add Start\add* wailing because Yahweh’s day is close,
\q1 and destruction from the powerful one will come.
\q1
\v 7 That’s why all hands hang limp,
\q1 and the heart of every person melts.
\q1
\v 8 They’ll have pangs of dismay,
\q1 and agony will seize them like a woman who writhes in labour.
\q1 They’ll stare at their neighbours in astonishment
\q1 with their faces flushed red.
\q1
\v 9 Yes, Yahweh’s day is coming—
\q1 a cruel day with of fierce anger and rage,
\q1 to make the earth a desolate place
\q1 as sinners are destroyed from it.
\q1
\v 10 The stars in the sky and their constellations
\q1 won’t shine their light.
\q1 The sun coming out will be dark,
\q1 and the moon won’t be shining its light.
\q1
\v 11 I will punish the world for its evil
\q1 and the wicked for their disobedience.
\q1 I’ll put an end to the pride of the arrogant,
\q1 and bring down the arrogance of the ruthless.
\q1
\v 12 I’ll make humans more rare than pure gold,
\q1 and people more scarce than the gold from Ofir.
\q1
\v 13 So I’ll make the sky wobble,
\q1 and the earth will be shaken out of its place
\q1 by the fury of Commander-in-chief Yahweh
\q1 on the day of his raging anger.
\q1
\v 14 Then like a gazelle being hunted
\q1 or sheep without a shepherd,
\q1 everyone will turn to their own people group
\q1 and they’ll all flee back to their own countries.
\q1
\v 15 Everyone who’s discovered \add (in Babylon\add* \add )\add* will be run through \add with a spear\add*,
\q1 and everyone who’s caught will be killed by the sword.
\q1
\v 16 Their children will be smashed to pieces right in front of them.
\q1 Their homes will be looted and their wives raped.
\q1
\v 17 Listen, I’m stirring the Medes up against them,
\q1 who don’t value silver or care about gold.
\q1
\v 18 Their arrows will strike down the young men.
\q1 They’ll have no mercy on your infants,
\q1 and won’t show any pity on your children.
\q1
\v 19 \add Yes,\add* that admirable Babylon kingdom,
\q1 the pride of the Chaldeans,
\q1 will be destroyed by God like Sodom and Gomorrah \add were\add*.
\q1
\v 20 It’ll never be inhabited again,
\q1 or lived in from one generation to the next.
\q1 The Arabs won’t even set up their tents there,
\q1 and the shepherds won’t rest their flocks there.
\q1
\v 21 But desert animals will live there,
\q1 and jackals will live in the houses.
\q1 Ostrich chicks will grow there
\q1 and wild goats will romp around there.
\q1
\v 22 Hyenas will howl in its towers,
\q1 and jackals in the nice palaces.
\q1 Her time is coming to an end,
\q1 and her lifetime won’t be extended.
\c 14
\s1 Return from exile
\p
\v 1 Yahweh will have compassion on Jacob,
\q1 and will choose Israel again,
\q1 and settle them on their own land.
\q1 Foreigners will join with them,
\q1 and attach themselves to the Israelite people.
\q1
\v 2 The nations will take them back to their \add own\add* place,
\q1 and in Yahweh’s land, the Israelites will take them as male and female servants.
\q1 They’ll take their captors captive,
\q1 and they’ll rule over their oppressors.
\q1
\v 3 Then at the time when Yahweh gives you a break from your anguish and suffering,
\q1 and from the hard \add labour\add* you did as slaves,
\q1
\v 4 then you’ll taunt Babylon’s king \add with this song\add*,
\q1 “How the oppressor has stopped,
\q1 and how his fury has ceased.
\q1
\v 5 Yahweh has broken the wicked peoples’ staff,
\q1 the golden rod of the rulers.
\q1
\v 6 In your rage, you struck the people groups with never-ending blows,
\q1 ruling the nations in anger—persecuting them without restraint.
\q1
\v 7 \add Now\add* the whole world is in restful peace.
\q1 People have broken out with a shout of celebration.
\q1
\v 8 Even the cypress trees are happy about your demise,
\q1 as the cedars in Lebanon.
\q1 Now that you’re powerless,
\q1 →no one comes up to cut us down.
\p
\v 9 \add Down\add* below, the grave is excited
\q1 waiting for your arrival.
\q1 It wakes up the dead for you—all the kings of the earth,
\q1 raising them from their thrones—all the kings of the nations.
\q1
\v 10 They’ll all speak and tell you
\q1 that you have been made weak like them,
\q1 \add in fact\add* become \add completely\add* like them.
\q1
\v 11 Your reputation has been brought down to the grave,
\q1 along with the sound of your harps.
\q1 Maggots are spread to be your mattress,
\q1 and worms are your blanket.
\p
\v 12 How you’ve fallen from heaven, you shining son of the dawn.
\q1 You’ve been cut down to the ground, you who defeated nations.
\q1
\v 13 You had said to yourself,
\q1 ‘I will climb up to the heavens above God’s stars.
\q1 I will elevate my throne and I will rule on the mountain
\q1 where they assemble on Tsafon’s heights.
\q1
\v 14 I’ll go up above the tops of the clouds,
\q1 I’ll make myself like the most high one.’
\q1
\v 15 Nevertheless you’ve been taken down to the grave—
\q1 to the remotest parts of the pit.
\q1
\v 16 Those who see you \add there\add* will stare at you.
\q1 They’ll ponder your fate—
\q1 if you’re the man who make the earth tremble?
\q1 The one who made kingdoms shake?
\q1
\v 17 The one who turned the world into a wilderness,
\q1 and tore down its cities?
\q1 The one wouldn’t release his prisoners \add of war\add* to go home?
\q1
\v 18 All the kings of the nations have laid down in honour—
\q1 each one in his own tomb,
\q1
\v 19 but you have been thrown out of your grave,
\q1 like an unwanted branch that’s thrown away.
\q1 Those killed with the sword surround you like how clothes wrap around a person.
\q1 They went down to the stones in the pit like a trampled corpse.
\q1
\v 20 You won’t be buried with them because you’ve ruined your land.
\q1 You’ve killed your people—
\q1 the descendants of the wicked will never be mentioned again.
\q1
\v 21 Prepare for the slaughter of his children because of their fathers’ disobedience,
\q1 in case they rise up and possess the earth,
\q1 and fill the world with their cities.
\s1 Yahweh will destroy Babylon
\p
\v 22 Commander-in-chief Yahweh declares, “I will take action against them and erase Babylon’s name and their remnant and their future descendants.
\v 23 I’ll make it into a place where owls live, and pools of water, and I’ll sweep her with the broom of destruction.” That’s the declaration from Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\s1 Destroyed by God the ones from-Asirya
\p
\v 24 Commander-in-chief Yahweh has promised,
\q1 “Everything that I intended will certainly happen,
\q1 and what I’ve planned will come to pass.
\q1
\v 25 To break Assyria in my land,
\q1 and trample them \add down\add* on my hills.
\q1 Its yoke will be lifted from their \add necks\add*
\q1 and its burden lifted from their shoulder.
\q1
\v 26 That’s the plan for the whole world,
\q1 and Yahweh’s hand is ready to strike all the nations.
\q1
\v 27 Commander-in-chief Yahweh has made his plans and who could prevent him?
\q1 His arm is stretched out and who could turn it back?
\s1 Yahweh will destroy the Philistines
\p
\v 28 In the year that King Ahaz died, this message came:
\p
\v 29 All you Philistines, don’t celebrate that the rod that struck you is \add now\add* broken,
\q1 because out of the root of that snake, a viper will grow,
\q1 and its result will be a darting adder.
\q1
\v 30 Then the poor will graze in my pastures,
\q1 and the needy will lie down in safety.
\q1 I will kill your root with famine,
\q1 and it will kill your survivors.
\p
\v 31 Wail, \add you\add* gate. Cry out, \add you\add* city.
\q1 Philistia, all of you will melt away,
\q1 because smoke comes from the north,
\q1 and no \add soldier\add* is alone in his ranks.
\p
\v 32 What will Philistia answer to the messengers from that nation?
\q1 \add Remember\add* that Yahweh has founded Zion,
\q1 and \add it’s\add* in her that the afflicted among his people will find safety.
\c 15
\s1 Yahweh will destroy Moab
\p
\v 1 \add Here is\add* a message for Moab:
\p So, in a night Ar in Moab will be laid waste, destroyed.
\q1 So, in a night Kir in Moab will be laid waste, destroyed.
\q1
\v 2 They’ve gone up to the temple, and Divon up to the hills to weep.
\q1 Moab wails about Nevo and Meydeva.
\q1 All their heads are bald.
\q1 All their beards are cut off.
\q1
\v 3 In their streets they wear \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth.
\q1 On their roofs and in the square, everyone wails,
\q1 →coming down in tears.
\q1
\v 4 Heshbon and El’aleh cry out—their voice is heard as far as Yahats.
\q1 So too, the soldiers from Moab call out as their spirit trembles inside them.
\q1
\v 5 My heart cries out for Moab, those who flee to Tsoar and to Eglat-Shelishiyah,
\q1 because they weep as they climb the Luhit ascent,
\q1 yes, on the road to Horonayim, they cry loudly from their suffering.
\q1
\v 6 The Nimrim spring has dried up so the grass has withered.
\q1 The vegetation has disappeared \add so\add* there’s no more greenery.
\q1
\v 7 Because of that, they \add take\add* the wealth they’ve made and their savings,
\q1 →\add and\add* they carry it away across the stream with the willows.
\q1
\v 8 Yes, the cry has surrounded the Moab region,
\q1 and her wailing has carried as far as Eglayim and Be’er-Elim,
\q1
\v 9 because the Dimon stream is full of blood,
\q1 →However, I’ll bring more on Dimon.
\q1 \add I’ll send\add* a lion for Moab’s survivors,
\q1 and for the remnant of the land.
\c 16
\s1 Moab’s hopeless situation
\p
\v 1 Send a ram to Judah’s ruler
\q1 from Sela in the wilderness to the mountain of the daughter of Zion.
\q1
\v 2 Then just like birds flutter away from a nest that’s disturbed,
\q1 \add so too\add* the Moab women \add will head to\add* the fords at the Arnon \add river\add*.
\q1
\v 3 Bring \add some\add* advice. Make a decision.
\q1 Make some shade that’s dark in the middle of the day.
\q1 Hide the fugitives.
\q1 Don’t expose the refugees.
\q1
\v 4 Let Moab’s refugees live among you.
\q1 Be a shelter for them from the destroyer,
\q1 because the oppressor will give up.
\q1 Destruction will cease.
\q1 The oppressors will disappear from the land.
\q1
\v 5 A king will be established based on loyal commitment.
\q1 A descendant of David who will rule with faithfulness—
\q1 one who judges \add fairly\add* and strives for justice,
\q1 and who quickly does what is right.
\p
\v 6 We’ve heard about Moab’s excessive pride,
\q1 and their haughtiness and arrogance,
\q1 but their boasting is empty.
\p
\v 7 So Moab mourns for itself—all of them.
\q1 They long for the raisin-cakes from Kir-Hareset.
\q1 They’re completely overcome
\q1
\v 8 because Heshbon’s fields have dried up
\q1 as have Sivmah’s grapevines.
\q1 The rulers of nations have trampled down its best grapevines.
\q1 They reached Yazer—wandering through the wilderness.
\q1 Its shoots spread out—passing over the ocean.
\q1
\v 9 So I’ll weep along with those weeping in Yazer for Sivmah’s grapevines.
\q1 I’ll water you with my tears, Heshbon and El’aleh,
\q1 because they’re shouting about your summer fruits and your harvest.
\q1
\v 10 \add People\add* will no longer be happy in their orchards,
\q1 and celebrations will disappear from the vineyards.
\q1 There won’t be any singing or shouting in the wine-pressing \add pits\add*,
\q1 and the shouting of those treading the grapes will cease.
\q1
\v 11 My stomach tightens like a harp \add string when I think\add* about Moab.
\q1 My insides groan about Kir-Hareset.
\q1
\v 12 \add The people of\add* Moab wear themselves out \add praying\add* at their hilltop shrines.
\q1 They go to their temple to pray, but it doesn’t help them.
\p
\v 13 That’s what Yahweh already said about Moab,
\v 14 and now he says that within three years like a hired labourer, Moab’s honour will disappear, because out of their large population, only a small and insignificant group \add will remain\add*.
\c 17
\s1 Bad news for Damascus
\p
\v 1 A message for Damascus:
\q1 Listen, Damascus will cease to be a city
\q1 and will become a heap of rubble.
\q1
\v 2 Aroer’s cities will be abandoned to sheep and goats.
\q1 They’ll lie down there and there’ll be no one \add around\add* to frighten them.
\q1
\v 3 Forts will disappear from Israel
\q1 and Damascus will lose its kingdom.
\q1 The remnant from Syria will be a prize for the Israelites.
\m That’s what Commander-in-chief Yahweh says.
\p
\v 4 At that time, Jacob \add meaning Israel’s\add* \add people)\add* will become insignificant.
\q1 The fat on his body will waste away.
\q1
\v 5 Initially the harvester cut off all the stalks of grain and gathered them together,
\q1 →then an individual \add came along\add* picking up \add any dropped\add* stalks,
\q1 →in the Refaim valley.
\q1
\v 6 There’ll be a few stalks left over,
\q1 like beating an olive tree to ge the two or three olives at the top,
\q1 or four or five on the upper branches.
\m That’s what Israel’s god Yahweh says.
\p
\v 7 At that time humankind will look at their maker,
\q1 and turn their eyes to the holy one of Israel.
\q1
\v 8 They’ll stop trusting the altars that they themselves made,
\q1 They’ll stop noticing the Asherah poles and incense altars crafted with their own fingers.
\p
\v 9 At that time, their fortified cities will be abandoned like a deserted part of the forest,
\q1 and their summits will be abandoned and desolate as Israel advances,
\q1
\v 10 because you’ve forgotten the god who saved you,
\q1 and you didn’t remember the rock that you used for protection.
\q1 →So you sow pleasant plantations
\q1 and plant vine cuttings from a stranger.
\q1
\v 11 You nurture what you’ve planted,
\q1 and in the morning, your seed will have sprouted,
\q1 ^but the harvest will become a heap in a time of sickness,
\q1 and the pain will be incurable.
\s1 The defeat of enemies nations
\p
\v 12 The confusion of many peoples won’t end well.
\q1 they roar like the roaring oceans
\q1 and the nations rumble like the rumble of a large waterfall.
\q1
\v 13 \add Yes,\add* the nations rumble like many rivers,
\q1 but he’ll scold them and they’ll flee away.
\q1 and be chased like how the wind chases chaff over the hills
\q1 \add and\add* like whirling dust blown ahead of a storm.
\q1
\v 14 Then in the evening, oh my, terror!
\q1 ^but it’s gone by morning.
\q1 That’s what happens to those who plunder us,
\q1 and the outcome for those who rob us.
\c 18
\s1 Yahweh’s message to Ethiopia/Cush
\p
\v 1 You won’t end well, you land of whirring wings
\q1 that’s on the far side of Ethiopia’s rivers.
\q1
\v 2 You who send ambassadors across the ocean
\q1 skimming over the water in papyrus vessels.
\q1 You swift messengers, go to the nation where the people are tall and smooth-skinned—
\q1 a people who are feared—a very powerful nation
\q1 from a land that’s divided by rivers.
\p
\v 3 All you inhabitants of the world
\q1 and those who live on the earth,
\q1 look when a signal is raised on the hills,
\q1 and listen when you hear a trumpet sounding.
\p
\v 4 This is what Yahweh told me:
\q1 I’ll remain quite and pay attention from my place,
\q1 like when heat shimmers with light,
\q1 like a cloud of mist in the heat at harvest \add time\add*.
\q1
\v 5 Before the harvest starts, when the blossoming has finished,
\q1 and the flowers are forming into grapes,
\q1 then he’ll cut off the \add unwanted\add* shoots with the pruning knife
\q1 and cut away the \add long\add* tendrils.
\q1
\v 6 They’ll be left there for the birds of prey from the hills,
\q1 and the \add wild\add* animals \add across\add* the land.
\q1 The birds of prey will spend the summer on them,
\q1 and then the animals \add across\add* the land will spend the winter on them.
\p
\v 7 At that time, a gift will be brought for Commander-in-chief Yahweh,
\q1 from those tall, smooth-skinned people who are feared by every nation.
\q1 That very powerful nation whose land is divided by rivers.
\q1 \add They’ll take it\add* to Mt. Zion where Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s name is known.
\c 19
\s1 Yahweh’s punishment for Egypt
\p
\v 1 A message for Egypt:
\p Look, Yahweh’s riding on a swift cloud and he’s coming to Egypt.
\q1 Egypt’s idols will tremble in front of him,
\q1 and its people will lose their courage.
\q1
\v 2 Then I’ll stir Egypt up against Egypt,
\q1 →so brothers will fight their brothers and neighbours against neighbours,
\q1 city against city and kingdom against kingdom.
\q1
\v 3 Egypt’s spirit will be weakened from within
\q1 →and I will confuse its advice,
\q1 so they’ll consult idols and the spirits of the dead,
\q1 as well as mediums and diviners.
\q1
\v 4 Then I’ll hand Egypt over to a cruel master—
\q1 a strong king will rule over them.
\m That’s what the master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says.
\p
\v 5 The waters from the sea will dry up,
\q1 and any river will drop in level and empty out.
\q1
\v 6 Then the low rivers will stink,
\q1 and Egypt’s canals will dry up.
\q1 The reeds and rushes will wither.
\q1
\v 7 There’ll be exposed areas at the river mouth,
\q1 and all the cultivated land along the river
\q1 will turn to dust and cease to exist.
\q1
\v 8 The fishermen will wail and mourn—
\q1 everyone who throws a hook into the Nile will grieve,
\q1 along with all those who spread a fishing net over the surface of the waters.
\q1
\v 9 Flax workers will be ashamed,
\q1 as will those who weave white cloth.
\q1
\v 10 Egypt’s foundations will be crushed,
\q1 and their hired workers will be discouraged.
\p
\v 11 Tsoan \add city’s\add* officials are foolish.
\q1 Pharaoh’s wise counsellors are stupid.
\q1 How could you all tell Pharaoh that you’re the son of wise people—
\q1 a son of ancient kings?
\q1
\v 12 \add So\add* where are your wise advisors now?
\q1 Please let them explain to you
\q1 so they’ll know what Commander-in-chief Yahweh has planned for Egypt.
\q1
\v 13 Tsoan’s officials have become fools.
\q1 Memphis’ leaders have been deceived.
\q1 Their tribal leaders have led Egypt astray.
\q1
\v 14 Yahweh has mixed a confused spirit into them,
\q1 so they’ve misled Egypt is everything it does,
\q1 making them stagger like a drunkard in his \add own\add* vomit.
\q1
\v 15 No one in Egypt can do anything—
\q1 \add whether\add* head or tail, palm branch or reed.
\s1 Egypt will fear Yahweh
\p
\v 16 When that happens, the Egyptians will tremble like women, dreading Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s raised fist held against them.
\v 17 Judah the land of \add Judah)\add* will become an object of terror to Egypt. Whenever they’re reminded \add of Judah\add* they’ll become afraid, because of what Commander-in-chief Yahweh is planning against them.
\p
\v 18 At that time, there’ll be five cities in Egypt where they’ll speak out in Hebrew, and make oaths by Commander-in-chief Yahweh. (One of those cities will be called ‘the city of destruction’.)
\p
\v 19 At that time, there’ll be an altar to Yahweh in the middle of Egypt, and a \add stone\add* pillar to him on \add their shared\add* border.
\v 20 It’ll be for a sign in Egypt and a witness to Commander-in-chief Yahweh, because when they’re oppressed \add and\add* cry out to Yahweh \add for help\add*, he’ll send a saviour and defender for them who will rescue them.
\v 21 Then Yahweh will become known to Egypt, and the Egyptians will know Yahweh that day, and they’ll worship \add him\add* with sacrifices and offerings, and they’ll make a commitment to Yahweh and then they’ll honour it.
\v 22 Then Yahweh will strike Egypt—striking then healing, and they’ll turn to Yahweh and he will take notice of their requests and will heal them.
\p
\v 23 At that time, there’ll be a highway from Egypt to Assyria, so Assyrians will go to Egypt and Egyptians will go to Assyria, and they’ll worship together.
\v 24 Israel will be the third \add in the group\add* with Egypt and Assyria—a blessing in the middle of the region
\v 25 when Commander-in-chief Yahweh will bless them saying, “Blessed be my people Egypt, and Assyria, the work of my hands, and Israel, my inheritance.”
\c 20
\s1 The undressed prophecy about Egypt and Ethiopia
\p
\v 1 In the year \add before\add* the Assyrian King Sargon sent his top army commander to Ashdod, and he attacked and captured it,
\v 2 Yahweh told Isaiah Amots’ son), “Take off the \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth and the sandals that you’ve been wearing.” So he started walking around naked\f + \fr 20:2 \ft This doesn’t necessarily mean that he wore absolutely nothing.\f* and barefoot.
\v 3 Later Yahweh said, “Just as my servant Isaiah has been walking around naked and barefoot for three years, it’s a sign and a prophecy about Egypt and Ethiopia.
\v 4 It’s a sign that the Assyrian king will lead away captives from Egypt and Kush—young men and old, barefoot and naked with their buttocks exposed—to Egypt’s shame.
\v 5 They’ll be discouraged and ashamed because of their \add unfounded\add* confidence in Kush and their boasting about Egypt.
\v 6 Those who live along those coastlines will say, “Listen, we fled there for help because that was our hope to be saved from the Assyrian king—how can we escape \add now\add*?”
\c 21
\s1 Babylon’s coming destruction
\p
\v 1 A message for the wilderness beside the ocean:
\p It comes like whirlwinds sweeping through the desert,
\q1 coming from a nation that induces terror.
\q1
\v 2 I’ve been given a distressing vision.
\q1 The deceitful deceive and the destroyer destroys.
\q1 Go up Eylam. Lay siege, Media.
\q1 I’ll make all her groaning come to an end.
\p
\v 3 That’s why my stomach tightens.
\q1 Pangs of anguish seize me like \add a woman\add* giving birth.
\q1 I’m stunned by what I hear.
\q1 I’m terrified by what I see.
\q1
\v 4 My heart falters—I’m overwhelmed and I shudder.
\q1 The twilight that I longed for has turned into trembling.
\p
\v 5 They’re setting the table \add and\add* spreading out the rugs.
\q1 They’re eating and drinking.
\q1 Take action you officials. Oil up your shields.
\p
\v 6 My master has told me \add to\add* station a watchman who’ll tell what he sees.
\v 7 When he sees a rider \add or\add* a pair of horses, \add or\add* someone riding a camel or a donkey, he must pay attention—very careful attention.
\p
\v 8 Then the watchman\f + \fr 21:8 \ft We follow the Salt/Dead sea scrolls here—the Hebrew has ‘a lion’.\f* calls out,
\q1 “My master, I’ve stood day after day on this watchtower,
\q1 and spent the nights stationed at my post,
\v 9 so listen, a rider is coming with a pair of horses, and he brings the news that Babylon has fallen, \add yes\add* fallen, and all its carved idols lie broken on the ground.”
\p
\v 10 \add Oh\add* my down-trodden \add people\add* who’ve been trampled all over.
\q1 I’ve passed on to you all what I heard from Israel’s god, Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\s1 A message about Edom/Se’ir
\p
\v 11 A message for Dumah:
\p Someone’s calling me from Edom, “Watchman, how long will it be before the night is over?”
\p
\v 12 “The morning’s coming but also the \add next\add* night. If you all want to ask, ask \add now\add*, \add then\add* come back \add again later\add*.”
\s1 A message about Arabia
\p
\v 13 A message for Arabia:
\p You’ll all overnight in Arabia’s forest, you Dedani caravans.
\q1
\v 14 Bring water out to meet the thirsty.
\q1 You who live in Teyma bring \add some\add* food for the fugitives
\q1
\v 15 because they’ve fled from the approaching army with their swords held out in front of them,
\q1 and from the taut bows and from the stress of battle.
\p
\v 16 Yahweh told me \add that\add* in exactly one year from now,
\q1 like the end of the labourer’s contract,
\q1 all the greatness of Kedar \add in Arabia\add* will come to an end.
\q1
\v 17 Only a few of Kedar’s archers will remain from their warriors,
\q1 because Israel’s god Yahweh has spoken.
\c 22
\s1 A message about Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 A message for the Valley of Vision \add (Jerusalem\add* \add )\add*:
\p What’s happening to you all that you’ve all gone up to your \add flat\add* rooftops?
\q1
\v 2 The city is full of noise and commotion.
\q1 The town is so boisterous.
\q1 Those dead bodies weren’t killed in fighting.
\q1 They didn’t die in battle.
\q1
\v 3 All your leaders fled together \add but\add* they were captured without an arrow being shot.
\q1 All the others were captured together, \add even though\add* they had fled far away.
\q1
\v 4 Therefore I said: Don’t look at me!
\q1 I’m weeping bitterly \add so\add* don’t rush to comfort me
\q1 concerning the destruction of my people,
\q1
\v 5 because it’s a day of panic and trampling and confusion
\q1 from the master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh—
\q1 breaking down a wall in the Valley of Vision and crying out to the mountain.
\p
\v 6 Eylam picked up the quiver with chariots and horseriders,
\q1 and Kir uncovered their shields.
\q1
\v 7 Our most productive valleys became full of chariots,
\q1 and the horseriders took position at the \add city\add* gates.
\q1
\v 8 He uncovered Judah’s defences,
\q1 and that day you looked for the weapons in the Forest House.
\q1
\v 9 You all saw that there were many breaches on David’s city,
\q1 and you fetched water from the lower pool.
\q1
\v 10 You all counted Jerusalem’s houses,
\q1 and pulled some down to fortify the city wall.
\q1
\v 11 You all made a reservoir between the two walls for the water from the old pool,
\q1 but you didn’t put your trust in the one who made it
\q1 and didn’t consider its designer from long ago.
\p
\v 12 Then my master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh called for weeping and wailing,
\q1 and for heads to be shaved and for \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth to be worn.
\v 13 But look! \add There’s\add* celebration and gladness.
\q1 They’re killing cattle and slaughtering sheep,
\q1 and eating meat and drinking wine,
\q1 saying, ‘Let’s eat and drink \add today\add* because we’ll die tomorrow.’
\p
\v 14 So now, Commander-in-chief Yahweh has revealed to me: ‘You all won’t be forgiven for that wickedness until you die.’
\s1 A message about Shevna
\p
\v 15 My master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh says:
\q1 Go to the administrator who’s in charge of Shevna.
\q1
\v 16 \add Ask\add* what right you have \add to come\add* here to carve yourself a tomb—
\q1 chiselling a tomb to rest in, out of the tall cliff?
\q1
\v 17 Listen man, Yahweh is about to take firm hold of you,
\q1 and he’s about to hurl you away.
\q1
\v 18 He’ll swing you round and round like a ball,
\q1 tossed out into a wide open land where you’ll die.
\q1 Your impressive chariots will be there,
\q1 \add but\add* what a terrible disgrace for your master’s household.
\q1
\v 19 I will push you out of your official role,
\q1 and he’ll pull you down from your position.
\p
\v 20 Then at that time, I’ll summon my servant Eliakim (Hilkiyah’s son)
\v 21 and I’ll put your robe and sash on him, and give him the authority that you had. He’ll be like a father to those who live in Jerusalem and \add right across\add* Judah.
\v 22 Then I’ll place the key to David’s house on his shoulder and he’ll open the door that no one will be able to shut. Then he’ll shut it, and no one will be able to open it.
\v 23 I’ll fasten him in, like a peg into something solid, and he’ll become a wonderful throne for his father’s house.
\p
\v 24 Then they’ll hang all the great things from his father’s house onto him—the offspring and descendants from the small containers and cups through to the \add largest\add* jugs.
\v 25 Commander-in-chief Yahweh declares that at that time, the peg driven into the firm place will give way, and break off and fall, and the burden that was \add relying\add* on it will fail. Yahweh has spoken.
\c 23
\s1 A message for Tyre and Sidon
\p
\v 1 A message for Tyre:
\p Cry loudly, you ships from Tarshish because your home \add port\add* has been destroyed. It’s been reported to them from Kitim.
\v 2 Be silent you who live along the coast, you traders from Sidon who cross the ocean to be filled.
\v 3 The grain from Shihor across the waters \add and\add* the harvest from the Nile became hers, and it became the commerce of the nations.
\p
\v 4 Be ashamed Sidon because the ocean has spoken.
\q1 The fortress by the sea said,
\q1 ‘I haven’t been in labour or given birth,
\q1 and I haven’t raised young men or young women.’
\p
\v 5 When the report reaches Egypt,
\q1 they’ll be very upset about what happened to Tyre.
\q1
\v 6 Sail over to Tarshish.
\q1 Cry loudly, you who live along the coast.
\q1
\v 7 Is this your happy city that’s been around since ancient times—
\q1 whose feet carried her to settle in a far away place?
\q1
\v 8 Who planned that for Tyre, the bestower of crowns,
\q1 whose traders are princes,
\q1 whose merchants were honoured around the world?
\p
\v 9 Commander-in-chief Yahweh planned it to dishonour the pride from all that splendour—
\q1 to treat the honoured people of the world with contempt.
\q1
\v 10 Go across your land like the river does.
\q1 You daughter of Tarshish, there’s no restraint any more.
\q1
\v 11 He’s stretched his hand over the ocean.
\q1 He’s made the kingdoms tremble.
\q1 Yahweh has commanded Canaan to destroy its fortresses.
\q1
\v 12 He said, ‘You won’t continue celebrating, you oppressed young daughter of Sidon.
\q1 Take action and go across to Cyprus but you won’t get any peace there.’
\p
\v 13 Look at the Chaldeans’ country—those people have come to nothing.
\q1 The Assyrians allocated it to animals that live in the wilderness.
\q1 They erected siege-towers against it—
\q1 demolishing its palaces and turning it into a pile of rubble.
\p
\v 14 Cry loudly, you ships from Tarshish.
\q1 because your fortress has been destroyed.
\p
\v 15 \add After\add* that time, Tyre will be forgotten for seventy years like the lifespan of a single king. At the end of those seventy years, what will happen to Tyre will be like the prostitute in this song:
\q1
\v 16 Take a harp and go around the city, you forgotten prostitute.
\q1 ^Play many songs so that you’ll be remembered.
\p
\v 17 Then at the end of those seventy years, Yahweh will visit Tyre. It’ll return to hiring itself out and to her prostitution with all the world’s kingdoms on this planet.
\v 18 But their earnings and profits will be set apart for Yahweh. It won’t be stored up or hoarded, because it will belong to those who live in Yahweh’s presence for buying plenty of food and beautiful clothes.
\c 24
\s1 The cursing of God of world
\p
\v 1 Listen, Yahweh is about to devastate the world
\q1 and about to leave it in ruins.
\q1 He’ll twist its surface and scatter its inhabitants.
\q1
\v 2 Then the priest \add won’t be any better off\add* than the people,
\q1 the master than his slave,
\q1 the mistress than her maids,
\q1 the seller than the buyer,
\q1 the borrower than the lender,
\q1 \add or\add* the creditor than the debtor.
\q1
\v 3 The world will be totally devastated and completely plundered.
\q1 It’ll be plundered because Yahweh has spoken this message.
\p
\v 4 The land has dried up \add and everything has\add* withered.
\q1 The world has declined and withered.
\q1 Its prominent people declined \add as well\add*.
\q1
\v 5 The world has been defiled by its inhabitants
\q1 because they’ve broken the laws,
\q1 disregarded the decree,
\q1 broken the eternal agreement.\f + \fr 24:5 \ft Perhaps referring to the instructions given to Noah and family in \xt Genesis 9:1.\f*
\q1
\v 6 That’s why a curse has consumed the world,
\q1 and its inhabitants are found guilty.
\q1 →Therefore earth’s inhabitants will decrease
\q1 and \add only\add* a few will be left.
\q1
\v 7 The new wine has dried up—the grapevine has deteriorated.
\q1 All the people who were cheery are \add now\add* groaning.
\q1
\v 8 The happy sound of the tambourines ceases.
\q1 The loud celebrations stop.
\q1 The harp’s cheery \add music\add* has gone silent.
\q1
\v 9 They don’t sing \add and\add* drink wine any more.
\q1 Strong drink \add now\add* tastes bitter when they drink it.
\q1
\v 10 The town is completely disorganised.
\q1 All the homes have been boarded up to keep people out.
\q1
\v 11 There’s an outcry in the streets about the wine.
\q1 Cheeriness has degraded into twilight.
\q1 Happiness has departed from the world.
\q1
\v 12 Desolation has remained in the city.
\q1 The gates will be crushed into ruins.
\q1
\v 13 It’ll be like that all over the world—among the nations
\q1 like when an olive tree is beaten \add to shake the fruit off\add*
\q1 \add or\add* like the few left-over grapes when the grape harvest is finished.
\p
\v 14 They’ll start singing with gusto and with happiness.
\q1 From the west they’ll shout cheerfully about Yahweh’s greatness.
\q1
\v 15 Therefore in the east, praise Yahweh.
\q1 On the islands, praise the name of Israel’s god, Yahweh.
\q1
\v 16 We’ve heard songs from the end of the earth, ‘Praise to the one who’s always fair and right.’
\p Then I said, ‘I’m wasting away—losing weight—I won’t end well.
\q1 The treacherous have been treacherous,
\q1 \add yes,\add* the treacherous have been treacherously treacherous.’
\q1
\v 17 Terror and pit-traps and snare-traps await you,
\q1 you inhabitants of the earth.
\q1
\v 18 The one who runs away from the sound of terror, will fall into the pit,
\q1 and those who get up out of the pits, will be caught in the snare,
\q1 because the windows in the high place have been opened,
\q1 and the earth’s foundations have been shaken.
\q1
\v 19 The earth will be totally shattered.
\q1 The earth will be split completely through.
\q1 The earth will be violently shaken.
\q1
\v 20 The earth staggers around like a drunkard,
\q1 and sways like a hut in a gale.
\q1 Its sin will weigh it down
\q1 →and it’ll fall—never to rise again.
\p
\v 21 At that time, Yahweh will punish the heavenly army
\q1 and the earthly kings on the earth.
\q1
\v 22 They’ll be rounded up and imprisoned in a pit.
\q1 They’ll be shut up in that prison and after a long time, they’ll be punished.
\q1
\v 23 Then the moon will go shy, and the sun will hide in shame,
\q1 because Commander-in-chief Yahweh will reign on Mt. Zion there in Jerusalem
\q1 and with splendour in front of his elders.
\c 25
\s1 A song for praising Yahweh
\p
\v 1 Yes, Yahweh, you’re my god.
\q1 I will honour you and thank your name/reputation
\q1 because you’ve done incredible things,
\q1 planned long back in perfect faithfulness.
\q1
\v 2 Yes, you \add can\add* make a city into a heap \add of rubble\add*,
\q1 \add or\add* a fortified town into ruins.
\q1 You’ve destroyed some strangers’ fortresses—never to be rebuilt again.
\q1
\v 3 So yes, strong people will honour you—
\q1 The towns in ruthless nations will fear you,
\q1
\v 4 because you’ve been a safe place to be shelter for those who are distressed—
\q1 a shelter from the rain and a shade from the hot sun,
\q1 because the breath of ruthless people is like rain beating against a wall,
\q1
\v 5 \add and\add* like heat in a dry land.
\q1 You subdued the uproar from foreigners,
\q1 like the shade of a cloud reduces the heat.
\q1 He’ll quieten down the song of ruthless people.
\s1 Yahweh hosts a banquet
\p
\v 6 Then Commander-in-chief Yahweh will hold a banquet of good things for all that people on that mountain.
\q1 There’ll be vintage wine and choice meat.
\q1
\v 7 Then on that mountain, he’ll swallow up the bedsheet that’s covering all the peoples,
\q1 and the web that’s woven over all the nations.
\q1
\v 8 My master will permanently swallow death up.
\q1 Yahweh will wipe the tears away from every face.
\q1 He’s removed his people’s disgrace from all the earth, because Yahweh has spoken.
\p
\v 9 Then at the time it will be said, ‘Look, that’s our god.
\q1 We put our hope in him, and he saved us.
\q1 \add Yes,\add* that’s Yahweh—we maintained our trust in him.
\q1 We’ll be happy and celebrate how he’s saved us.’
\s1 The cursing of God of Moab
\p
\v 10 Yahweh’s hand rests on this mountain \add to secure it\add*.
\q1 Moab will be trampled down like a heap of straw in a dung pile.
\q1
\v 11 Yahweh will spread out his hands in the middle of it,
\q1 like a swimmer spreading his arms to swim,
\q1 and he’ll cancel their pride with the skill of his hands.
\q1
\v 12 He’ll knock down your high fortress walls,
\q1 and \add Moab’s cities\add* will be broken right down low.
\c 26
\s1 Judah’s victory song
\p
\v 1 A song will be sung in Judah at that time:
\q1 Jerusalem city is strong.
\q1 He gave us walls and ramparts to keep us safe.
\q1
\v 2 Open the gates so a righteous nation can enter—
\q1 one that remains faithful.
\q1
\v 3 The one with their mind made up firmly will have true peace,
\q1 because he puts his trust in you.
\q1
\v 4 Put your trust in Yahweh forever,
\q1 because Yah (Yahweh) is a stable rock.
\q1
\v 5 \add That’s\add* because he humbles those who live in high places.
\q1 He’ll bring low the town that’s been elevated.
\q1 He’ll will lay it low to the ground—levelling it down to dust.
\q1
\v 6 It will be trampled down by feet—
\q1 the feet of the oppressed,
\q1 the footsteps of the poor.
\b
\q1
\v 7 The righteous person’s path is level.
\q1 You, the one who does what’s right, will flatten their paths.
\q1
\v 8 \add We’ve\add* we’ve followed your instructions, Yahweh, we’ve kept our trust in you—
\q1 \add we\add* desire \add to honour\add* your name and reputation
\q1
\v 9 From deep inside me, I have longed for you in the night—
\q1 my spirit within me earnestly waits for you,
\q1 because when people have your instructions,
\q1 the world’s inhabitants learns \add to discern\add* what’s right.
\q1
\v 10 \add If\add* the wicked person is shown favour,
\q1 they won’t learn to do what’s right.
\q1 \add Even\add* in a land where doing right is valued,
\q1 they act unjustly and won’t \add get to\add* see Yahweh’s majesty.
\q1
\v 11 Yahweh, when you take action, they don’t perceive it.
\q1 Let them see and be ashamed by the people’s zeal,
\q1 Also, the fire will consume your enemies.
\b
\q1
\v 12 Yahweh, you’ll establish peace for us,
\q1 because you indeed have taken action on our behalf.
\q1
\v 13 Yahweh our god, we’ve been ruled over by other masters,
\q1 ^but you only, are the one that we honour.
\q1
\v 14 \add Those\add* dead ones won’t live—those departed spirits won’t rise \add again\add*,
\q1 indeed you’ve visited and destroyed them,
\q1 and erased all memory of them.
\q1
\v 15 You’ve enlarged the nation, Yahweh,
\q1 \add yes,\add* enlarged it and gained honour—
\q1 you’ve extended all the land’s boundaries.
\q1
\v 16 Yahweh, when they were distressed,
\q1 the people went to find you with their quiet \add request\add*, when you’d disciplined them.
\q1
\v 17 Like a pregnant woman who’s close to giving birth cries out from her labour pains,
\q1 that’s what we were like in front of you, Yahweh.
\q1
\v 18 \add It’s like\add* we were pregnant and in labour, giving birth to wind.
\q1 We haven’t rescued anyone on the earth,
\q1 and the world’s inhabitants haven’t fallen.
\b
\q1
\v 19 Your dead will live—their corpses will rise.
\q1 Wake up and sing happily, you all who’ve lived in the dust,
\q1 because your dew is the dew \add that gives\add* light
\q1 and the earth will release her dead.
\s1 Judgement is coming then restoration
\p
\v 20 Go my people—enter into your rooms and shut the door behind you.
\q1 Hide a little while until the indignation has passed by.
\q1
\v 21 Listen, Yahweh is about to come out of his place, to punish all the world’s inhabitants,
\q1 and the earth will reveal \add all\add* its bloodshed and will no longer cover over those who were killed.
\c 27
\p
\v 1 At that time, Yahweh will use his extremely powerful sword,
\q1 to punish the slithering sea monster Livyatan—Livyatan the twisting snake-like creature,
\q1 and he’ll kill the monster that’s in the ocean.
\p
\v 2 When that happens, sing about a delightful vineyard.
\q1
\v 3 I, Yahweh, will look after it.
\q1 I’ll \add keep\add* it watered in case someone attacks it.
\q1 I’ll guard it night and day.
\q1
\v 4 I don’t have \add any\add* anger—who will give me thorn bushes?
\q1 I will march against it—I’ll completely burn it up.
\q1
\v 5 If anyone takes advantage of my protection,
\q1 →then they’ll make peace to me—
\q1 let them make peace with me.
\p
\v 6 The (time) is coming when Jacob will take root.
\q1 Israel will blossom and bud,
\q1 and they’ll fill the world with fruit.
\p
\v 7 Has he attacked \add us\add* like he attacked those who attacked \add us\add*,
\q1 or did he kill \add us\add* like he killed those who killed \add us\add*?
\q1
\v 8 By sending her away, you have contended with her.
\q1 He released his fierce breath on an east-wind day.
\q1
\v 9 So Jacob’s disobedience will be made right again by that,
\q1 and this is all the fruit of the removal of his sin:
\q1 when he’ll make all the altar stones like pulverised pieces of chalk.
\q1 No Asherah poles or incense altars will \add remain\add* standing,
\q1
\v 10 because the fortified city is desolate.
\q1 That habitation is deserted and abandoned like a wilderness.
\q1 A calf grazes there, then it will lie down.
\p It will bring its branches to an end—
\q1
\v 11 when its branches dry up, they’ll be broken \add off\add*.
\q1 Women will come and light fires with them because they’re not a people with understanding.
\q1 →Therefore the one who made and formed it, won’t show \add any\add* favour towards it.
\p
\v 12 Then at that time, Yahweh will thresh \add people\add*
\q1 from the Euphrates river \add in the northeast\add* to Egypt’s river \add down in the southwest\add*,
\q1 and you Israelites will be gathered \add in\add* one by one.
\q1
\v 13 Then on that \add same\add* day, a loud trumpet will be blown,
\q1 and those who are perishing in Assyria will come
\q1 along with those who were banished to Egypt,
\q1 and they will bow down to Yahweh on Jerusalem’s sacred mountain.
\c 28
\s1 A warning for Samaria
\p
\v 1 You won’t end well \add Samaria\add*, you proud crown of Israel’s drunkards
\q1 or its beauty \add and\add* splendour that’s fading like a flower \add past its prime\add*.
\q1 There at the head of a lush valley that’s struck down by wine.
\q1
\v 2 Look, the master \add is sending\add* a very powerful one,
\q1 like a hailstorm, a destructive windstorm, or a huge flood.
\q1 He’ll drive it down to the ground with his hand.
\q1
\v 3 The proud crown of Israel’s drunkards will be trampled underfoot.
\q1
\v 4 The fading flower of its beauty \add and\add* splendour at the head of a lush valley
\q1 will be like a \add very\add* early fig before summer.
\q1 The ones who see it will grab it and swallow it.
\p
\v 5 At that time, Commander-in-chief Yahweh will become a beautiful crown,
\q1 and a splendid diadem for the remnant of his people,
\q1
\v 6 and a spirit of justice for those who need judgements,
\q1 and strength for those who turn the battle back at the gates.
\s1 Judah’s drunken prophets
\p
\v 7 Even those reeled from wine
\q1 and stagger from strong drink.
\q1 Priest and prophet reeled from the strong drink,
\q1 and they were confused by from the wine.
\q1 They staggered from the strong drink—
\q1 reeling in vision, they stagger in indecision.
\q1
\v 8 Indeed, all their tables are covered with vomit—
\q1 there’s no place without filth.
\p
\v 9 Who will he teach knowledge to,
\q1 and who will he get to understand the message?
\q1 Those who’ve been weaned from milk—
\q1 who’ve already been taken off the breasts.
\p
\v 10 Because it’s ‘command to command to command, line to line to line, a little there, a little there’.\f + \fr 28:10 \ft The Hebrew for this verse (and v13) includes ‘tsav latsav, tsav latsav, kav lakav, kav lakav,’ etc., suggesting that it might possibly have been intended as drunken gibberish.\f*
\p
\v 11 Indeed, with stammering lips and foreign language,
\q1 he will speak to this people \add group\add*
\q1
\v 12 telling them, ‘This is the resting place \add where\add* the weary ones can rest’,
\q1 and, ‘This is the place for refreshing’.
\q1 but they weren’t willing to listen.
\q1
\v 13 So Yahweh’s message to them,
\q1 ‘command to command to command, line to line to line, a little there, a little there’,
\q1 →so they can go, but they’ll stumble backwards and will be broken,
\q1 plus they’ll become trapped and will be captured.
\s1 A vital foundation for Zion
\p
\v 14 Therefore, listen to Yahweh’s message,
\q1 you people who are always mocking,
\q1 you rulers of the people there in Jerusalem,
\q1
\v 15 because you’ve all said, “We’ve come to an agreement with death,
\q1 and we have an understanding with the grave.
\q1 When the overwhelming scourge goes past,
\q1 it won’t bother us because we’ve made lies our shelter,
\q1 and we hide in deception.”
\q1
\v 16 Therefore, my master Yahweh says:
\q1 Look, I’ve put a \add foundation\add* stone \add in place\add* in Zion—one that’s been tested.
\q1 It’s a valuable cornerstone—a reliable foundation.
\q1 Anyone who trusts \add in it\add* won’t \add have to\add* rush away.
\q1
\v 17 Then I’ll make justice into a measuring stick,
\q1 and righteous into a levelling tool.
\q1 Hail will sweep away the shelter of lies,
\q1 and floods will drown the hiding place.
\q1
\v 18 Then your agreement with death will be dissolved,
\q1 and your understanding with the grave will collapse.
\q1 When the overwhelming scourge comes past,
\q1 →you’ll all be overwhelmed by it.
\q1
\v 19 When it comes past, it’ll take you all
\q1 because it’ll come past morning by morning, \add and\add* day and night.
\p Understanding the message will bring only terror,
\q1
\v 20 because the bed is too short for stretching yourself out on,
\q1 and the woven \add blanket\add* is too narrow to wrap around you.
\q1
\v 21 Yahweh will take action like he did at Mt. Peratsim.
\q1 He’ll stir himself up like he did in Gibeon valley
\q1 to do his work—his special work.
\q1 To perform his task—his special task.
\v 22 So now, don’t show yourselves to be mockers
\q1 in case your chains become heavier,
\q1 because I’ve heard from my master, Commander-in-chief Yahweh
\q1 about complete destruction and the rest that’s been determined against the entire land.
\s1 The wisdom of God
\p
\v 23 Listen carefully to what I say.
\q1 Pay attention to my words.
\q1
\v 24 Does a farmer spend all day ploughing and not sow anything?
\q1 Does he \add just continue\add* to break up and harrow his land?
\q1
\v 25 Doesn’t he level its surface and scatter fennel and sow cumin,
\q1 and put the wheat in rows and the barley in plots, and spelt \add around\add* the border?
\q1
\v 26 Yes, his god instructs him,
\q1 he teaches him to make good decisions.
\q1
\v 27 Fennel isn’t threshed with a threshing board,
\q1 nor is a cart wheel rolled over cumin,
\q1 ^but rather, fennel is beaten with a stick,
\q1 and cumin with a rod.
\q1
\v 28 \add Grain for\add* bread must be ground because anyone threshing it doesn’t keep at it forever.
\q1 He can run his cart wheel over it and his horses don’t crush it.
\q1
\v 29 That too comes from Commander-in-chief Yahweh
\q1 who gives good advice—he has great wisdom.
\c 29
\s1 The siege of Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 You won’t end well Ariel—the town where David camped.
\q1 Year follows year—let the holidays come around,
\q1
\v 2 but I’ll bring distress to Ariel
\q1 and there’ll be mourning and wailing
\q1 and it’ll become like an altar hearth\f + \fr 29:2 \ft Here we use one of the possible translations of ‘ʼArīʼēl’ (rather than transliterating it as a name), but none of those three possible options seem to make a lot of sense.\f* to me.
\q1
\v 3 Then I’ll \add get troops\add* to camp all around you and besiege you—
\q1 building towers and ramps against your fortified walls.
\q1
\v 4 You’ll be brought down and will speak from the ground,
\q1 and your words will come from the dust.
\q1 Your voice will sound like a spirit speaking out of the ground,
\q1 and your speech will whisper from the dust.
\p
\v 5 But the multitude of foreigners will be like fine dust,
\q1 and that mass of ruthless people will be like chaff that gets blown away.
\q1 and it will happen so suddenly.
\q1
\v 6 Commander-in-chief Yahweh will send thunder, an earthquake, and a loud noise,
\q1 a strong gale, a windstorm and a fire burning things up.
\q1
\v 7 It’ll be like a dream—a vision in the night.
\q1 Many of the world’s nations will go to war against Ariel.
\q1 All those will attack its fortifications and put her under distress.
\q1
\v 8 It’ll be like a hungry person dreaming and eating \add in the dream\add*, then waking up with an empty stomach.
\q1 Or like a thirsty person dreaming and drinking \add in the dream\add*, then waking up with a dry throat and feeling faint.
\q1 That’s what it’ll be like for all those nations which go to war against Mt. Zion.
\s1 Compromised prophets
\p
\v 9 Astonish yourselves and be astonished.
\q1 Blind yourselves and be blind.
\q1 Get drunk, but not with wine.
\q1 Stagger, but not from strong drink,
\q1
\v 10 because Yahweh has poured a spirit of deep sleep out onto you all,
\q1 and has shut your prophets’ eyes,
\q1 and has covered your visionaries’ heads.
\q1
\v 11 Then to you all, the entire vision will be like the words sealed in a scroll—
\q1 if it’s handed to a person who’s educated to read out, they’ll say, ‘I can’t, because it is sealed up.’
\q1
\v 12 ^Or if the scroll is given to an uneducated person to read out, they’ll say, ‘I can’t read.’
\p
\v 13 My master says,
\q1 “Yes, those people come close to me with what their mouths say,
\q1 and honour me with lipservice,
\q1 but their heart is far from me,
\q1 and their respect for me is just something that they’ve been taught \add to do\add*.
\q1
\v 14 Because of that, I’ll confound those people again with wonder after wonder.
\q1 The wisdom of the wise will vanish,
\q1 and the intelligence of the intelligent will be hidden.”\f + \fr 29:14 \ft Septuagint: \fqa I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and will hide the intelligence of the intelligent. \ft Cited in \xt 1 Corinthians 1:19\ft .\f*
\s1 Hope for the future
\p
\v 15 Those who keep their plans down deep to hide them from Yahweh, won’t end well,
\q1 and they did what they wanted in dark places, saying, “Who can see us? Who \add could\add* know \add what we’re doing\add*?”
\q1
\v 16 Your thinking is crazy!
\q1 It’s as if the potter’s clay, the thing that was made by him, would be considered to say, ‘He didn’t make me.’
\q1 Or if the pot said to the potter, ‘He doesn’t understand.’
\p
\v 17 Hasn’t it been just a little while,
\q1 and Lebanon will turn into an orchard,
\q1 and the orchard will be considered as a forest?
\p
\v 18 At that time, deaf people will \add be able to\add* hear the words from a scroll,
\q1 and from their gloom and darkness, the eyes of blind people will \add be able to\add* see.
\q1
\v 19 The \add people of low status\add* will be happy again because of Yahweh,
\q1 and the poor people among us will celebrate because of Israel’s holy one,
\q1
\v 20 because ruthless people and mockers will come to an end,
\q1 and all those who plan wicked schemes will be eliminated—
\q1
\v 21 those who give false evidence against a person,
\q1 and lay a trap at the \add city\add* gate for the person who arbitrates,
\q1 and who deprive the innocent person of justice.
\p
\v 22 That’s why Yahweh (who redeemed Abraham) says this to Jacob’s descendants:
\q1 “My people will no longer be ashamed—
\q1 no longer will their faces be pale \add with fear\add*,
\q1
\v 23 because when they see their children who are the result of my work among them,
\q1 they will regard my name and reputation as sacred
\q1 and they will regard me as sacred and will show respect \add to\add* me.
\q1
\v 24 Then those whose spirits go astray, will know understanding,
\q1 and those who murmur will learn insight.
\c 30
\s1 Judah’s worthless treaty with Egypt
\p
\v 1 Yahweh declares that \add you\add* rebellious children won’t end well.
\q1 \add You’re\add* making plans that don’t come from me,
\q1 and pouring out drink offerings but not by my spirit,
\q1 →thus adding disobedience to disobedience.
\q1
\v 2 Those who go down to Egypt didn’t ask my advice,
\q1 \add before\add* trying to reach safety with Pharaoh,
\q1 and trying to find security in Egypt’s shadow.
\q1
\v 3 But Pharaoh’s safety will bring you all shame,
\q1 and Egypt’s security will bring you all humiliation,
\q1
\v 4 because its officials have been in Tsoan,
\q1 and its messengers have reached Hanes.
\q1
\v 5 Everyone will be ashamed because of a nation that won’t benefit them—
\q1 not help, nor advantage, but shame and even disgrace.
\p
\v 6 \add This\add* is a message about the Negev \add desert’s\add* animals:
\q1 In the area of trouble and danger
\q1 from the lioness and the lion,
\q1 and the viper and the flying serpent,
\q1 they carry their wealth on donkeys’ shoulders
\q1 and their treasures on camels’ humps,
\q1 to a nation that can’t help them.
\q1
\v 7 Egypt’s ‘help’ is futile and meaningless,
\q1 →so I’ve called her ‘Rahab who just sits there’.
\s1 The disobedient people
\p
\v 8 Now go to them \add and\add* write it on a tablet—
\q1 inscribe it on a scroll
\q1 →so that it’ll be there for a future day—
\q1 \add something that’ll endure\add* forever
\q1
\v 9 because this is a rebellious nation.
\q1 \add They’re\add* lying children—unwilling to listen to Yahweh’s instructions,
\q1
\v 10 who tell those who see visions, “Don’t see.”
\q1 And tell the prophets, “Don’t show us ‘what’s right’—
\q1 Just flatter us and give us deceptive messages.”
\q1
\v 11 Turn off the road.
\q1 Go on a different path.
\q1 Keep Israel’s holy one away from us.
\p
\v 12 Therefore Israel’s holy one says,
\q1 “Because you’ve all rejected this message
\q1 and have trusted in oppression and perversion and depended on it,
\q1
\v 13 then that disobedience will become your downfall,
\q1 →like a bulge in a high wall that will at some unexpected time, suddenly come smashing down.
\q1
\v 14 It’ll be like the smashing of a clay jar—completely shattered.
\q1 Among the fragments, a person wouldn’t be able to find a piece \add big enough\add* to pick up a hot coal from the fire,
\q1 or to dip some water out of a cistern \add to drink\add*.”
\p
\v 15 So this is what Israel’s holy one says,
\q1 “You’ll all be saved by returning and quietly \add waiting\add*.
\q1 Your strength will \add come from\add* quietness and confidence,
\q1 →but you all weren’t willing.
\q1
\v 16 You all said, ‘Nah, we’ll flee away on horses,’
\q1 and indeed you all will flee,
\q1 and you said, ‘We’ll ride on swift horses,’
\q1 ^but those who chase after you all will prove to be swifter.
\q1
\v 17 A thousand will flee from the threat of \add just\add* one.
\q1 You’ll all flee from the threat of five \add of the enemy\add*
\q1 until you’re left like \add a lone\add* flagstaff on the top of a mountain
\q1 or like a banner \add poked into\add* a hill.”
\s1 Yahweh will bless his people
\p
\v 18 And yet Yahweh is waiting to show favour to you all,
\q1 so he takes action to show you mercy
\q1 because Yahweh is a god of justice.
\q1 Those who wait for him \add to rescue them\add* will be blessed,
\q1
\v 19 because people will live in Zion—in Jerusalem.
\q1 You certainly won’t weep anymore because at the sound of your crying out, he’ll show favour to you.
\q1 As soon as he hears, he’ll answer you.
\q1
\v 20 My master will give you distress for your food
\q1 and oppression for your water,
\q1 and your teacher will no longer hide himself,
\q1 so you’ll see him will your own eyes.
\q1
\v 21 Your ears will hear a command coming from behind you saying,
\q1 ‘That’s the right way to go,’
\q1 so you’ll be guided whether you go to the right or to the left.
\q1
\v 22 Then you all will desecrate your silver-plated idols
\q1 and your gold-sheathed images.
\q1 You’ll throw them away like a menstrual rag
\q1 and you’ll say, ‘\add You piece of\add* filth!’
\q1
\v 23 Then he’ll give you rain for the seed that you’ve planted in the ground,
\q1 →and the soil will produce plenty of food,
\q1 and your livestock will graze in wide pastures at that time.
\q1
\v 24 The bulls and the male donkeys that till the ground
\q1 →will eat human-quality grain that’s been properly winnowed.
\q1
\v 25 On every high mountain and every substantial hill,
\q1 there’ll be streams flowing with water,
\q1 at the time of much slaughter when fortified towers are defeated.
\q1
\v 26 The light from the moon will be like the light from the sun,
\q1 and the sun’s light will be seven times brighter
\q1 at the time when Yahweh bandages up his people’s fracture
\q1 and when he heals their severe wound.
\s1 Yahweh’s judgement for Assyria
\p
\v 27 Look, Yahweh is coming from far away.
\q1 He’s very angry and his weight ascends.
\q1 His lips are full of indignation
\q1 and his tongue is like a consuming fire.
\q1
\v 28 His breath is like a rushing river that comes up to the neck,
\q1 to sift the nations in a sieve that separates out worthlessness,
\q1 and \add with\add* a bridle in the people’s jaws to lead them astray.
\q1
\v 29 You’ll all have your own song like you would at a religious festival,
\q1 and be happy inside like when someone walks to Yahweh’s mountain with their flute—
\q1 going to Israel’s rock \add that gives protection\add*.
\p
\v 30 Then Yahweh will make his majestic voice heard
\q1 and lowering his arm, he’ll reveal his raging anger and the consuming flame,
\q1 \add as well as\add* a windstorm, a rainstorm, and a hailstorm,
\q1
\v 31 because Yahweh’s voice will shatter Assyria—
\q1 he’ll strike them with a rod.
\q1
\v 32 With every stroke from the punishing rod that he puts across them,
\q1 there’ll be tambourines and harps as he fights them in battle—brandishing \add his arm\add*,
\q1
\v 33 because a cremation place was arranged long ago.
\q1 Indeed, it’s been prepared for the \add Assyrian\add* king.
\q1 Its funeral pyre is deep and wide with the firewood piled high.
\q1 Yahweh’s breath will ignite it like a stream of burning sulphur.
\c 31
\s1 Yahweh’s protection of Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 Those who go down to Egypt for help won’t end well.
\q1 They depend on horses and rely on chariots that are many,
\q1 and on cavalry that are very numerous,
\q1 but they don’t look towards Israel’s holy one,
\q1 and they don’t request help from Yahweh.
\q1
\v 2 Yahweh is also wise and he brought calamity.
\q1 He hasn’t changed his mind since his messages
\q1 and he’ll take action against the place of evil-doers
\q1 and against those who do wicked things.
\q1
\v 3 Egypt is human and not a god.
\q1 Their horses are physical and not spiritual
\q1 and Yahweh will stretch out his hand and the ones \add trying\add* to help will stumble,
\q1 and the ones being helped will be defeated,
\q1 →and all of them will die together.
\q1
\p
\v 4 Because this is what Yahweh has told me,
\q1 “As a lion growls and the young lion growls over its prey,
\q1 even if a large group of shepherds shout at it,
\q1 →it’s not put off by their voices
\q1 and it doesn’t cringe because of the commotion they make,
\q1 →so too Commander-in-chief Yahweh will descend to go to war on Mt. Zion and its hill.
\q1
\v 5 Just like birds do, Commander-in-chief Yahweh will protect Jerusalem.
\q1 He’ll defend and rescue it—passing over it and saving it.”
\p
\v 6 You Israelite people, return to the one that you’ve seriously rebelled against,
\v 7 because at that time, everyone will reject their gold and silver idols that you’ve all made with your disobedient hands.
\q1
\v 8 Then Assyria will fall from a sword (but not a human one),
\q1 and a sword (not a human one) will destroy it.
\q1 It will flee away from that sword,
\q1 and its young men will become forced labour.
\q1
\v 9 Their security will pass away from terror,
\q1 and their officers will be dismayed from \add seeing the\add* banner \add flying\add*.
\q1 That’s the declaration from Yahweh who has fire in Zion
\q1 and a furnace in Jerusalem.
\c 32
\s1 Predicting an honest government
\p
\v 1 Listen, a king will reign with fairness,
\q1 and officials will rule with justice.
\q1
\v 2 Each of them will be like a hiding place from the wind
\q1 and a shelter in a rainstorm.
\q1 They’ll be like streams in the desert,
\q1 \add and\add* like the shade from a large rock in an arid land.
\q1
\v 3 Then won’t the eyes of those who see, look,
\q1 and the ears of those who hear, pay attention?
\q1
\v 4 Then the heart of the hasty will understand knowledge,
\q1 and the tongue of stutterers will speak fluently and clearly.
\q1
\v 5 The fool won’t be called ‘noble’ any more,
\q1 and scoundrels will no longer be respected,
\q1
\v 6 because the fool speaks foolishness
\q1 and his heart plans disobedience for doing ungodly things
\q1 and by speaking error about Yahweh.
\q1 He keeps those empty who have a hungry appetite,
\q1 and he deprives those who are thirsty.
\q1
\v 7 As for the scoundrel, evil is his weapon.
\q1 He plans to ruin those \add already\add* suffering with false stories,
\q1 and speaks \add against\add* justice for the needy.
\q1
\v 8 In contrast, the honourable person plans to do honourable things,
\q1 and they will stand on those noble actions.
\s1 Jerusalem’s women in despair
\p
\v 9 Take action, you carefree women.
\q1 Listen to my voice you confident daughters.
\q1 Pay attention to what I have to say.
\q1
\v 10 In just over a year you confident women will be agitated
\q1 because the grape harvest will fail and the \add fruit\add* gathering won’t happen.
\q1
\v 11 Tremble you carefree women.
\q1 Be agitated you confident women.
\q1 Strip yourselves bare then put \add coarse, abrasive sackcloth\add* around your waists.
\q1
\v 12 Beat your breasts \add with grief\add* over the pleasant fields
\q1 \add and\add* over the fruitful grapevines.
\q1
\v 13 My people’s land has thorns and thistles growing up over it—
\q1 even in all the happy homes in the cheery town,
\q1
\v 14 because the palace will be abandoned.
\q1 The busy city will be deserted.
\q1 The hill and the watchtower will forever be like caves—
\q1 a delight for the wild donkeys and a pasture for the flocks
\q1
\v 15 until the spirit is poured \add down\add* on us from up high,
\q1 and the wilderness becomes an orchard,
\q1 and the orchard is considered as a forest.
\q1
\v 16 Then justice will live in the wilderness,
\q1 and righteousness will stay in the orchard.
\q1
\v 17 The result of their doing what’s right will be peace,
\q1 and the service of righteousness will be quietness and permanent security.
\q1
\v 18 Then my people will live peacefully,
\q1 and in secure homes and undisturbed resting places.
\q1
\v 19 It will hail when the forest comes down,
\q1 and the city will be completely levelled.
\q1
\v 20 You all who sow wherever there’s water, will be blessed—
\q1 you all who untie your cow and donkey \add to give them free range\add*.
\c 33
\s1 Yahweh pushes Assyria away
\p
\v 1 The destroyer won’t end well, but you haven’t been destroyed.
\q1 The one who acts treacherously hasn’t been dealt with treacherously.
\q1 When you finish destroying, \add that’s when\add* you’ll be destroyed.
\q1 When you’ve stopped dealing treacherously, \add that’s when people\add* will deal treacherously with you.
\p
\v 2 Yahweh, show your favour to us because we’ve waited for your \add help\add*.
\q1 Be our strength each morning as well as our rescuer when trouble comes.
\q1
\v 3 The peoples flee when they hear the commotion.
\q1 When you took action, the nations were scattered.
\q1
\v 4 You all gather the plunder like locusts—
\q1 like locusts swarming in over it.
\p
\v 5 Yahweh is praised because he lives in a high place.
\q1 He will rule Zion by doing what’s both right and fair.
\q1
\v 6 He will be your constant source of stability—
\q1 a large supply of security, wisdom, and knowledge.
\q1 Respect and honour for Yahweh will be its treasure.
\p
\v 7 Look, their warriors have cried outside—
\q1 the messengers of peace weep bitterly.
\q1
\v 8 The highways are deserted—
\q1 no one is going past on the paths.
\q1 Agreements are broken, people avoid cities, no one values people.
\q1
\v 9 The land has dried up and become unproductive.
\q1 Lebanon is ashamed \add and\add* has withered.
\q1 The Sharon \add plain\add* is like the desert,
\q1 and Bashan and Carmel are losing their leaves.
\s1 Yahweh warns his enemies
\p
\v 10 “Now I’ll take action” says Yahweh.
\q1 “Now I’ll be praised.
\q1 Now I’ll be elevated.
\q1
\v 11 You’ll all conceive chaff \add and\add* give birth to stubble.
\q1 Your breath is a fire that will devour you all.
\q1
\v 12 The people will be burnt down into ashes,
\q1 \add like\add* thorn bushes cut down and burnt \add in a heap\add*.
\q1
\v 13 You who are far away, listen to what I’ve done.
\q1 ^and you all who are nearby, feel my strength.”
\p
\v 14 The sinners in Zion are very afraid—
\q1 trembling has seized the godless.
\q1 “How can we stay \add in this place\add* with a devouring fire?
\q1 Which of us can live with continual burning?”
\q1
\v 15 \add The one who\add* does what’s right,
\q1 and is honest in what they say,
\q1 who rejects dishonest gain from extortion,
\q1 and who avoids shaking hands on a deal involving a bribe,
\q1 who refuses to listen to \add ideas of\add* murder,
\q1 and who won’t let his eyes look into evil \add schemes\add*,
\q1
\v 16 that person will live in high places—
\q1 the mountain fortress will be their refuge.
\q1 They’ll have a reliable source of food and water.
\s1 The ruling king
\p
\v 17 Your eyes will see the king in his beauty.
\q1 They’ll see a distant land.
\q1
\v 18 Your heart will recall the terror.
\q1 “Where’s the \add taxation\add* accountant? Where’s the one doing the weighing?
\q1 Where’s the one who counted the towers?”
\q1
\v 19 You’ll no longer see those fierce people
\q1 whose language is too confusing to comprehend
\q1 \add and\add* whose sounds are too foreign to understand.
\q1
\v 20 Look at Zion, the town of our festivals.
\q1 Your eyes will see Jerusalem—a place to live undisturbed.
\q1 No tents will be packed up and no tent pegs will ever be pulled out,
\q1 and none of its ropes will be snapped.
\q1
\v 21 Yahweh the majestic one will be there with us—
\q1 a place with rivers and wide canals.
\q1 No ship that’s rowed will be able to go over it,
\q1 and no huge sailing ships will pass along it.
\q1
\v 22 Yes, Yahweh is our judge—he’s our commander.
\q1 Yahweh is our king—he’ll rescue us.
\q1
\v 23 Your ropes are coming undone—they can’t secure the base of the mast.
\q1 They can’t spread the sail out.
\q1 →Then the large booty onboard will be divided as plunder—
\q1 even lame people will take some spoil.
\q1
\v 24 None of the residents will say that they’re sick—
\q1 the people who live there will be forgiven for their disobedience.
\c 34
\s1 Yahweh punishes his enemies
\p
\v 1 Come close you nations to listen.
\q1 and you peoples pay attention.
\q1 The earth and everything that fills it must listen—
\q1 the world and everything that it’s produced.
\q1
\v 2 Yahweh is very angry at all the nations
\q1 and furious at all their armies.
\q1 He’ll completely destroy them—
\q1 he’ll hand them over to be slaughtered.
\q1
\v 3 Then their slain will be tossed out
\q1 and the hills will soak up their blood,
\q1 and their corpses will release a stench.
\q1
\v 4 All the stars in the sky will rot away,
\q1 and the skies will be rolled up like a scroll.
\q1 All their objects will wither like leaves falling off a grapevine,
\q1 and like withered fruit \add dropping\add* off a fig tree.
\p
\v 5 Once my sword will have drunk its fill in the skies/heavens,
\q1 look, it will descend on Edom and on the people who \add will receive\add* total destruction as my judgement.
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh’s sword is full of blood and fattened with fat
\q1 from the blood of lambs and goats, and from the fat from rams’ kidneys,
\q1 because Yahweh has a sacrifice in Batserah,
\q1 and a great slaughter in the nation of Edom.
\q1
\v 7 Wild bulls will go down with them,
\q1 as well as young bulls and powerful ones.
\q1 Their land will drink its fill of blood,
\q1 and the dust will be smeared with fat,
\q1
\v 8 because Yahweh will have his day of vengeance—
\q1 a year of retribution for Zion’s sake.
\p
\v 9 Its streams will be changed into tar,
\q1 and its dust into sulfur,
\q1 and its land will become burning pitch.
\q1
\v 10 It’ll burn night and day and its smoke will permanently go up.
\q1 It’ll be a wasteland throughout the generations
\q1 and no one will ever pass through it again.
\q1
\v 11 Desert owls and hedgehogs will take it over—
\q1 owls and ravens will live there.
\q1 Yahweh will stretch the measuring line of ruin over it
\q1 and plumb lines of destruction.
\q1
\v 12 Its leading citizens will have nothing left to call a kingdom,
\q1 and all its princes will become nobodies.
\q1
\v 13 Thorns will grow up its fortresses.
\q1 Nettles and thornbushes will grow on its high walls,
\q1 and it’ll become a place for jackals to live in—
\q1 grass for ostriches.
\q1
\v 14 Wild animals will meet hyenas,
\q1 and the wild goats will bleat to each other.
\q1 The lilith owl\f + \fr 34:14 \ft See article on ‘lilith’ at https://www.sil.org/resources/publications/entry/111035.\f* will rest there,
\q1 and find a place for itself to nest.
\q1
\v 15 Owls will nest and lay eggs there,
\q1 and hatch and brood the chicks in its shade.
\q1 Indeed the hawks will gather there with their mates.
\p
\v 16 Look through Yahweh’s scroll and read—not one of them will be missing.
\q1 Not one will lack its mate because Yahweh has commanded it,
\q1 and his spirit will gather them \add there\add*.
\q1
\v 17 He has thrown dice for them,
\q1 and his hand divided out their territory with a measuring line.
\q1 It’ll be theirs forever—they’ll live there throughout the generations.
\c 35
\s1 The happiness of those saved by God
\q1
\v 1 The wilderness and the desert will celebrate,
\q1 and the Aravah \add plain\add* will be happy and blossom like a lily.
\q1
\v 2 It will blossom widely and be glad,
\q1 even with happiness and cheery shouting.
\q1 Lebanon’s glory will be given to it,
\q1 as well as the splendour of Carmel and Sharon.
\q1 They will see Yahweh’s brightness—the splendour of our god.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Strengthen the limp hands,
\q1 and steady the stumbling knees.
\q1
\v 4 Tell those with anxious hearts,
\q1 “Be strong—don’t be afraid.
\q1 Listen, your god will come with vengeance—
\q1 paying them back for what they’ve done.
\q1 →He himself will come and rescue you.”
\b
\q1
\v 5 Then the blind will be able to see again,
\q1 and deaf people will be able to hear.
\q1
\v 6 Then the lame person will leap like a deer
\q1 and the one who’s mute will shout happily
\q1 because springs will burst out in the wilderness,
\q1 and streams in the Aravah \add plain\add*.
\q1
\v 7 The parched ground will become a pool,
\q1 and the thirsty place will become springs of water.
\q1 The place where jackals lived and relaxed
\q1 →will grow grass, reeds, and papyrus.
\b
\q1
\v 8 There’ll be a highway to travel on
\q1 →and it’ll be called ‘the sacred road’.
\q1 The ‘unclean’ won’t use it
\q1 but it’ll be for those walking in the right direction—
\q1 fools won’t \add be allowed\add* to wander onto it.
\q1
\v 9 There’ll be no lions there
\q1 and no dangerous wild animals on it.
\q1 Those \add creatures\add* won’t be found there,
\q1 but those people who’ve been bought back will walk on it.
\q1
\v 10 Then the people ransomed by Yahweh will return
\q1 and they’ll come to Zion with a cheerful shout.
\q1 They’ll always be happy and gladness and celebration will overwhelm them,
\q1 and sorrow and groaning will disappear.
\c 36
\s1 Assyria threatens Jerusalem
\r (2 Kgs 18:13-27; 2 Chr. 32:1-19)
\p
\v 1 In the fourteenth year of \add the reign of\add* King Hezekiah, Assyria’s King Sennacherib attacked Judah’s fortified cities and captured them.
\v 2 Then from Lakish, the Assyrian king sent his top negotiator to King Hezekiah in Jerusalem with a large army. He stopped by the aqueduct at the upper pool on the road to the field where women wash clothes,
\v 3 and Hilkiyah’s son Eliakim, the palace supervisor, went out to meet him, taking Shevna the administrator, and Asaf’s son Yoah the secretary.
\v 4 The negotiator instructed them, “Tell Hezekiah that Assyria’s powerful king wants to know who or what you think you’re trusting in to rescue you?
\v 5 Your plan and war preparations are only hot air, so who are you relying on \add that encourages you\add* to rebel against me?
\v 6 Listen, if it’s Egypt, \add that’s like\add* trusting a staff made from a splintered reed. If a man were to lean on it, it would pierce his palm—that’s what relying on Egypt’s King Pharaoh is like for those who put their trust in him.
\v 7 But if you tell me that you’re relying on your god Yahweh, hasn’t Hezekiah removed his hilltop shrines and altars, and \add forced all\add* Judah to worship him in front of the altar there in Jerusalem?
\p
\v 8 “So now, make a deal with my master, the Assyrian king. Let me give you two thousand horses if you can \add find that many\add* riders for them.
\v 9 How could you \add even\add* repel a captain who’s one of the least of my master’s servants since you’ve been relying on Egypt for chariots and horsemen to rescue you?
\v 10 Also, listen, I didn’t come here to destroy this region without Yahweh—\add it was\add* Yahweh who told me to come here and destroy this nation.”
\p
\v 11 Then Eliakim and Shevna and Yoah asked the negotiator, “Please speak Syrian to your servants, because we understand it. Don’t speak to us in Hebrew in the hearing of the people who are \add up\add* on the wall.”
\p
\v 12 But the negotiator replied, “\add Do you think that\add* my master sent me to give this message \add just\add* to you and your master? Don’t you think that it’s also for the men sitting on the wall \add who’ll have to\add* join you in eating their \add own\add* dung and drinking their \add own\add* urine?”
\p
\v 13 Then the negotiator stood \add and looked up\add*, and shouted in Hebrew, “Listen to this message from the great Assyrian king.
\v 14 The king says not to let your \add king\add* Hezekiah deceive you all, because he won’t be able to save you.
\v 15 Don’t let him make you rely on Yahweh by telling you all that Yahweh will rescue you, and that this city won’t be captured by the Assyrian king.
\v 16 \add No,\add* don’t listen to Hezekiah, because the Assyrian king says that if you all surrender and come out here, you’ll be allowed to continue to harvest your own grapevines and fig trees, and everyone will be able to drink the water from their own wells
\v 17 until I come and take you to a land like your own \add that has\add* grain and new wine—a land with bread and vineyards.
\p
\v 18 “Don’t let Hezekiah mislead you all by saying that Yahweh will rescue you. Did the gods of \add any other\add* nations saved them from the Assyrian king’s power?
\v 19 Where were Hamat and Arpad’s gods? Where were Sefarvayim’s gods? Did any gods save Samaria from my power?
\v 20 From all those regions, which of their gods saved them from my power, \add so what makes you all think that\add* Yahweh will save Jerusalem from me.”
\p
\v 21 But those on the wall kept silent and didn’t answer him, because King \add Hezekiah\add* had ordered them not to respond.
\v 22 Then Eliakim (Hilkiyah’s son who was the palace supervisor) and Shevna (the administrator) and Yoah (the secretary) returned to Hezekiah with their clothes torn \add in grief\add* and passed the negotiator’s messages on to him.
\c 37
\s1 Hezekiah consults Isaiah
\r (2 Kgs 19:1-7)
\p
\v 1 When King Hezekiah heard the report, he torn his clothes \add too\add*, and covered himself with \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth and went into Yahweh’s residence.
\v 2 Then he sent Eliakim (the palace supervisor) and Shevna (the administrator) and the elders of the priests to \add go to\add* Isaiah, the son of Amots the prophet,
\v 3 and they told him, “\add King\add* Hezekiah says that today is a day of distress and rebuke and disgrace, like when children go to their mother who’s about to deliver her baby but she doesn’t have the strength to give birth.
\v 4 Perhaps your god Yahweh heard the messages from the negotiator who was sent by his master, the Assyrian king, to defy the living god. Perhaps Yahweh will rebuke \add him for\add* the words that Yahweh heard. So send up a prayer for the remnant of us here.”
\p
\v 5 When King Hezekiah’s servants got to Isaiah,
\v 6 he told them to tell their master, “Yahweh says not to be afraid because of the words that you heard when the Assyrian king’s servants insulted me.
\v 7 Listen, I’ll put a spirit in him and he’ll hear a report \add that he needs\add* to return to his own country, and then I’ll cause him to be assassinated at home.”
\s1 A further threat from Assyria
\r (2 Kgs 19:8-9)
\p
\v 8 Then the \add Assyrian\add* negotiator returned \add to find\add* the Assyrian king because he’d heard that he had left Lakish, and he found him fighting against Livnah.
\v 9 Then the king heard that Ethiopia’s King Tirhakah was marching out to attack him, so he sent messengers \add back\add* to Hezekiah with instructions,
\v 10 “Tell Judah’s King Hezekiah: don’t let your god that you trust in, deceive you by telling you that Jerusalem won’t be conquered by the Assyrian king.
\v 11 Listen, you’ve heard how that the Assyrian kings have destroyed all the \add other\add* regions. Don’t think \add that\add* you’ll be saved.
\v 12 Did the gods of those \add other\add* nations save them—\add places like\add* Gozan and Haran and Retsef, and the people of Eden who \add lived\add* in Telasar?
\v 13 What happened to the kings of Hamat, Arpad, La’ir, Sefarvayim, Hena, and Ivah?”
\s1 Hezekiah’s Prayer
\p
\v 14 Hezekiah accepted the letter brought by the messengers and read it. Then he went into Yahweh’s residence and spread it out in front of Yahweh
\v 15 and prayed to Yahweh, saying,
\v 16 “Commander-in-chief Yahweh, Israel’s god, who’s enthroned above the winged creatures. You alone are God over all of earth’s kingdoms—you made the heavens and the earth.
\v 17 Yahweh, please pay attention and listen. Open your eyes, Yahweh, and look, and listen to everything that Sennacherib has said to defy the living god.
\v 18 Yahweh, it’s true that the Assyrian kings have destroyed all those nations and their regions,
\v 19 and his men threw their gods into the fire, because they weren’t gods \add at all\add*—\add they’re just\add* crafted from wood and stone, so they destroyed them.
\v 20 But now, Yahweh our god, save us from his power so that all of the world’s kingdoms will know that you alone are Yahweh \add who is God\add*.
\s1 Isaiah’s message to Hezekiah
\r (2 Kgs 19:20-37)
\p
\v 21 Then Amots’ son Isaiah sent this message to \add King\add* Hezekiah: “Israel’s god Yahweh says that because you prayed to me about the Assyrian King Sennacherib,
\v 22 then this is what Yahweh pronounces about him:
\q1 Zion’s virgin daughter despises you—she scorns you.
\q1 Jerusalem’s daughter shakes her head at you.
\q1
\v 23 Who is it that you’ve defied and insulted?
\q1 Who did you raise your voice \add against\add*
\q1 and arrogantly challenged?
\q1 It was Israel’s holy one.
\q1
\v 24 You’ve defied my master by sending your servants,
\q1 and you’ve said that you’ve gone to the tops of the mountains with your chariots—
\q1 up to the remotest parts of Lebanon to cut down its tallest cedars and best pines.
\q1 You’ve said that you’ll enter the highest parts of its forest plantation.
\q1
\v 25 It’s me who has dug and drunk water,
\q1 and dried up all of Egypt’s canals with the soles of my feet.
\p
\v 26 “Haven’t you heard that I determined it from long ago.
\q1 I planned it since ancient times and now I’m making it happen,
\q1 →and you’re about to make fortified cities into desolate heaps of ruins.
\q1
\v 27 Their powerless inhabitants are dismayed and ashamed.
\q1 They \add come and go like\add* the vegetation in the countryside,
\q1 and \add like\add* how a grass roof doesn’t stay green for long
\q1 and \add like\add* a field as the grain crop grows.
\p
\v 28 “I know when you sit down and when you go in or out,
\q1 and I know your raging against me.
\q1
\v 29 Because of that raging against me,
\q1 and your arrogance that’s reached my ears,
\q1 I’ll put my hook in your nose
\q1 and my bit in your mouth,
\q1 and I’ll send you home on the same road that you came here on.”
\p
\v 30 So this will be the sign for you \add Hezekiah\add*:
\q1 This year you’ll eat wild crops,
\q1 and next year you’ll eat the self-seeded growth.
\q1 ^But in the third year you must sow and harvest,
\q1 and plant vineyards and eat their fruit.
\q1
\v 31 The people of Judah who’ve remained \add here\add*
\q1 will send their roots down and their fruit will grow up,
\q1
\v 32 because a remnant will come out of Jerusalem,
\q1 and survivors from Mt. Zion.
\q1 Commander-in-chief Yahweh will accomplish that through his zeal.
\p
\v 33 Therefore Yahweh says this about the Assyrian king:
\q1 He won’t come into this city and he won’t shoot an error here.
\q1 He won’t bring a shield near it, and he won’t build ramps up against it.
\q1
\v 34 He’ll return by the road that he came on, and he won’t enter this city.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration
\v 35 and I’ll protect this city \add and\add* rescue it for my own sake and for the sake of my servant David.
\s1 Sennacherib’s retreat and assassination
\p
\v 36 Then Yahweh’s messenger went out and killed 185,000 Assyrian soldiers—when the others got up early \add the next\add* morning, wow, there were corpses \add all around\add*!
\v 37 Then the Assyrian King Sennacherib left and headed back \add there\add*, and he settled down in Nineveh.
\v 38 \add One day\add* he was worshipping in the temple of his god Nisrok, and his sons Adramelek and Sharetser ran a sword through him before escaping to the Ararat region, and his son Esar-Hadon replaced him as king.
\c 38
\s1 Hezekiah’s sickness and recovery
\r (2 Kgs 20:1-11; 2 Chr. 32:24-26)
\p
\v 1 Then a time came when \add King\add* Hezekiah became critically ill, and the prophet Isaiah \add Isaiah\add*, Amots’ son) went to him and told him, “Yahweh says that you should put all your affairs into order, because you won’t recover—your time is up.”
\p
\v 2 Then Hezekiah rolled over to face the wall and prayed to Yahweh,
\v 3 “Oh Yahweh, please remember how I’ve served you faithfully and with integrity, and done what pleases you.” Then Hezekiah cried and cried.
\p
\v 4 Later, Yahweh gave Isaiah this message,
\v 5 “Go and tell the king that Yahweh, the god of his father David has taken notice of his prayer \add and\add* seen his tears. So he’ll give him another fifteen years
\v 6 and will rescue \add both\add* him and Jerusalem from the Assyrian king. Yahweh will defend this city
\v 7 and this will be the sign from him that he will do what he’s said,
\v 8 ‘Look, I will turn the shadow on the steps \add built by King\add* Ahaz, back ten steps.’ ” So the sun’s shadow went back \add up\add* ten steps that it had gone down.
\s2 Hezekiah’s poem of praise
\p
\v 9 Judah’s King Hezekiah wrote this \add poem\add* after he’d recovered from his sickness:
\q1
\v 10 I said, “I’ll go through the gates to the grave in the prime of my life,
\q1 I’ll miss out on the remainder of my years.”
\q1
\v 11 I said, “I won’t see Yah in the land of the living.
\q1 I’ll no longer look at humankind but rather those who’ve ceased living.”
\q1
\v 12 The body that I live in has been pulled up and removed from \add over\add* me like a shepherd’s tent.
\q1 I’ve rolled my life up like a weaver cutting me off from the loom.
\q1 From day until night, you make an end of me—
\q1
\v 13 I lie down\f + \fr 38:13 \ft The Hebrew could be read: \fqa I cried out \ft or \fq I lie down\ft .\f* until the morning.
\q1 He breaks all my bones like a lion \add would\add*.
\q1 From day until night, you make an end of me.
\q1
\v 14 I chirp like a swallow and moan like a dove.
\q1 My eyes feel weak from looking upwards.
\q1 I’m oppressed, my master, help me out.
\b
\q1
\v 15 What will I say? He told me and then he did it.
\q1 I’ll walk slowly my remaining years because of my soul’s bitterness.
\b
\q1
\v 16 My master, people live by such things,
\q1 and my spirit finds life in all of them.
\q1 Please restore my health and preserve my life.
\q1
\v 17 Look, \add this\add* severe bitterness was for my welfare,
\q1 and you held my life back from the pit of destruction
\q1 because you threw all my sins back over your shoulder.
\q1
\v 18 No, the grave doesn’t thank you.
\q1 Death doesn’t praise you.
\q1 Those who go down into the pit don’t expect you to be faithful.
\q1
\v 19 The living, \add yes only\add* the living, give thanks to you like I do today.
\q1 A father tells his children about your faithfulness.
\q1
\v 20 Yahweh will save me and we’ll play stringed instruments
\q1 at his residence for the rest of our lives.
\p
\v 21 Then Isaiah said, “Get them to apply a cake of figs to the boil so he’ll recover.”
\p
\v 22 Now Hezekiah asked, “What will be the sign that I should go up to Yahweh’s residence?”
\c 39
\s1 Hezekiah shows off his wealth
\r (2 Kgs 20:12-19)
\p
\v 1 At that time, Babylonian King Merodak-Bal’adan (son of Baladan) sent \add messengers with\add* letters and a gift to \add King\add* Hezekiah because he had heard that he’d been sick and then recovered.
\v 2 Hezekiah happily welcomed them, and showed them his treasure house—the gold and silver and spices and high-quality oil, as well as his armoury and other storehouses. Hezekiah showed them everything in his palace and all over his kingdom.
\p
\v 3 Afterwards, the prophet Isaiah went to King Hezekiah and asked him where those men had come from and what they had said.
\p “They came from the distant region of Babylon,” Hezekiah replied.
\p
\v 4 “What did they see in your palace?” Isaiah asked.
\p “They’ve seen everything that’s in my palace,” Hezekiah answered. “There’s none of my treasures that I didn’t show them.”
\p
\v 5 Then Isaiah told Hezekiah, “Listen to this message from Commander-in-chief Yahweh:
\v 6 The time is definitely coming when everything in your palace will be carried away to Babylon—everything that your ancestors collected \add in the past\add* until now. Yahweh says that nothing will be left.
\v 7 In addition, some of your descendants, your own flesh and blood, will be taken away and be castrated \add so as to become servants\add* in the Babylonian king’s palace.”
\p
\v 8 “Yahweh’s message that you’ve passed on is appropriate,” Hezekiah told Isaiah, “because \add at least\add* there’ll be peace and stability during my time.”
\c 40
\s1 Yahweh will comfort his people
\p
\v 1 “Encourage my people,” says your god. “Encourage \add them\add*.
\q1
\v 2 Speak to Jerusalem’s heart and tell her that her \add period of\add* service is ended,
\q1 because \add the punishment for\add* her disobedience has been accepted.
\q1 She’s received double \add punishment\add* from Yahweh for all her sins.
\b
\q1
\v 3 A voice calls out in the wilderness\f + \fr 40:3 \ft It’s unclear from the Hebrew if the \+bdit voice\+bdit* is in the wilderness, or if the \+bdit path\+bdit* is in the wilderness.\f* to clear the path for Yahweh.
\q1 Make a straight road in the Aravah \add plain\add* for our god.
\q1
\v 4 Every valley will be filled in and every mountain and hill will be flattened,
\q1 and \add so\add* the slopes will be levelled and the rough places smoothed out,
\q1
\v 5 then Yahweh’s splendour will be revealed and all people will see it simultaneously,
\q1 because that’s what Yahweh has declared.
\b
\q1
\v 6 A voice says, ‘Call out!’ and I ask, ‘What should I call out?’
\q1 \add Say that\add* all people are like grass—
\q1 all their ‘loyal commitment’ \add is as temporary\add* as a flower in the countryside.
\q1
\v 7 The grass dries up \add and\add* the flower wilts
\q1 when Yahweh’s breath blows over it.
\q1 Surely humankind is \add like\add* grass.
\q1
\v 8 The grass dries up, the flower wilts,
\q1 but the decree from our god will stand forever.
\b
\q1
\v 9 Go up onto a high hill you bearer of news for Zion.
\q1 Raise your voice you bringer of news to Jerusalem—shout loudly and don’t be afraid.
\q1 Tell Judah’s cities that their god is here.
\b
\q1
\v 10 Look, my master Yahweh will be coming with strength,
\q1 and he will be ruling with power.
\q1 Look, he’s bringing his reward
\q1 and his wages are \add there\add* in front of him.
\q1
\v 11 He will tend his flock like a shepherd \add does\add*.
\q1 He will hold the lambs to his chest.
\q1 He will lead along the ewes who’ve just had lambs.
\s1 Not/None like of Master of Israel
\q1
\v 12 Who has measured the oceans in their palm,
\q1 and has marked off the sky with their handspan?
\q1 Who can count the dust grains on the earth,
\q1 and has weighed the mountains with scales and the hills with balances?
\q1
\v 13 Who has told Yahweh’s spirit what to do,
\q1 and has any person given him good advice?
\q1
\v 14 Who did he ask for advice?
\q1 Who informed him about something?
\q1 Who taught him how to judge fairly,
\q1 and taught him knowledge and helped him to understand something?
\b
\q1
\v 15 Listen, the nations are like a drop in a bucket,
\q1 and are as insignificant as dust on the scales.
\q1 He lifts the islands as if they just specks.
\q1
\v 16 It’s not enough to set fire to Lebanon,
\q1 and it doesn’t have enough animals to be an adequate burnt offering.
\q1
\v 17 All the nations \add together\add* are \add still\add* nothing to him.
\q1 He \add considers their combined power\add* as useless and worthless.
\b
\q1
\v 18 Who could you compare God with?
\q1 What is there that you all could say is anything like him?
\q1
\v 19 An idol that was cast by a craftsman,
\q1 overlaid with gold by a goldsmith,
\q1 and then decorated with silver chains?
\q1
\v 20 The poorer person chooses a contribution of wood that won’t rot.
\q1 They choose a skillful craftsman to make an idol that won’t topple over.
\b
\q1
\v 21 Don’t you all know? Haven’t you heard?
\q1 Didn’t anyone tell you all from the beginning?
\q1 Don’t you all understand the earth’s foundations?
\q1
\v 22 \add He’s\add* the one who sits above the circle of the earth,
\q1 and its inhabitants are like grasshoppers.
\q1 \add He’s\add* the one who stretches the skies out like a curtain
\q1 and spreads them out like a tent to live in.
\b
\q1
\v 23 He decides which officials will come to nothing.
\q1 He makes the world’s judges irrelevant.
\q1
\v 24 Indeed, they’re hardly planted, yes, hardly sown.
\q1 Indeed, their stem has barely taken root in the earth,
\q1 ^but he also blows on them and they wither,
\q1 and the wind carries them away like straw.
\b
\q1
\v 25 “Who \add else\add* is like me?” asks the holy one.
\q1 “Who \add else\add* could be equal to me?”
\b
\q1
\v 26 Look up and see \add the stars\add*.
\q1 Who created them?
\q1 Yahweh knows their number as he brings them out.
\q1 He calls them all by name.
\q1 Because of his great strength and mighty power
\q2 not a single one is missing.
\b
\q1
\v 27 Why do you talk, Jacob?
\q1 And why do you speak, Israel?
\q1 \add Saying,\add* ‘My path is hidden from Yahweh,
\q2 and my rights disappear.’
\q1
\v 28 Don’t you know?
\q1 Haven’t you heard?
\q1 The eternal God Yahweh is the creator of one end of the earth to the other.
\q1 He doesn’t get tired
\q1 and he won’t become weary.
\q1 You won’t find a limit to his understanding.
\q1
\v 29 He gives energy to those who are weary,
\q1 and increases the strength of those who are weak.
\q1
\v 30 \add Even\add* young people get tired and weary,
\q1 and young men can stumble clumsily,
\q1
\v 31 but those who trust Yahweh will renew their strength.
\q1 They will rise up with wings like eagles.
\q1 They will run and not get tired.
\q1 They will walk and not become weary.
\c 41
\s1 Yahweh will help Israel
\p
\v 1 Stay silent in front of me, you islands,
\q1 and let the peoples renew their strength.
\q1 Let them approach then let them speak.
\q1 Let’s gather together for judgement.
\q1
\v 2 Who has stirred up punishment from the east?
\q1 It meets with his feet.
\q1 He destroys nations in front of him.
\q1 He causes kings to rule.
\q1 He makes them like dust with his sword—
\q1 like chaff driven by his bow.
\q1
\v 3 He chases after them—going forward in peace.
\q1 He comes on a path without \add using\add* his feet.
\q1
\v 4 Who has done it and has accomplished it—
\q1 calling the generations out from the beginning?
\q1 I Yahweh, am the first and the last. I am he.
\b
\q1
\v 5 The islands have seen and are afraid.
\q1 The whole world trembles.
\q1 They approach and come.
\q1
\v 6 Everyone helps out their neighbours,
\q1 and tells each other to be strong.
\q1
\v 7 The craftsman encourages the goldsmith who smooths with a hammer.
\q1 The one who strikes the anvil says that the welding is good
\q1 and the strengthen it with nails so it won’t topple over.
\b
\q1
\v 8 But you, my servant Israel,
\q1 Jacob who I’ve chosen,
\q1 the descendants of my friend Abraham,
\q1
\v 9 I pulled you back from the ends of the earth,
\q1 calling you from its most distant corners,
\q1 and I told you, ‘You’re my servant.’
\q1 I’ve chosen you and won’t reject you.
\q1
\v 10 Don’t be afraid, because I’m with you.
\q1 Don’t be anxious, because I’m your God.
\q1 I’ll strengthen you, indeed, I’ll help you.
\q1 Yes, I’ll support you with my strength—always doing what’s right.
\b
\q1
\v 11 Listen, all those who are angry with you will be ashamed and disgraced.
\q1 Those who contend with you will be reduced to nothing and will perish.
\q1
\v 12 You’ll search for those who contended against you, but you won’t be able to find them.
\q1 Those who battled against you will be useless and irrelevant,
\q1
\v 13 because I, your god Yahweh, will hold \add up\add* your right hand—
\q1 the one who tells you not to be afraid \add because\add* I’ll help you.
\p
\v 14 Don’t be afraid, Jacob, you worm—you Israelite people.
\q1 I will help you declares Yahweh—the one who rescues you—
\q1 Israel’s holy one.
\q1
\v 15 Listen, I’m making you into a threshing sledge—sharp and double-edged.
\q1 You’ll thresh mountains and crush them,
\q1 and make the hills like chaff.
\q1
\v 16 You’ll toss them into the air and the wind will carry them away,
\q1 and a whirlwind will scatter them.
\q1 →Then you’ll be happy about Yahweh—
\q1 you’ll boast about Israel’s holy one.
\b
\q1
\v 17 The poor and needy people look for water, but there’s none.
\q1 Their tongues are dry from thirst.
\q1 I, Yahweh, will respond to them.
\q1 I, Israel’s god won’t abandon them.
\q1
\v 18 I’ll make streams flow down from barren hills,
\q1 and will cause springs to appear in the middle of the valleys.
\q1 I’ll make the wilderness into a pool of water,
\q1 and \add fill\add* the dry land with springs.
\q1
\v 19 I’ll grow cedar, acacia, myrtle, and olive trees in the wilderness.
\q1 I’ll plant cypress, elm, and boxwood trees on the desert plain,
\q1
\v 20 so that \add people\add* will be able to see, to realise, and to have determination and insight together
\q1 →because Yahweh has personally done that—Israel’s holy one has created it.
\s1 Yahweh’s challenge to idols
\p
\v 21 “Bring your case to me,” says Yahweh.
\q1 “Present your arguments,” says Jacob’s king.
\q1
\v 22 Let them bring them out and tell us what will happen.
\q1 What happened before that?
\q1 Tell us so we can ponder them and so we can know what’s been done
\q1 and then tell us what’s still coming.
\q1
\v 23 Tell the signs of the future so that we can know if you’re all gods.
\q1 Yes, do something good or bad so that we can be amazed or afraid together.
\q1
\v 24 Look, you’re all nothing and your actions are worthless.
\q1 Anyone who chooses you all to worship is disgusting.
\b
\q1
\v 25 I have stirred up \add a ruler\add* from the north,
\q1 and he’s coming from the east.
\q1 He’ll put his trust in me
\q1 and he’ll step on officials like mud
\q1 and like a potter who works the clay with his feet.
\q1
\v 26 Who told us \add that\add* from the beginning so that we’d know?
\q1 Who predicted it so that we can \add now\add* say that they were correct?
\q1 Actually, no one said or announced it—
\q1 no, no one heard your messages.
\q1
\v 27 Firstly to Zion, look they’re here!
\q1 Then I sent a runner to Jerusalem to give them the news.
\q1
\v 28 Then I looked \add at those idols\add* but there was no life \add there\add*.
\q1 There wasn’t any advisor among them.
\q1
\v 29 No, all of them are a delusion—they accomplish nothing.
\q1 Their cast metal images are as empty as the wind.
\c 42
\s1 Yahweh’s chosen servant
\s2 The first servant song
\q1
\v 1 Look, here’s my servant who I support—my chosen one who I’m pleased with.
\q1 I’ve put my spirit onto him. He’ll bring justice to the nations.
\q1
\v 2 He won’t cry out or lift up \add his head\add*.
\q1 and he won’t make his voice heard in the streets.
\q1
\v 3 He won’t break a crushed reed
\q1 and nor will he extinguish a dim oil-lamp.
\q1 He will faithfully bring justice to the people.
\q1
\v 4 He won’t grow dim or be crushed
\q1 before he’s established justice in the world,
\q1 and while the islands wait for his instructions.
\b
\q1
\v 5 The god Yahweh who created the skies and stretched them out,
\q1 who formed the earth and all its produce,
\q1 who gives breath to the people on it
\q1 and a spirit to those who walk on it, says this:
\q1
\v 6 I, Yahweh, have called you \add my servant\add* for a godly purpose,
\q1 and I’ll take hold of your hand and watch over you.
\q1 I will make you into a promise for the people—a light for the nations:
\q1
\v 7 to open the eyes of the blind—
\q1 to bring prisoners out of the dungeon—
\q1 to release those who live in dark prisons.
\b
\q1
\v 8 I am Yahweh. That’s my name
\q1 and I won’t allow anyone else to receive the honour that only I deserve,
\q1 and nor will I allow images to receive the praise that should come to me.
\q1
\v 9 Listen, things \add said\add* before have come \add to pass\add*
\q1 and I’m about to reveal new things—
\q1 I’ll let you all know about them before they come to be.
\s1 A song to praise Yahweh
\q1
\v 10 Sing a new song for Yahweh—praising him from the end of the earth.
\q1 Those who go down to the sea and everything that fills it.
\q1 You islands and their inhabitants.
\q1
\v 11 Let the wilderness and its cities lift up \add their voice\add*—
\q1 the villages where the Bedouins live.
\q1 Let the inhabitants of \add Edom’s capital\add* Sela sing.
\q1 Let them shout from the mountaintops.
\q1
\v 12 Let them honour Yahweh
\q1 and praise him from the islands.
\q1
\v 13 Yahweh will go ahead like a warrior—
\q1 he’ll stir up zeal like a man of war.
\q1 He’ll raise a war cry—shouting out loudly,
\q1 →\add and then\add* he’ll defeat his enemies.
\s1 Yahweh promises help
\p
\v 14 I’ve been silent for a long time now.
\q1 I’ve been still—I’ve restrained myself.
\q1 \add Now\add* I’ll cry out like a woman giving birth.
\q1 I’ll puff and pant at the same time.
\q1
\v 15 I’ll make the mountains and hills dry up
\q1 and all their vegetation will wither.
\q1 plus I’ll turn the rivers into islands
\q1 and dry up the pools.
\b
\q1
\v 16 I’ll lead the blind down a path that they don’t know.
\q1 I’ll guide them on roads that they’re not familiar with.
\q1 I’ll make the dark places in front of them, light,
\q1 and the rough places into level ground.
\q1 I’ll do those things for them, and I won’t abandon them.
\q1
\v 17 Those who trust in idols will be turned back and totally humiliated—
\q1 those who tell a cast metal figure, ‘You’re our god.’
\s1 Israel’s stubborn deafness
\p
\v 18 Listen you deaf people,
\q1 and look you blind people and see.
\q1
\v 19 Who is blind other than my servant,
\q1 or deaf like my messenger who I’ll send?
\q1 Who’s blind like \add my partner\add* in our agreement,
\q1 or blind like Yahweh’s servant.
\q1
\v 20 You see many things but don’t take any notice.
\q1 Your ears are open but you don’t listen.
\p
\v 21 Yahweh was pleased to do what’s right—
\q1 he made his instructions honourable and splendid.
\q1
\v 22 But this is a people who’ve been robbed and plundered.
\q1 They’re all trapped in holes and hidden away in prisons.
\q1 They’ve become the spoil, and there’s no one to rescue them.
\q1 They’ve become plunder and no one speaks up to help them back.
\b
\q1
\v 23 Will any of you take any notice of that?
\q1 Will anyone listen or pay attention afterwards?
\q1
\v 24 Who gave Jacob to the robbers,
\q1 and Israel to the looters?
\q1 →Wasn’t it Yahweh, the one we disobeyed?
\q1 They weren’t willing to do what he wanted,
\q1 and they didn’t listen to his instructions.
\q1
\v 25 Then he poured out his rage and the violence of war.
\q1 The flames burnt all around, yet Israel didn’t realise.
\q1 They burnt it up, but it didn’t take it to heart.
\c 43
\s1 Yahweh promises rescue and return
\p
\v 1 But now Yahweh, the one who created you, Jacob,
\q1 and who formed you, Israel, says this:
\b
\q1 Don’t be afraid, because I’ve paid the price to free you.
\q1 I’ve called you by name—you belong to me.
\q1
\v 2 When you pass through the waters, I’m with you,
\q1 and through the rivers, they won’t carry you away.
\q1 When you walk through the fire, you won’t get scorched
\q1 and the flames won’t burn you \add up\add*
\q1
\v 3 because I’m your god, Yahweh—
\q1 your rescuer, Israel’s holy one.
\q1 I’ve given Egypt as your ransom—
\q1 Ethiopia and Seva in your place
\q1
\v 4 since you are precious in my eyes.
\q1 You are honoured and I love you,
\q1 and I’ll give humankind in your place,
\q1 and nations in exchange for your life.
\q1
\v 5 Don’t be afraid, because I’m with you.
\q1 I’ll bring your descendants from the east,
\q1 and I’ll gather you in from the west.
\q1
\v 6 I’ll tell the north to hand you over,
\q1 and tell the south not to hold you back.
\q1 Bring my sons \add back\add* from distant lands,
\q1 and bring my daughters from the ends of the earth—
\q1
\v 7 \add yes,\add* all those who belong to me
\q1 and who I’ve created as my showpiece—
\q1 those who I formed \add when\add* I made them.
\s1 The calling of God of Israel as witness
\p
\v 8 Bring out the people with eyes who \add still seem\add* blind,
\q1 and those who have ears but \add still seem\add* deaf.
\q1
\v 9 All the nations have gathered together,
\q1 and the peoples assemble.
\q1 Which of them could have predicted this?
\q1 They announce \add things that have happened in the past\add*!
\q1 Let them appoint their \add own\add* witnesses so they can be justified.
\q1 Let them hear and then let them say that it’s true.
\b
\q1
\v 10 \add But\add* Yahweh declares that you all are \em my\em* witnesses
\q1 and my servant who I’ve chosen
\q1 →so that you all can know and believe me
\q2 and understand that I am God.
\q1 There’s no other god that was formed before me,
\q1 ^and none will be format after me.
\b
\q1
\v 11 I (and no one else) am Yahweh.
\q1 and other than me, there’s no one else who can save you.
\q1
\v 12 I have declared that I would save and I have saved,
\q1 and I have declared that I have done it,
\q1 and you all are my witnesses that I am God.
\q1 That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1
\v 13 From \add the first\add* day, I am he,
\q1 and no one can oppose my power.
\q1 I take action and who could reverse it?
\s1 The punishment of Babylon
\p
\v 14 Yahweh, the one who paid to set you all free, Israel’s holy one, says this:
\q1 For your sake, I’ve sent \add an army\add* to Babylon,
\q1 and I’ll bring all of them down \add as fugitives\add* in boats,
\q1 and all those Chaldeans in the ships that they usually cheer about.
\q1
\v 15 I am Yahweh, your holy one
\q1 creator of Israel, your king.
\b
\p
\v 16 \add I am\add* Yahweh,
\q1 the one who made a path through the sea
\q1 and a road through the surging waters.
\q1
\v 17 I enticed the chariots and horses out,
\q1 together with a strong army,
\q1 They fell down together and didn’t rise \add again\add*.
\q1 They were extinguished like \add how\add* a wick is quenched.
\p
\v 18 \add Yahweh says\add*,
\q1 Don’t meditate on what happened previously,
\q1 and don’t keep thinking about past history.
\q1
\v 19 Listen, I’m going to do \add something\add* new.
\q1 It’s springing up—haven’t you noticed it?
\q1 Yes, I’ll make a road in the wilderness—
\q1 streams in that desolate place.
\q1
\v 20 The animals in the countryside will honour me—
\q1 the jackals and the ostriches
\q1 because I’ve put water in the wilderness,
\q1 streams in the desolate place
\q1 to \add provide\add* water for my chosen people.
\q1
\v 21 I formed that nation for myself.
\q1 They will discuss \add their\add* praise for me.
\s1 Israel’s sin
\p
\v 22 But you didn’t call to me \add for help\add*, Jacob,
\q1 because you’ve grown tired of me, Israel.
\q1
\v 23 You haven’t brought sheep to me as your burnt offerings,
\q1 and you haven’t honoured me with your sacrifices.
\q1 I haven’t burdened you with \add grain\add* offerings,
\q1 and I haven’t made you weary by demanding incense.
\q1
\v 24 You didn’t spend any money to buy fragrant reeds for me,
\q1 and you haven’t satisfied me with the fat from your sacrifices.
\q1 ^Nevertheless you’ve burdened me with your disobedience—
\q1 you’ve tired me out with your wickedness.
\q1
\v 25 I’m the only one who wipes your offenses out for my own sake,
\q1 and then I won’t remember your sins any more.
\b
\q1
\v 26 Remind me, let’s plead the case together.
\q1 You retell it \add all\add* so that you can be declared innocent.
\q1
\v 27 Your founding father sinned,
\q1 and your spokesmen have disobeyed me,
\q1
\v 28 so I’ll defile your sacred officials,
\q1 and send destruction to Jacob,
\q1 and insults to Israel.
\c 44
\s1 Yahweh’s chosen people
\q1
\v 1 Now listen Jacob my servant,
\q1 and Israel who I’ve chosen.
\q1
\v 2 The one who made you and formed you in the womb,
\q1 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Don’t be afraid, Jacob my servant,
\q1 and Israel who I’ve chosen,
\p
\v 3 because I’ll pour water out onto the thirsty ground
\q1 and streams on the dry ground.
\q1 I’ll pour my spirit out onto your offspring
\q1 and my blessing onto your descendants.
\q1
\v 4 They’ll spring up among the grasses
\q1 like poplars beside flowing streams.
\b
\q1
\v 5 This one will say that they’re in favour of Yahweh,
\q1 and that one will out Jacob’s name out.
\q1 Another one will write ‘Belonging to Yahweh’ on their hand,
\q1 and \add others\add* will use the title ‘From Israel’.
\p
\v 6 Israel’s king and the one who bought their freedom,
\q1 Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this:
\q1 I’m the first and the last,
\q1 and there’s no other god—just me.
\q1
\v 7 Yes, who’s like me? Let him declare it,
\q1 and let him declare it.
\q1 Let him recount to me the historical sequence of how this nation was established in the past,
\q1 and let them declare what’s about to happen.
\q1
\v 8 Don’t dread the future
\q1 and don’t be afraid.
\q1 Didn’t I already declare it to you
\q1 and announce \add what would happen\add*, and you all are my witnesses?
\q1 Is there some other god\f + \fr 44:8 \ft This is the only place where Isaiah uses Eloah (singular) for God instead of Elohim (plural).\f* than me?
\q1 There’s no other ‘rock’ that I know of.
\s1 The absurdity of crafting an idol
\p
\v 9 All those who form idols are losers
\q1 and their expectations won’t be realised.
\q1 Their witnesses can’t see and they don’t know anything,
\q1 →so they’ll be ashamed.
\q1
\v 10 Who would form a god
\q1 or an idol that never helps them?
\q1
\v 11 All their associates will be put to shame—
\q1 craftsmen are \add just\add* human beings.
\q1 Let all of them gather together \add and\add* take their stand.
\q1 Let them dread the consequences and be ashamed together.
\p
\v 12 The blacksmith takes a tool and works on the iron over the coals.
\q1 He hammers it with his strong arm to form it \add into shape\add*.
\q1 But then he gets hungry and feels weaker.
\q1 \add If\add* he doesn’t drink water, he’ll feel exhausted.
\p
\v 13 The carpenter measures a piece of wood.
\q1 He outlines it with a marker and works it with his chisels.
\q1 He outlines it with a compass and makes it like the figure of a man—
\q1 like a good-looking man \add who might\add* live in a house.
\q1
\v 14 He cuts down cedar trees
\q1 or selects a cypress or an oak.
\q1 He lets it grow strong among the trees in the forest.
\q1 He plants a fir tree and the rain makes it grow.
\q1
\v 15 Then \add the wood\add* is used by humankind to burn.
\q1 The carpenter uses some of the wood to warm himself,
\q1 Yes, he kindles a fire and bakes bread \add on it\add*.
\q1 He also makes a god out of it and bows down to it.
\q1 He makes the wood into an idol and kneels in front of it.
\q1
\v 16 He burns half of the wood for the fire,
\q1 and roasts meat on that half—he eats the meat and is satisfied.
\q1 He also warms himself and says ‘Aha, I’m warm,’ as he looks at the fire.
\q1
\v 17 He uses the rest of the wood to make a god.
\q1 He makes an idol and kneels in front of it.
\q1 He bows down and prays to it
\q1 →and says, ‘Rescue me, because you’re my god.’
\p
\v 18 Those people don’t know and don’t understand
\q1 because their eyes are plastered over so they can’t see
\q1 \add and\add* their minds are prevented from having insight.
\q1
\v 19 No one gives much thought to their actions.
\q1 \add They don’t apply\add* knowledge and understanding, to say,
\q1 ‘I burnt half of the wood in the fire and baked bread on the coals.
\q1 I roasted meat on it and ate it.
\q1 Then I’ll take the remainder to make a detestable idol.
\q1 I’ll bow down to a piece of wood from a tree.’
\p
\v 20 He eats thanks to those ashes.
\q1 He’s deceived and been led astray.
\q1 He doesn’t escape from his own situation and say,
\q1 ‘I’m \add holding\add* a lie in my right hand.’
\p
\v 21 Remember that, Jacob,
\q1 and Israel because you’re my servant.
\q1 I formed you—you’re my servant Israel—I won’t forget about you.
\q1
\v 22 I’ve blotted out your disobedience like a cloud.
\q1 and your sins like a fog.
\q1 Return to me because I paid the price for your freedom.
\b
\q1
\v 23 Shout happily you skies because Yahweh has taken action.
\q1 Shout triumphantly you depths of the earth.
\q1 Sing loudly you mountains and forests and all you trees
\q1 because Yahweh has paid the price for Jacob’s freedom
\q1 and will be honoured by Israel.
\p
\v 24 Yahweh, the one who paid for your freedom
\q1 and the one who formed you in the womb, says this:
\q1 I am Yahweh who made everything,
\q1 who alone stretched out the skies \add and\add* formed the earth.
\q1 Who was it who helped me?
\q1
\v 25 Who else frustrates the sign of empty-talkers,
\q1 and makes diviners look foolish?
\q1 Who else turns wise people back
\q1 and makes their knowledge \add seem\add* foolish?
\q1
\v 26 Who else confirms the words of his prophet,
\q1 and fulfills the advice of his messengers
\q1 who say that Jerusalem will be inhabited,
\q1 and that Judah’s towns will be rebuilt,
\q1 and the country’s ruins will be rebuilt?
\q1
\v 27 Who else tells the deep sea to dry out,
\q1 and makes your rivers dry up?
\q1
\v 28 Who else calls \add King\add* Cyrus ‘my shepherd’,
\q1 and says that he’ll do everything I want?
\q1 Who else says that Jerusalem will be rebuilt,
\q1 and that the temple foundations will be restored.
\c 45
\s1 Yahweh send Cyrus
\p
\v 1 Yahweh has appointed Cyrus \add and\add* given him power
\q1 to subdue \add other\add* nations and defeat kings—
\q1 to open doors ahead of him,
\q1 and no gates will shut \add him out\add*.
\q1 \add Now\add* Yahweh says this:
\q1
\v 2 I’ll go ahead of you \add Cyrus\add* and level the mountains.
\q1 I’ll break down bronze doors and cut through iron bars,
\q1
\v 3 and I’ll give you the treasures that are hidden away
\q1 and the riches \add stored\add* in secret places
\q1 →so that you’ll know that it’s me, Yahweh, Israel’s god, who calls you by name.
\q1
\v 4 For the sake of my servant Jacob
\q1 and Israel my chosen one,
\q1 I’ve summoned you by name.
\q1 Although you don’t know me, I’ll give you a title \add of honour\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 5 I am Yahweh and there’s no one else.
\q1 Apart from me, there’s no \add other\add* God.
\q1 Even though you don’t know me,
\q2 I will strengthen you
\q1
\v 6 so that from the east to the west they’ll know
\q2 that there’s nothing apart from me.
\q1 I’m Yahweh, and there’s no other \add God\add*.
\q1
\v 7 I formed the light and created the darkness.
\q1 I bring peace and prosperity, and I cause disasters.
\q1 It’s me, Yahweh, who does all those things.
\q1
\v 8 Rain down from above you skies,
\q1 and let the clouds pour down godliness.
\q1 Let the earth open up and let salvation grow \add on the trees\add*
\q1 and at the same time, let righteousness sprout up.
\q1 I, Yahweh, have created it.
\s1 Don’t argue with Yahweh
\q1
\v 9 The person who argues against their creator won’t end well.
\q1 \add They’re like\add* a piece of broken pottery lying among all the other pieces on the ground.
\q1 Does the clay ask the potter what he’s making,
\q1 or ask why he didn’t do a very good job making what he did?
\q1
\v 10 Anyone who asks a father what he’s fathering won’t end well,
\q1 and nor will anyone who asks a woman what she’s in labour for.
\q1
\v 11 Yahweh, Israel’s holy one and the one who formed it, asks this:
\q1 ‘\add Concerning\add* the things to come, do you question me about my children?
\q1 And will you all tell me what actions that I should take?’
\q1
\v 12 I made the earth and created humankind on it.
\q1 My own hands stretched the skies out,
\q1 and I ordered all the heavenly bodies \add into place\add*.
\q1
\v 13 I stirred Cyrus up to do what’s right,
\q1 and I’ll smooth out his paths \add ahead of him\add*.
\q1 He’ll be the one to build my city, and he’ll release my exiles—
\q1 not for a ransom or for a bribe.
\m Commander-in-chief Yahweh says that.
\p
\v 14 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Egypt’s produce and Ethiopia’s profit and those tall people from Seva
\q1 will \add all\add* be turned over to you and they’ll belong to you.
\q1 They’ll walk behind you and will be handed over in chains.
\q1 They’ll bow down to you and state,
\q1 ‘Surely God is with you—he’s the only god—there’s no other.’
\q1
\v 15 You are certainly a god who hides himself,
\q1 you god who rescues Israel.
\q1
\v 16 Those who craft idols will be humiliated—
\q1 all of them will be put to shame together.
\q1 Those who craft idols will be publicly disgraced.
\q1
\v 17 Israel has been rescued by Yahweh—freed forever.
\q1 You all won’t be humiliated or put to shame ever again.
\b
\q1
\v 18 Yahweh who created the skies, is God.
\q1 He formed the earth and established it.
\q1 He didn’t create it as a wasteland,
\q1 ^but rather, he formed it to be lived in.
\q1 Yahweh says this:
\q1
\v 19 I didn’t speak in secrecy in a place where it’s all dark.
\q1 It wasn’t in vain that I told Jacob’s descendants to search for me.
\q1 I am Yahweh who says what is right and declares what is proper.
\s1 Yahweh and false gods
\p
\v 20 Gather together and come.
\q1 Assemble together you fugitives from the nations.
\q1 Those who carry their wooden idols don’t know \add what’s going on\add*—
\q1 those who pray to a god that can’t help them.
\q1
\v 21 Declare and approach. Let them also get advice together.
\q1 Who predicted that in the past and then declared it?
\q1 Wasn’t it me, Yahweh?
\q1 There’s no god other than me—
\q1 a god who does what is right and who rescues his people.
\q1 There’s no other rescuer except me.
\b
\q1
\v 22 Turn to me and be saved, all \add you\add* at the ends of the earth,
\q1 because I’m God and there isn’t any other.
\q1
\v 23 I’ve solemnly promised that—
\q1 I only say what’s right and I don’t change my mind,
\q1 because every knee will bow to me.
\q1 Every tongue will promise allegiance to me.
\b
\p
\v 24 They’ll say that righteousness and strength are only in Yahweh.
\q1 All those who were angry at him will come to him in their shame.
\q1
\v 25 Israel’s descendants will be declared innocent by Yahweh,
\q1 and they’ll boast \add about what he’s done for them\add*.
\c 46
\s1 The false gods there in Babylon
\p
\v 1 \add The Babylonian gods\add* Bel bows down and Nevo stoops low.
\q1 Their idols need to be carried by animals and are burdens for tired cattle.
\q2
\v 2 Together those gods bend low, kneeling down.
\q1 They’re not able to rescue the load
\q1 and they themselves have been taken into captivity.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Listen to me \add you\add* descendants of Jacob,
\q1 and all the remnant of Israel’s families—
\q1 carried since birth,
\q1 supported since the womb.
\q1
\v 4 I’ll be there even when you age,
\q1 and I’ll carry you when your hair goes grey.
\q1 I made you and I’ll support you,
\q1 and I’ll carry you and rescue you.
\p
\v 5 Who can you liken me to or say that I’m the same as,
\q1 or compare me to and \add say that\add* we’re alike?
\q1
\v 6 Those who pour gold out from a bag
\q1 and weigh their silver on a scale,
\q1 hire a metalsmith to make it into a god.
\q1 They kneel in front of it and worship it.
\q1
\v 7 They lift it up and carry it on their shoulders,
\q1 and then they put it down in its place.
\q1 It stands there and never leaves.
\q1 They call out to it \add for help\add* but it doesn’t answer—
\q1 it won’t rescue them from their troubles.
\b
\q1
\v 8 Remember that and pluck up courage.
\q1 \add You\add* rebels, take it to heart.
\q1
\v 9 Think about what happened long ago
\q1 because I’m God and there’s no other,
\q1 I’m God and there’s no one like me.
\q1
\v 10 From the beginning, I declare the end.
\q1 From ancient times, I announce what’s still to come.
\q1 I declare that my plan will come to be,
\q2 and all my intentions will be accomplished.
\q1
\v 11 Summoning a bird of prey from the east,
\q1 from a distant country—the man to carry out my plan.
\q1 Yes, I’ve spoken and I’ll make it happen.
\q1 I’ve planned, and I’ll also accomplish it.
\b
\q1
\v 12 Listen to me you stubborn people—
\q1 you who are so far from doing what’s right.
\q1
\v 13 I’ve brought my righteousness near—it’s not far away,
\q1 and my salvation won’t delay.
\q1 I’ll rescue Zion
\q1 \add and display\add* my splendour to Israel.
\c 47
\s1 Babylon’s coming judgement
\p
\v 1 Come down and sit in the dust \add you\add* virgin daughter of Babylon.
\q1 Sit on the ground without a throne \add you\add* daughter of the Chaldeans,
\q1 because people won’t call you tender and delicate any more.
\q1
\v 2 Turn those millstones and grind flour.
\q1 Remove your veil and strip off your skirt.
\q1 Uncover your legs to wade through rivers.
\q1
\v 3 Your nakedness will be exposed.
\q1 Yes, your shame will be visible.
\q1 I’ll take vengeance and won’t spare anyone.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Commander-in-chief Yahweh is the name of the one who bought our freedom—
\q1 Israel’s holy one.
\p
\v 5 Sit in silence and go in darkness \add you\add* daughter of the Chaldeans,
\q1 because you’ll no longer be called ‘the queen of kingdoms’.
\q1
\v 6 I was angry with my people—
\q1 I defiled my inheritance and handed them over to you.
\q1 You showed them no compassion—
\q1 \add even\add* the old had a heavy burden placed on them.
\q1
\v 7 You said that you’d be queen forever,
\q1 ^but you didn’t give it enough thought—
\q1 you didn’t consider its outcome.
\q1
\v 8 Now listen to this \add you\add* over-indulged woman who lives in security,
\q1 saying in her heart, ‘No one else is important except me.
\q1 I’ll never be a widow or lose my children.’
\q1
\v 9 But both of these things will suddenly happen to you:
\q1 Bereavement and childless \add will hit\add* in one day.
\q1 They’ll come on you with completeness \add despite\add* your plentiful sorceries
\q1 \add and\add* the power of your multiple spells.
\b
\q1
\v 10 You trusted in your wickedness and thought no one would notice you.
\q1 Your wisdom and knowledge has led you astray
\q1 and you told yourself that no one else was important except you.
\q1
\v 11 Calamity will hit you and you won’t be able to charm it away.
\q1 Disaster will fall on you and you won’t be able to avoid it—
\q1 disaster like you’ve never known before will come on you suddenly.
\q1
\v 12 Keep trusting your spells and your plentiful sorceries
\q1 that you’ve worked \add hard\add* at since your youth.
\q1 Perhaps you’ll be successful—maybe scaring \add disaster away\add*.
\q1
\v 13 You’ve become tired of listening to so much advice.
\q1 Please let them stand up and save you—
\q1 those ones who chart the skies and divine from moons
\q1 to \add discover\add* what’s going to happen to you.
\b
\q1
\v 14 Look, they’ve become like straw that get burnt in a fire.
\q1 They can’t rescue themselves from the flames.
\q1 There’ll be no coals to warm them—no fire to sit by.
\q1
\v 15 That’s what those you worked with have become—those traders from your youth.
\q1 Everyone has wandered in their own direction—there’s no rescuer.
\c 48
\s1 Yahweh knows and controls the future
\q1
\v 1 Listen to this \add you\add* descendants of Jacob,
\q1 who are called by the name Israel and descended from Judah.
\q1 You make promises using Yahweh’s name,
\q1 and mention Israel’s god,
\q1 but it’s not \add done\add* honestly or sincerely
\q1
\v 2 even though they state that they’re from the sacred city,
\q1 and that they’re supported by Israel’s god, named commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\p
\v 3 Long ago I predicted and explained what would happen,
\q1 →then suddenly, I took action and they happened
\q1
\v 4 because I knew that you were stubborn.
\q1 Your neck muscles are \add like\add* iron
\q1 and your forehead is \add like\add* bronze,
\q1
\v 5 so I announced those things in advance.
\q1 Before they happened, I informed you
\q1 so that you couldn’t say that your idol did them,
\q1 or say that your carved idols and cast figures ordered them \add to happen\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 6 You’ve heard—\add now\add* investigate all of it.
\q1 Won’t you admit that what I tell you \add is true\add*?
\q1 Now I’ll tell you new things,
\q1 and secret things that you didn’t \add previously\add* know.
\q1
\v 7 They’re activated now—not in the past,
\q1 and you hadn’t heard of them \add happening\add* before today
\q1 otherwise you might say that you expected them.
\b
\q1
\v 8 \add No,\add* you hadn’t heard and you didn’t know
\q1 because from way back, you never wanted to hear.
\q1 Yes, I knew that you act very treacherously
\q1 and \add even\add* from the womb you’ve been called a rebel.
\b
\q1
\v 9 For the sake of my reputation, I’ll hold back my anger,
\q1 and so that I’m praised, I’ll restrain myself from destroying you.
\q1
\v 10 Look, I’ve refined you, but not like silver—
\q1 I’ve tested you in the furnace of affliction.
\q1
\v 11 \add But\add* for my own sake I’ll take action,
\q1 because how could I allow my reputation to be damaged?
\q1 I won’t allow others to take the honour that’s due to me.
\s1 The chosen by God destroy of Babylon
\q1
\v 12 Listen to me Jacob,
\q1 and Israel who I called.
\q1 I am he, the first, \add and\add* also the last.
\q1
\v 13 Yes, my hand founded the earth,
\q1 and my right hand stretched out the skies.
\q1 I call to them, \add then\add* they stand together.
\b
\q1
\v 14 Gather together all of you and listen.
\q1 Which of those idols has announced these things?
\q1 Yahweh’s dearest ally will implement Yahweh’s desire on Babylon
\q1 and apply his power to those Chaldeans.
\q1
\v 15 I’ve spoken. I’ve summoned him.
\q1 I’ve brought him, and he will succeed in his mission.
\b
\q1
\v 16 Come close to me and listen to this:
\q1 From the beginning, I haven’t spoken in secrecy.
\q1 I was there when those things happened.
\q1 My master Yahweh has sent me and his spirit.
\p
\v 17 Yahweh, Israel’s holy one \add and\add* the one who paid the price for your freedom, says this:
\q1 I am your god, Yahweh, who teaches you to prosper—
\q1 who leads you in the path that you should follow.
\q1
\v 18 If you had paid attention to my instructions
\q1 →then you would have had peace like a river
\q1 and your righteousness like waves in the ocean.
\q1
\v 19 Your descendants would have been \add as numerous\add* as the sand,
\q1 and the offspring from your womb like its grains.
\q1 It wouldn’t have been destroyed
\q1 and had its name removed from my presence.
\b
\q1
\v 20 Go away from Babylon.
\q1 Flee from those Chaldeans.
\q1 Declare this with a happy song.
\q1 Send it out to the ends of the earth to say:
\q1 Yahweh has paid the price to free his servant Jacob.
\q1
\v 21 They weren’t thirsty when he led them through the wildernesses.
\q1 He made water flow out of a rock for them—
\q1 he split open the rock and the water gushed out.
\b
\q1
\v 22 There’s no peace for the wicked says Yahweh.
\c 49
\s1 Yahweh’s servant
\s2 The second servant song
\q1
\v 1 Listen to me \add you\add* islands,
\q1 and pay attention \add you\add* distant peoples.
\q1 Yahweh called me from before I was born—
\q1 he knew my name when I was still inside my mother.
\q1
\v 2 He made my mouth like a sharp sword \add that\add* he hid in the shadow of his hand.
\q1 He made me into a polished arrow \add that\add* he hid in his quiver.
\q1
\v 3 Then he told me, ‘Israel, you’re my servant,
\q1 and I’ll be honoured by others because of you.’
\b
\q1
\v 4 I replied, ‘I’ve worked with no results
\q1 and used my strength for nothing at all.
\q1 ^Nevertheless, my justice is with Yahweh,
\q1 and my wages are with my god.’
\b
\q1
\v 5 Now Yahweh has spoken—
\q1 the one who formed me from the womb as his servant—
\q1 to restore Jacob to himself,
\q1 and gather Israel to him.
\q1 I’ll be honoured in Yahweh’s eyes,
\q1 and my god has been my strength.
\b
\q1
\v 6 Also he says,
\q1 ‘It’s too insignificant for you to be my servant to raise up Jacob’s tribes
\q1 and to bring back those from Israel who’ve been preserved.
\q1 I’ll make you into a light for the nations—
\q1 to be \add the one who\add* takes salvation to the ends of the earth for me.’
\b
\q1
\v 7 Yahweh, Israel’s holy one,
\q1 the one who paid the price for their freedom
\q1 says to the one whose life is despised,
\q1 the one who’s hated by the nations—a slave of rulers:
\q1 ‘Kings will see and stand up,
\q1 and princes will bow down
\q1 on account of Yahweh who is faithful—Israel’s holy one
\q1 because he has chosen you.’
\s1 Israel’s restoration
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh says this:
\q1 I will answer you in a time of favour,
\q1 and I will help you on a day of salvation
\q1 and protect you and appoint you to be an agreement for the people,
\q1 to rebuild the land so that desolate place can be redistributed as inheritances.
\q1
\v 9 You’ll tell the prisoners to come out
\q1 and those in darkness to show themselves.
\q1 They’ll graze along roads,
\q1 and over all the pasture on bare hills.
\q1
\v 10 They won’t be hungry or thirsty,
\q1 and the burning heat of the sun won’t beat down on them,
\q1 because they’ll be guided by \add one\add* who has compassion on them
\q1 and he’ll lead them to springs of water.
\b
\q1
\v 11 I’ll make all my mountains into a road,
\q1 and I’ll fill all the low spots on my highways.
\q1
\v 12 Look, they’ll come from far away,
\q1 and look, some from the north and from the west, and from Sinim.
\b
\q1
\v 13 Shout happily \add you\add* sky and be cheerful \add you\add* earth
\q1 and shout with joy \add you\add* mountains
\q1 because Yahweh has comforted his people
\q1 and has compassion on them when they’re suffering.
\p
\v 14 But Zion said, ‘Yahweh has abandoned me,
\q1 and my master has forgotten me.’
\p
\v 15 Would a woman forget her young baby
\q1 \add or\add* have no compassion on the child that she’s given birth to?
\q1 Even if she could forget, I won’t forget you.
\q1
\v 16 Look, I’ve written \add your name\add* on my palms.
\q1 I’m always thinking about your \add city\add* walls.
\b
\q1
\v 17 Your children are hurrying.
\q1 Those who destroyed and ravaged you, are going away from you.
\q1
\v 18 Look up and see all around you,
\q1 they’re all gathering together—coming towards you.
\q1 Yahweh declares, As surely as I live,
\q1 you’ll ‘put them all on’ like jewelry,
\q1 and you’ll tie them on like a bride.
\q1
\v 19 Look up because your ruined and desolate places,
\q1 and your devastated land,
\q1 will be too cramped for the coming inhabitants,
\q1 and those who swallowed you up will be far away.
\q1
\v 20 Yet your descendants who were born in captivity will say in your ears,
\q1 ‘This place is too cramped for us.
\q1 Move over to make room for us to live \add here\add*.’
\q1
\v 21 Then you’ll think to yourself, ‘Who fathered these children?
\q1 I was bereaved and barren—taken into exile and thrown away. Who raised these children?
\q1 I was left alone, \add so\add* where did these children \add come from\add*?’
\b
\q1
\v 22 My master Yahweh says this:
\q1 Look, I’ll lift my hand up to the nations,
\q1 and raise my banner to the peoples.
\q1 They’ll bring your sons on their laps,
\q1 and they’ll carry your daughters on their shoulders.
\q1
\v 23 Kings will become your children’s tutors,
\q1 and their noble ladies will become your wet nurses.
\q1 They’ll bow down to you with their faces to the ground
\q1 and lick the dust from your feet.
\q1 →Then you’ll know that I am Yahweh—those who patiently rely on my assistance won’t be humiliated.
\b
\q1
\v 24 Can the booty be snatched from a warrior,
\q1 or can captives escape from a tyrant.\f + \fr 49:24 \ft Using the reading from the Qumran scroll here.\f*
\q1
\v 25 But Yahweh says this:
\q1 Even the warrior’s captives will be taken \add from him\add*,
\q1 and the spoils will be recovered from a tyrant,
\q1 →and I’ll oppose your opponent and rescue your children.
\q1
\v 26 I’ll cause those who mistreated you to eat their own flesh,
\q1 and they’ll get drunk on the wine of their own blood.
\q1 →Then everybody will know that I am your rescuer, Yahweh,
\q1 and Jacob’s powerful one who paid the price for your freedom.
\c 50
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Where’s the divorce papers for your mother who I sent away?
\q1 Which of my creditors did I sell you all to,
\q1 because you were all sold because of your disobedience?
\q1 And your mother was sent away because of your rebellion.
\b
\q1
\v 2 Why did I come when there wasn’t anyone \add there\add*?
\q1 Why did I call when no one answered?
\q1 Was I unable to pay the ransom?
\q1 Didn’t I have enough strength to rescue you?
\q1 Look, when I scold the ocean, it dries up.
\q1 I can turn rivers into a wilderness—
\q1 →its fish will stink because they’ll die of thirst because there’s no water.
\q1
\v 3 I clothe the skies with darkness,
\q1 and I place \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth as their covering.
\s1 Yahweh’s obedient servant
\s2 The third servant song
\q1
\v 4 My master Yahweh, has given me a tongue that keeps learning—
\q1 knowing how to help the weary with a message.
\q1 He wakes me in the morning—
\q1 wakes me up to listen to his teaching.
\q1
\v 5 My master Yahweh, has opened my ears.
\q1 I haven’t been rebellious
\q1 \add and\add* I haven’t turned back \add from what I was asked\add*.
\q1
\v 6 I gave my back to those who struck me,
\q1 and my cheeks to those who made them bare.
\q1 I didn’t hide my face from insults and getting spat on
\q1
\v 7 but my master Yahweh will help me.
\q1 →Therefore I’m not humiliated,
\q1 →so I’ve made my face \add look determined\add* like flint
\q1 because I know that I won’t be ashamed.
\q1
\v 8 The one who’ll declare me innocent is close by.
\q1 Who will bring charges against me? Let’s stand \add in court\add* together.
\q1 Who is my accuser? Let him approach me.
\q1
\v 9 Listen, my master Yahweh will help me.
\q1 Who is it who condemns me as guilty?
\q1 They’ll all wear out like clothes—the moths will eat them.
\b
\q1
\v 10 How many of you strive to obey Yahweh,
\q1 listening to his servant’s words?
\q1 Who among you all walks in deep darkness without light?
\q1 \add Learn to\add* trust Yahweh and to depend on your god.
\q1
\v 11 Look, all of you who kindle fires and carry flaming torches.
\q1 Walk in the light from your fire and from the kindling that you’re all burning.
\q1 That is what you’ll receive from my hand.
\q1 You’ll all lie down in pain.
\c 51
\s1 Comforting words for Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 Listen to me, all of you who strive to do what’s right—
\q1 you who want to follow Yahweh’s instructions.
\q1 Look at the rock that you where chiselled from,
\q1 and at the mouth of the cistern that you were quarried out of.
\q1
\v 2 Look at your ancestor, Abraham,
\q1 and Sarah who bore you all,
\q1 because I called him as one person,
\q1 →then I blessed him and made him many,
\q1
\v 3 because Yahweh will comfort Zion.
\q1 He will comfort all its ruined places,
\q1 and make her wilderness like Eden
\q1 and her desert plains like Yahweh’s garden.
\q1 Joy and happiness will come from there—
\q1 thanksgiving and the sounds of music.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Pay attention to me, my people.
\q1 and \add you\add* my nation, listen carefully,
\q1 because I will send out my instructions,
\q1 and in an instant, I’ll make my justice as a light for the nations.
\q1
\v 5 I’m ready to declare you as innocent.
\q1 My salvation will go out.
\q1 My arms will bring justice to the peoples.
\q1 The islands wait eagerly—waiting for my arm.
\q1
\v 6 Look upwards to the skies
\q1 ^and \add down\add* to the earth below,
\q1 because the skies will be dispersed like smoke,
\q1 and the earth will wear out like a piece of clothing
\q1 and its inhabitants will die like gnats.
\q1 However, when I save you, you’ll be free forever,
\q1 and I’ll only ever do what is right.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Listen to me, all of you who know what is right—
\q1 people with my instructions \add always\add* in their thoughts.
\q1 Don’t be afraid of getting insulted by people,
\q1 and don’t be dismayed by their abuse,
\q1
\v 8 because moths will eat them like a piece of clothing,
\q1 and their larva will devour them like \add they do with\add* wool.
\q1 ^However, I will only ever do what is right,
\q1 and when I save you, you’ll be free throughout the generations.
\b
\q1
\v 9 Wake up, wake up! Yahweh, strengthen yourself!
\q1 Wake up like \add back\add* in the old days—
\q1 the generations from long ago.
\q1 Wasn’t it you who pierced the sea monster Rahab and cut it into pieces?
\q1
\v 10 Wasn’t it you who dried up the ocean—those large, deep waters—
\q1 \add the one\add* who made the deep seas into a way for those who’ve been set free to pass over?
\q1
\v 11 Then those who Yahweh has paid the price for their freedom, will return,
\q1 and go to Zion with happy shouts,
\q1 and with happiness always showing on their faces.
\q1 Gladness and joy will overtake them—
\q1 sorrow and mourning will disappear.
\b
\q1
\v 12 It’s me who’s the one who comforts you all.
\q1 Why would you be afraid of \add other\add* humans who are just mortal,
\q1 or \add other\add* descendants of humankind who are a temporary as grass.
\q1
\v 13 Then you’ve forgotten Yahweh who made you
\q1 and who stretched out the skies and made the earth’s foundations.
\q1 You’ve been in dread the entire day as your angry oppressor approaches ready to destroy you.
\q1 But where is the oppressor’s anger \add now\add*?
\q1
\v 14 The one who stoops over will be quickly released.
\q1 He won’t die in the pit and won’t lack his food.
\b
\q1
\v 15 I am your god Yahweh who stirs up the sea
\q1 and its waves roar.
\q1 Commander-in-chief Yahweh is his name.
\q1
\v 16 I’ve given you my messages to declare,
\q1 and I’ve protected you with my hand—
\q1 planting the skies and founding the earth,
\q1 and telling Zion that you are my people.
\s1 The cup of anger removed from Jerusalem
\q1
\v 17 Rouse yourself, rouse yourself. Get up, Jerusalem—
\q1 you who drank the cup from Yahweh’s hand \add that was filled with\add* his anger.
\q1 You drank the cup that makes you stagger—you drained it.
\q1
\v 18 Despite all the children that she’s borne, there’s no one to lead her,
\q1 and there’s no one to hold her hand out of all the children that she’s brought up.
\b
\q1
\v 19 Those two things have happened to you:
\q1 Who shows sympathy to you \add after\add* the devastation and destruction?
\q1 Who comforts you \add after\add* the famine and war?
\q1
\v 20 Your children have fainted and they lie at the end of every street like antelope \add caught\add* in a net.
\q1 They’re full of Yahweh’s anger—the rebuke from your god,
\q1
\v 21 so listen to this \add you\add* oppressed one,
\q1 and \add the one\add* who’s drunk, but not from wine.
\q1
\v 22 This is what your master Yahweh,
\q1 and your god who defends your people, says:
\q1 Listen, I’ve taken that cup of suffering out of your hand—
\q1 the goblet containing my rage. You’ll no longer have to drink it.
\q1
\v 23 Instead, I’ll put it into your tormentors’ hand—
\q1 those who told you to bow down so they could walk over the top of you,
\q1 and made your back like the ground or the street for walking on.
\c 52
\s1 Yahweh will rescue Jerusalem
\q1
\v 1 Wake up, wake up, strengthen yourself Zion.
\q1 Put your beautiful clothes on, Jerusalem, sacred city,
\q1 because the uncircumcised and the ‘unclean’ people won’t enter you again.
\q1
\v 2 Shake yourself free from the dust.
\q1 Take action, \add you\add* captive Jerusalem.
\q1 Release the chains from your neck, \add you\add* captive daughter of Zion
\q1
\v 3 because Yahweh says this:
\q1 You were sold for nothing
\q1 and you’ll gain your freedom without any payment,
\q1
\v 4 because my master Yahweh says this:
\q1 My people went down to Egypt to stay there long ago,
\q1 and then Assyria has oppressed the nation for nothing.
\p
\v 5 Now Yahweh declares:
\q1 My people were taken for no reason.
\q1 His rulers mock him
\q1 and my name is continually slandered throughout the day.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 6 Therefore my people will know my name because on that day
\q1 it will be me speaking and saying, ‘Here I am.’
\q1
\v 7 How delightful is the one who brings good news across the mountains announcing peace—
\q1 bringing good news that announces a coming rescuer,
\q1 \add and\add* telling Zion that their god reigns.
\q1
\v 8 Your watchmen will \add all\add* call out loudly at the same time.
\q1 They’ll \add all\add* shout happily because each of them will see that Yahweh has returned to Zion.
\b
\q1
\v 9 Start shouting out happily \add you\add* ruined places in Jerusalem
\q1 because Yahweh has encouraged his people—he’s paid the price for Jerusalem’s freedom.
\q1
\v 10 In front of all the nations, Yahweh has revealed that he’s holy and powerful,
\q1 and \add even\add* the ends of the earth will see that our god rescues his people.
\q1
\v 11 Get out, get out, leave that place! Don’t touch anything that’s ‘unclean’.
\q1 Get out from among it. Purify yourselves, all of you who carry Yahweh within you,
\q1
\v 12 because you won’t leave out in a rush.
\q1 You all won’t have to flee \add hurriedly\add*
\q1 because Yahweh will go ahead of you all,
\q1 and Israel’s god will bring up the rear.
\s1 The suffering servant
\s2 The fourth servant song
\p
\v 13 Listen, my servant will prosper—
\q1 he’ll be promoted and highly honoured.
\q1
\v 14 Many people were horrified at you—his appearance was so disfigured
\q1 and his body was so disfigured that he no longer looked like a man.
\q1
\v 15 So he’ll startle many nations.
\q1 Kings will shut their mouths because even if they hadn’t been told about it,
\q1 they’ll see and understand what they hadn’t heard about.
\c 53
\p
\v 1 Who has believed our message,
\q1 and who has Yahweh’s power been revealed to?
\q1
\v 2 He grew in front of him like a young plant,
\q1 and like a root out of the dry ground.
\q1 He had no \add fancy\add* form or splendour to look at,
\q1 and his appearance didn’t attract us to him.
\q1
\v 3 He was despised and rejected by people—
\q1 a man with a lot of pain and familiar with sickness
\q1 like someone that most people would look away from
\q1 and despise him, rather than to esteem him.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Despite that, he’s borne our sicknesses
\q1 and carried our pains.
\q1 We thought that he’d been struck down by God—
\q1 attacked and made to suffer.
\q1
\v 5 But he was pierced for our disobedience,
\q1 and crushed for our wicked actions.
\q1 The punishment that \add gave us\add* peace was on him,
\q1 and we were healed \add thanks\add* to his wounds.
\q1
\v 6 We’ve all strayed away like sheep—
\q1 everyone’s gone in their own direction
\q1 and Yahweh has placed all our disobedience onto him.
\b
\q1
\v 7 He was oppressed and tormented
\q1 but like a lamb being led away to be slaughtered, he didn’t open his mouth,
\q1 and like a sheep that’s silent in front of its shearers, he didn’t open his mouth.
\q1
\v 8 After oppression and judgement, he was taken away,
\q1 but how many of his peers even cared that he was cut off from the land of the living?
\q1 He was wounded because of the disobedience of my people.
\q1
\v 9 His burial place was among the criminals,
\q1 and a rich man at his death,
\q1 ^although he’d committed no violent acts,
\q1 and had never spoken deceitfully.
\b
\q1
\v 10 But Yahweh desired to crush him—
\q1 he made \add him\add* weak.
\q1 If he would make his life a guilt offering,
\q1 he’ll live long and see offspring,
\q1 and Yahweh’s plans will prosper through his work.
\q1
\v 11 Due to his internal anguish, he’ll be able to see.
\q1 He’ll be satisfied with his knowledge.
\q1 My obedient servant will declare many to be innocent,
\q1 and he will carry their disobedience.
\q1
\v 12 Because of that, I’ll allocate a portion for him.
\q1 He’ll divide out the plunder to a powerful people
\q1 because he poured out his life resulting in his death.
\q1 He was included with the wicked,
\q1 and he bore the disobedience of many people,
\q1 and he intervened on behalf of the disobedient.
\c 54
\s1 Yahweh’s love for Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 Sing happily \add you\add* barren women who has never given birth.
\q1 Break into a cheery song and celebrate, \add you\add* who’ve never been in labour,
\q1 because the desolate woman \add now\add* has more children than the married woman did says Yahweh.
\q1
\v 2 Widen your tent and stretch your tent curtains further out without restraint.
\q1 Make your tent ropes longer and drive your stakes in deep
\q1
\v 3 because you’ll spread out \add both\add* to the right and to the left,
\q1 and your descendants will conquer nations
\q1 and settle in cities that had become desolate.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Don’t be afraid because you won’t be put to shame,
\q1 and don’t be discouraged because you won’t be disgraced,
\q1 because you’ll forget the shame from your youth.
\q1 You’ll no longer remember the disgrace from when you were made a widow
\q1
\v 5 because your husband is the one who made you—
\q1 his name is Commander-in-chief Yahweh.
\q1 Israel’s holy one is your protector.
\q1 He’s called the god of the whole world.
\b
\q1
\v 6 Yes, Yahweh has called you back
\q1 as if you were an abandoned wife with a wounded spirit,
\q1 or a young wife who was rejected.
\p Your god says:
\q1
\v 7 I abandoned you for a little while,
\q1 ^but \add now\add* I’ll bring you back with great compassion.
\q1
\v 8 I kept away from you for a while in a flood of severe anger,
\q1 but with my permanent loyal commitment, I’ll have compassion on you.
\p Yahweh who paid the price for your freedom says:
\q1
\v 9 For me, this is like Noah’s flood—
\q1 just as I promised to never again to cover the earth,
\q1 so \add too\add* I promise not to be angry at you or to rebuke you.
\q1
\v 10 Even if the mountains disappear and the hills shake,
\q1 my loyal commitment to you won’t disappear and my agreement for peace won’t be shaken,
\q1 says Yahweh, the one who has compassion for you.
\s1 The new Jerusalem
\p
\v 11 You \add city\add* which is suffering like being battered in a storm
\q1 with no one to comfort you,
\q1 listen, I’ll rebuild you with turquoise stones,
\q1 and place sapphires in your foundations.
\q1
\v 12 I’ll make your towers out of rubies
\q1 and your gates from beryl stones,
\q1 and all your walls from other precious stones.
\b
\q1
\v 13 All your children will be followers of Yahweh,
\q1 and they’ll experience extended peace.
\b
\q1
\v 14 You’ll be established with justice—far away from oppression.
\q1 You won’t be afraid of \add acts of\add* terror because it won’t approach you.
\b
\q1
\v 15 Listen, no one will attack you that I haven’t sent.
\q1 Anyone stirring up trouble against you will fail.
\b
\q1
\v 16 Look, I created the craftsman who fans the charcoal fire,
\q1 and produces weapons as his work,
\q1 and it’s me who created a destroyer to cause destruction.
\q1
\v 17 No weapon that’s made to be used against you will succeed,
\q1 and those who speak out accusations against you will be condemned as guilty.
\q1 That’s the inheritance of Yahweh’s servants,
\q1 and I, Yahweh, will pronounce them as innocent.
\c 55
\s1 Come and freely find Yahweh
\p
\v 1 Hey, \add you\add* who are thirsty, go to the water supplies,
\q1 and \add you\add* who have no money, go and buy grain and eat.
\q1 Yes, come and buy grain without money,
\q1 and wine and milk with no price on them.
\q1
\v 2 Why pay money for what’s not food,
\q1 and work hard for what doesn’t satisfy?
\q1 Listen carefully to me and eat good things
\q1 and enjoy rich foods.
\q1
\v 3 Pay attention and come to me.
\q1 Listen so you all can live,
\q1 and I’ll make a permanent agreement with you all—
\q1 the reliable, loyal commitment \add shown to\add* David.
\q1
\v 4 Listen, I’ve made him a witness to the nations—
\q1 a leader and commander of the people \add groups\add*.
\q1
\v 5 Look, you’ll call a nation that you didn’t know,
\q1 and that nation that didn’t know you \add either\add*, will run to you
\q1 because of your god Yahweh,
\q1 and because Israel’s holy one has caused you to be honoured.
\b
\q1
\v 6 Search for Yahweh while he lets himself be found.
\q1 Call out to Yahweh \add for help\add* while he’s near.
\q1
\v 7 Let the wicked person abandon their behaviour,
\q1 and the evil person abandon their plans.
\q1 →Then let them return to Yahweh so that he can have compassion for him,
\q1 and return to our god because he’ll freely forgive them.
\p
\v 8 Yes, my thoughts aren’t \add like\add* your thoughts,
\q1 and your ways aren’t \add like\add* my ways.
\m That’s what Yahweh declares.
\q1
\v 9 Yes, \add just\add* as the skies are higher than the earth,
\q1 so too my ways are higher than your ways
\q1 and my thoughts are higher than your thoughts.
\b
\q1
\v 10 Just like how the rain and snow come down from the skies
\q1 and don’t return there, but water the land
\q1 that then makes it produce and sprout,
\q1 seed is given to the sower and bread to the consumer,
\q1
\v 11 so too when my message leaves my mouth.
\q1 It won’t return to me empty,
\q1 but when it’s done what was desired,
\q1 and after having prospered \add in the place where\add* I sent it.
\b
\q1
\v 12 You’ll all go out cheerfully
\q1 and be led in peace.
\q1 The mountains and hills will let out a happy shout,
\q1 and all the trees in the countryside will clap their hands.
\q1
\v 13 Instead of thornbushes, cypress \add trees\add* will grow up.
\q1 Rather than nettles, myrtle \add trees\add* will rise up,
\q1 and it’ll \add increase\add* Yahweh’s reputation
\q1 as a permanent sign that will never be destroyed.
\c 56
\s1 Salvation for others
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says this:
\q1 Promote justice and do what is right
\q1 because the time when I’ll save you is coming soon,
\q1 and my righteousness will be revealed.
\q1
\v 2 Everyone who does that will be blessed,
\q1 and the son of humanity who takes hold of it,
\q1 who observes the rest day—not defiling it,
\q1 and who stays away from doing anything evil.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Don’t let the son of any foreigner who’s decided to follow Yahweh,
\q1 say, ‘Yahweh will certainly separate me out from his people.’
\q1 And don’t let the castrated slave say, ‘Look, I’m a withered-up tree,’
\q1
\v 4 because Yahweh says this:
\q1 To the castrated slaves who observe my rest days
\q1 and choose what pleases me and are faithful to my agreement,
\q1
\v 5 I will give them a monument and a good name in my house and inside my walls
\q1 that’s better than sons and daughters.
\q1 I’ll give them a permanent \add good\add* reputation which won’t be terminated.
\q1
\v 6 Then the children of foreigners who strive to follow Yahweh,
\q1 to serve him and to love his name, \add and\add* to be his servants,
\q1 who observe the rest day without defiling it,
\q1 and who cling to my agreement,
\q1
\v 7 I’ll bring them to my sacred mountain
\q1 and make them happy in my temple where people pray.
\q1 Their burnt offerings and sacrifices will be acceptable on my altar,
\q1 because my temple will be called a prayer house for all the nations.
\p
\v 8 My master Yahweh, who gathers Israel’s outcasts, says this:
\q1 I’ll gather others \add beside those already\add* gathered.
\s1 The judging of teachers of Israel
\p
\v 9 All you animals in the countryside, come to devour,
\q1 \add yes,\add* all you animals in the forest.
\q1
\v 10 All of its watchmen are blind—they don’t know what’s going on.
\q1 They’re all voiceless dogs, unable to bark.
\q1 They dream about lying down—enjoying a nap.
\q1
\v 11 The dogs have a fierce appetite—they’re never full.
\q1 The shepherds know nothing and don’t understand.
\q1 They’ve all gone their own way and make money from their illegal sidelines.
\q1
\v 12 Come on, take some wine and let’s drink strong drink.
\q1 Tomorrow will be just like today except we’ll have even more.
\c 57
\s1 Israel’s idolatry is condemned
\p
\v 1 The godly people die but no one takes it to heart.
\q1 Those with loyal commitment are removed but no one knows what’s going on
\q1 because the godly people are pulled back from facing evil.
\q1
\v 2 They go in peace.
\q1 They’ve walked on a straightforward \add path\add*
\q1 \add and now\add* they rest on their beds.
\b
\q1
\v 3 But come here \add you\add* children of a sorceress—
\q1 descendants of adulterers and prostitutes.
\q1
\v 4 Who are you all making fun of?
\q1 Who are you speaking out against and poking your tongues out against?
\q1 You’re all children of wickedness—offspring of deception
\q1
\v 5 who burn with lust under oak \add trees\add*—\add in fact\add* under every green tree.
\q1 You all slaughter children in valleys under the crevices in the cliffs.
\q1
\v 6 You \add stand up large,\add* smooth stones from the riverbed
\q1 and offer them a portion of your food.
\q1 You pour out \add wine as\add* a drink offering and offer up a grain offering.
\q1 Will I relent \add in taking action about those activities\add*?
\b
\q1
\v 7 You slept up high at the hilltop shrines
\q1 where you went up to also offer a sacrifice,
\q1
\v 8 and you’ve put your symbols behind the door and doorposts.
\q1 Yes, away from me you stripped off
\q1 and filled your bed with others.
\q1 You had an agreement with those whose bed you loved—
\q1 enjoying their bodies.
\q1
\v 9 You took oil and travelled to the king,
\q1 along with lots of perfumes.
\q1 Then you sent your ambassadors to distant places,
\q1 and even descended to the world of the dead.
\q1
\v 10 You were tired from your long journey,
\q1 ^but you didn’t say that it’s all just hopeless.
\q1 You found new things to do so then you didn’t become weak.
\b
\q1
\v 11 Who very you so afraid of that you lie,
\q1 and don’t remember me or think about me?
\q1 Haven’t I been silent for a long \add time now\add*
\q1 →so it’s not me that you’re afraid of.
\q1
\v 12 I’ll talk about your activities and how you do what’s right
\q1 but they won’t benefit you.
\q1
\v 13 When you call out \add for help\add*, let your collection rescue you,
\q1 but a wind will carry them all away—
\q1 a breath will take them away.
\q1 But those who rely on me for safety will inherit the land,
\q1 and will take possession of my holy mountain.
\s1 God will help and heal
\p
\v 14 Then he’ll say:
\q1 Pile it up, pile it up, make a track \add through\add*.
\q1 Remove the stumbling blocks from the path for my people.
\b
\p
\v 15 The \add one\add* who’s high and honoured, who lives forever and whose name is holy, says:
\q1 I live in a place that’s holy and up high,
\q1 but also with the oppressed person and with the humble person,
\q1 →to restore the spirit of the humble and to encourage those who \add feel\add* crushed.
\q1
\v 16 However, I won’t argue the point forever,
\q1 and I won’t remain permanently angry,
\q1 because those who stand in front of me would faint—
\q1 the creatures that I myself made.
\q1
\v 17 I was angry at the wickedness of their dishonest gain and I struck them.
\q1 I hid and I was angry, but they decided to go against me.
\b
\q1
\v 18 I’ve seen their ways and I’ll heal them,
\q1 and I’ll lead them and comfort them and their mourners.
\q1
\v 19 I’ll enable them to shout happily,
\q1 ‘Peace, peace, to those \add both\add* near and far,’
\q1 and I will heal them says Yahweh.
\q1
\v 20 But the wicked are like the ocean in a storm,
\q1 because it’s unable to stay calm, but rather it stirs up slush and mud.
\q1
\v 21 My god says that there’s no peace for those who are wicked.
\c 58
\s1 Don’t fast just for looks
\p
\v 1 Shout loudly. Don’t hold back.
\q1 Raise your voice like a trumpet
\q1 and confront my people with their wickedness,
\q1 and Jacob’s descendants with their disobedience.
\q1
\v 2 They search for me every day
\q1 and want to learn about my ways like a nation that does what is right,
\q1 and they haven’t abandoned the values of their god.
\q1 They ask me to make judgements that are far and right.
\q1 They want god to approach them.
\q1
\v 3 Why did we fast, as you didn’t even look?
\q1 We humbled ourselves and you didn’t even notice?
\b
\q1 Listen, when you fasted was it just for your own needs?
\q1 You all certainly drive your labourers hard!
\q1
\v 4 Look, you all fast with strife and contention,
\q1 and strike out with vicious clenched fists.
\q1 You all don’t fast today to make your voices heard up on high.
\q1
\v 5 Is that the kind of fasting that I would choose?
\q1 A day when a person humbles themself and bows their head down like a bulrush does.
\q1 \add They wear\add* \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth and form a bed of ashes \add to sit on\add*.
\q1 Is that \add really\add* a fast that’s acceptable to Yahweh?
\b
\q1
\v 6 Isn’t this the fast that I would prefer?
\q1 \add When you all\add* remove chains that were wickedly \add put on others\add*,
\q1 and undo the ropes from the yoke \add that forced them to comply\add*?
\q1 Let go all those who’ve been crushed,
\q1 and break every yoke \add that burdened them\add*.
\q1
\v 7 Isn’t it to share your food \add with those who are hungry\add*,
\q1 and to bring the poor, homeless people into your house?
\q1 When you see \add someone\add* naked, clothe them,
\q1 and don’t hide yourself from your own relatives.
\b
\q1
\v 8 Then your light will break through like the dawn,
\q1 and your healing will quickly spring up.
\q1 Your right actions will go ahead of you
\q1 and Yahweh’s honour will follow along behind you.
\q1
\v 9 Then you’ll call out and Yahweh will answer.
\q1 You’ll cry for help and he’ll say, ‘Here I am.’
\b
\q1 If you remove the \add burden of\add* the yoke from among you,
\q1 \add as well as\add* the pointing finger and wicked speech,
\q1
\v 10 give yourself to the hungry
\q1 and give those who are suffering what they need
\q1 →then your light will rise in the darkness
\q1 and your darkness will be like the noonday.
\q1
\v 11 Then Yahweh will continually lead you
\q1 and satisfy your appetite in arid regions.
\q1 He’ll strengthen your bones and will be like a watered garden,
\q1 and like a spring that never runs dry.
\q1
\v 12 Then some of you will rebuild the ancient ruins—
\q1 you’ll restore the foundations from generations ago
\q1 and you’ll be called the repairer of the wall—
\q1 the one who restores streets to live in.
\s1 Blessings for keeping the rest day
\p
\v 13 If on the rest day, you turn back from what you wanted to do
\q1 and don’t try to please yourself on my sacred day,
\q1 enjoy Yahweh’s sacred and honourable rest day,
\q1 and honour it instead of doing your own activities such as finding your own pleasure or delivering a speech,
\q1
\v 14 then you’ll take delight in Yahweh.
\q1 I’ll make you ride on the heights of the earth,
\q1 and I’ll give you the same blessings that I gave your ancestor, Jacob.
\q1 That’s what Yahweh has spoken.
\c 59
\s1 Israel’s sins and punishment
\p
\v 1 Listen, \add don’t think that Yahweh can’t\add* rescue you,
\q1 or that he won’t be able to hear you.
\q1
\v 2 However, your disobedience causes separation between you all and your god,
\q1 and your sins have made him turn away from listening to you,
\q1
\v 3 because your hands are defiled with blood,
\q1 and your fingers with sin.
\q1 Your lips \add spew out\add* lies
\q1 and your tongue \add works against justice\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 4 No one stands up for justice,
\q1 and no one pleads their case truthfully.
\q1 They rely on being meaningless, or fancy words of no value—
\q1 conceiving mischief and advancing wickedness.
\q1
\v 5 They hatch vipers’ eggs
\q1 and weave spiders’ webs
\q1 Anyone who eats any of their eggs will die—
\q1 when they’re cracked open, a viper will be hatched.
\q1
\v 6 Their weavings aren’t for clothes,
\q1 so they don’t cover themselves with their products.
\q1 Their products are products of wickedness,
\q1 and their hands are involved in violence.
\q1
\v 7 Their feet run towards evil,
\q1 and they’re quick to shed innocent blood.
\q1 They’re always thinking about wickedness—
\q1 they walk the paths of \add causing\add* ruin and destruction.
\q1
\v 8 They don’t know the way of peace,
\q1 and there’s no justice on their tracks.
\q1 They’ve make their paths crooked—
\q1 none of those who tread on them will experience peace.
\s1 Societal sinfulness
\p
\v 9 Therefore, justice is far from us
\q1 and righteousness doesn’t reach us.
\q1 We wait for light, but look, \add we get\add* darkness.
\q1 We wait for gleams of light, \add but\add* walk in the gloom.
\q1
\v 10 We grope \add along\add* the wall like the blind,
\q1 and feel around as though we had no eyes.
\q1 Even at midday, we stumble as if it’s twilight—
\q1 among the living \add but behaving\add* like the dead.
\q1
\v 11 All of us growl like bears,
\q1 and moan mournfully like doves.
\q1 We keep hoping for justice, but none comes,
\q1 for rescue, but it’s far away
\q1
\v 12 because our transgressions in front of you are many.
\q1 Our sins have testified against us
\q1 because our transgressions are with us,
\q2 and as for our iniquities, we know them.
\q1
\v 13 \add We’ve\add* rebelled and denied Yahweh,
\q1 and turned away from following our god—
\q1 promoting oppression and rebellion \add instead\add*.
\q1 We’ve conceived and told lies as if they were the heart-felt truth.
\q1
\v 14 Justice is driven right back,
\q1 and righteousness stands off at a distance,
\q1 because truth has stumbled in the public square,
\q1 and honesty can’t enter.
\q1
\v 15 So then truth went missing,
\q1 and the one who turned away from evil got robbed.
\q1 Yahweh saw it and wasn’t pleased that there was no justice.
\s1 The assisting of Master of his people
\q1
\v 16 Yahweh saw that there wasn’t anyone \add to make a stand\add*,
\q1 and was appalled that there was no one to intervene.
\q1 So he applied his own power to bring salvation,
\q1 and his own desire to do what’s right, sustained him.
\q1
\v 17 He put righteousness on like armour,
\q1 and a helmet of salvation on his head.
\q1 He dressed in clothes of vengeance
\q1 and wrapped himself with zeal like a robe.
\q1
\v 18 He will repay his enemies for the evil things that they have done.
\q1 He will repay the islands with the appropriate reward.
\q1
\v 19 Then those in the west will respect Yahweh’s reputation,
\q1 and in the east, will respect his splendour,
\q1 →because he’ll come like a swift river with Yahweh’s wind driving it.
\p
\v 20 Then a redeemer will come to Zion
\q1 and to those \add descendants of\add* Jacob who repent of their disobedience.
\m That’s what Yahweh declares.
\p
\v 21 Then Yahweh says:
\q1 This is my agreement with them.
\q1 My spirit which is on you and my messages that I’ve put in your mouth,
\q1 they won’t leave your mouth or the mouths of your descendants,
\q1 even the distant generations from now and forever \add onwards\add*.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\c 60
\s1 Jerusalem’s future splendour
\q1
\v 1 Take action, shine, because your light has come,
\q1 and Yahweh’s brilliance has risen to shine on you,
\q1
\v 2 Yes, listen, darkness will cover the earth.
\q1 and thick darkness will blanket the nations,
\q1 and then Yahweh will dawn over you,
\q1 and his brilliance will appear over you.
\q1
\v 3 Nations will come to your light,
\q1 and kings to your rising brightness.
\q1
\v 4 Look up and all around and see all of them gathered together.
\q1 Your sons will come from far away,
\q1 and your \add little\add* daughters will be carried \add back\add* on the hip.
\q1
\v 5 Then you’ll see and be radiant,
\q1 and your heart will be in awe, and broaden,
\q1 because plenty of international shipping will come to you,
\q1
\b
\q1
\v 6 Plenty of camels will cover your \add land\add*—
\q1 young camels from Midian and Eyfah.
\q1 All of them will come from Sheba.
\q1 They’ll bring gold and frankincense
\q1 and will announce praises to Yahweh.
\q1
\v 7 All the flocks from Kedar will be gathered for you.
\q1 The rams from Nevayot will serve you.
\q1 They’ll be accepted to go up onto my altar,
\q1 and I’ll decorate my house beautifully.
\b
\q1
\v 8 Who are these who fly like a cloud,
\q1 and like doves to their windows?
\q1
\v 9 The islands wait for me, and the ships from Tarshish
\q1 will be first to bring your sons from far away
\q1 along with their gold and silver \add to honour\add* your god Yahweh
\q1 and for Israel’s holy one because he’s honoured you.
\p
\v 10 The sons of foreigners will rebuild your \add city\add* walls,
\q1 and their kings will serve you,
\q1 because although I struck you in my severe anger,
\q1 yet in my favour I’ve had compassion towards you.
\q1
\v 11 Then your \add city\add* gates will be always open—
\q1 they won’t be shut day or night,
\q1 so the wealth from \add other\add* nations can be brought to you,
\q1 with their kings being led \add captive\add*.
\q1
\v 12 Yes, any nation or kingdom that won’t serve you will perish,
\q1 and the nations will be completely destroyed.
\b
\q1
\v 13 Lebanon’s best will come to you—
\q1 cypress, fir, and cedar \add timber\add* all at once,
\q1 to make my sanctuary beautiful
\q1 and I’ll honour the place where I stand.
\q1
\v 14 The descendants of those who oppressed you will come to you
\q1 and all those who despised you will bow down at your feet.
\q1 They will call you Yahweh’s city—
\q1 Israel’s holy one—Zion.
\b
\q1
\v 15 Instead of your being abandoned and hated, with no one passing through you,
\q1 I’ll make you a thing of perpetual pride—the joy of future generations.
\q1
\v 16 You’ll suckle the milk from the nations,
\q1 and feed from the breast of kings,
\q1 and you know that I, Yahweh, am your rescuer and your redeemer—Jacob’s powerful one.
\b
\q1
\v 17 Instead of bronze and iron, I’ll bring gold and silver,
\q1 and instead of timber and stones, bronze and iron.
\q1 →Then I’ll appoint peace as your overseer,
\q1 and righteousness as your supervisors.
\q1
\v 18 Violence won’t be experienced in your land—
\q1 no more devastation or destruction in your territories.
\q1 You’ll realise that salvation protects you like \add city\add* walls
\q1 and praise like \add your city\add* gates.
\b
\q1
\v 19 The sun will no longer be your light during the day,
\q1 nor will the moon’s brightness shine for you,
\q1 but rather, Yahweh will be your perpetual light,
\q1 and your god will be your brilliant light.
\q1
\v 20 Your sun will no longer set,
\q1 and your moon will no longer disappear,
\q1 because Yahweh will be your perpetual light,
\q1 and your days of mourning will be over.
\q1
\v 21 As for your people, all of them will do what is right.
\q1 They will permanently possess the land—the shoot that I planted—
\q1 my handiwork to showcase my greatness.
\q1
\v 22 The little one will become a thousand,
\q1 and the little nation will become powerful.
\q1 I, Yahweh, will quickly accomplish it at the appropriate time.
\c 61
\s1 The assisting of Israel
\q1
\v 1 My master, Yahweh’s spirit is on me
\q1 because Yahweh has chosen me
\q1 →to bring good news to those who are suffering.
\q1 He’s sent me to bandage up the brokenhearted,
\q1 to decree freedom for those taken captive,
\q1 and to liberate those who are chained up.
\q1
\v 2 He’s sent me to declare the year of Yahweh’s favour,
\q1 and a day of our god’s vengeance,
\q1 and to comfort all those who mourn.
\q1
\v 3 It’ll be a time to give Zion’s mourners a \add nice\add* turban instead of ashes \add on their heads\add*,
\q1 oil \add to show\add* joy \add on their faces\add* instead of mourning,
\q1 a cloak of praise instead of a discouraged spirit.
\q1 →Then they’ll be called ‘oaks of righteousness’ that Yahweh planted to display his splendour.
\q1
\v 4 They’ll rebuild the ancient ruins—
\q1 restoring the places that had become deserted.
\q1 They’ll rebuild the devastated cities,
\q1 and places that have been in ruins for generations.
\b
\q1
\v 5 Strangers will stand \add out in your fields\add* and shepherd your flocks,
\q1 and sons of foreigners will take care of \add your farms and vineyards\add*.
\q1
\v 6 Then you’ll all be called Yahweh’s priests,
\q1 and it’ll be said that you’re servants of our god.
\q1 You’ll consume the wealth from the nations
\q1 →and you’ll also boast because there’ll be so much of it.
\q1
\v 7 Instead of shame, you’ll all have a double blessing,
\q1 and \add instead of\add* dishonour, they’ll shout happily about their portion,
\q1 \add Yes,\add* they’ll possess a double portion in their land
\q1 and they’ll have permanent happiness
\q1
\v 8 because I, Yahweh, love justice and hate theft and crime.
\q1 Due to my faithfulness, I’ll repay them for what they’ve been through,
\q1 and I’ll make a permanent agreement with them.
\q1
\v 9 Then their descendants will be known among the nations,
\q1 and their offspring among the people \add groups\add*.
\q1 Everyone who sees them will recognise them
\q1 because they are the people who Yahweh has blessed.
\b
\q1
\v 10 I’m going to really celebrate Yahweh.
\q1 My inner being will be glad about my god
\q1 because he has dressed me in clothes of a free person.
\q1 He’s clothed me with the robe of a person declared innocent
\q1 like the groom puts on a \add nice\add* turban,
\q1 and the bride adorns herself with jewelry.
\q1
\v 11 Just as the soil produces growth,
\q1 and as the seeds in the garden start to sprout,
\q1 so too my master, Yahweh, will make righteousness grow
\q1 as well as praise towards all the nations.
\c 62
\s1 Jerusalem’s coming rescue
\q1
\v 1 For the sake of Zion, I won’t be silent.
\q1 and for Jerusalem’s sake, I won’t be quiet,
\q1 until its righteousness proceeds as brightness
\q1 and its salvation like a burning torch.
\q1
\v 2 Then the \add other\add* nations will see your righteousness,
\q1 and all kings will see your glory.
\q1 and you’ll be called by a new name that Yahweh \add himself\add* will give.
\q1
\v 3 You’ll be a beautiful crown in Yahweh’s hand,
\q1 and a royal turban in your god’s palm.
\q1
\v 4 They won’t claim any longer that you’ve been abandoned,
\q1 and they won’t say again that your land has been deserted,
\q1 because you’ll be called ‘\add the place that\add* Yahweh likes’.
\q1 Also, your land will be called ‘married’
\q1 because your land will be married \add to him\add*.
\q1
\v 5 Just like a young man marries a young woman,
\q1 your sons will marry you,
\q1 and as the groom is full of happiness about his bride,
\q1 your god will be full of happiness about you.
\b
\q1
\v 6 I’ve put watchmen on your walls, Jerusalem.
\q1 They’ll never be silent—night or day.
\q1 You all who are reminding Yahweh, don’t ever stop to rest—
\q1
\v 7 don’t let him rest until he reestablishes Jerusalem,
\q1 and until he makes it something to be praised by the world.
\b
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh has using the strength from his power,
\q1 and raised his right hand to make a promise:
\q1 I’ll won’t give your grain to your enemies again for their food,
\q1 and foreigners’ children won’t be drinking the new wine that you worked so hard to make,
\q1
\v 9 because those who harvested the grain will eat it,
\q1 and those who collected \add the grapes\add* will drink in my sacred courtyards.
\b
\q1
\v 10 Go through, head out the \add city\add* gates. Clear the way for the people.
\q1 Build up, build up the highway. Clear away the stones to raise up a banner for the nations.
\q1
\v 11 Listen, Yahweh has declared to the ends of the earth:
\q1 Tell Zion’s daughter to look because her rescue is about to happen.
\q1 Look, he’s brought his reward with him,
\q1 and his wages go ahead of him.
\q1
\v 12 The ones that Yahweh has paid to have freed
\q1 will be called ‘the sacred people’ by others.
\q1 Also, you’ll be called ‘the desirable city’—
\q1 the one which hasn’t been abandoned.
\c 63
\s1 Yahweh’s vengeance
\p
\v 1 Who’s that coming from Edom,
\q1 coming from Batserah dressed in crimson?
\q1 This one dressed like an honoured person,
\q1 marching in the greatness of his strength?
\q1 It’s me, \add Yahweh\add*, declaring what’s right—powerful to rescue.
\b
\q1
\v 2 What’s that red on your clothes?
\q1 Your clothes look like you’ve been treading \add grapes\add* in a wine-pressing \add pit\add*?
\b
\q1
\v 3 I’ve trodden in the wine-pressing \add pit\add* alone,
\q1 and there wasn’t anyone from the nations with me.
\q1 I trod them in my anger,
\q1 and trampled them in my fury.
\q1 and their blood is splattered onto my clothes.
\q1 All my clothes are stained
\q1
\v 4 because I had decided that this was the day of vengeance,
\q1 and the year had come for me to buy my people’s freedom.
\q1
\v 5 I looked and there wasn’t anyone to help,
\q1 and I was appalled that no one assisted,
\q1 but my power gave me victory,
\q1 and my rage sustained me.
\q1
\v 6 I trod down peoples in my anger,
\q1 and in my rage, I made them drunk,
\q1 and I poured their blood out on the ground.
\s1 Remembering Yahweh’s goodness
\q1
\v 7 I’ll tell others about Yahweh’s loyal commitment—
\q1 praising him for his treatment of us.
\q1 Yes, for his amazing goodness to Israel’s descendants,
\q1 which he’s shown us according to his mercy,
\q1 and according to his incredible loyal commitment,
\p
\v 8 and he said, “They’re definitely my people—
\q1 children who wouldn’t be disloyal,”
\q1 and he became their rescuer.
\q1
\v 9 In all their suffering, he also suffered,
\q1 and his messenger went ahead of him to rescue them.
\q1 In his love and mercy, he paid the price for their freedom,
\q1 through that era, he lifted them up and carried them.
\q1
\v 10 However, they rebelled and grieved his holy spirit,
\q1 →so he turned into their enemy who fought against them.
\b
\q1
\v 11 Then his people recalled that era with Moses,
\q1 Where is the one who brought them up from the sea
\q1 along with those shepherds of his flock?
\q1 Where is the one who sent his holy spirit to be among \add them\add*,
\q2
\v 12 who transferred his glorious power to Moses,
\q1 \add who then\add* divided the sea in front of them
\q1 to gain himself a lasting reputation,
\q1
\v 13 who led them through the depths?
\q1 Like a horse in the wilderness, they didn’t stumble.
\q1
\v 14 Like cattle going down a valley, Yahweh’s spirit gave them peace,
\q1 →so you led your people to give yourself an incredible reputation.
\s1 The mercy
\q1
\v 15 Look from heaven
\q1 and see from your wonderful, holy residence.
\q1 Where’s your actions \add full of\add* zeal and power?
\q1 Your inner motivation and your compassion have restrained themselves from me.
\q1
\v 16 Yes, you are our father, although Abraham doesn’t know us
\q1 and Israel doesn’t recognise us.
\q1 You, Yahweh, are our father
\q1 and the one who bought us our freedom long ago.
\q1
\v 17 Yahweh, why do you make us wander from your paths—
\q1 hardening our hearts so we don’t want to obey you.
\q1 Return for your servants’ sake—
\q1 the tribes who are your inheritance.
\q1
\v 18 For a while, your holy people possessed your sanctuary.
\q1 \add Now\add* our enemies have trampled over your sacred place.
\q1
\v 19 We’ve become like those who you never ruled over—
\q1 like those who never called to you for help.
\c 64
\p
\v 1 If you’d torn the skies open, \add then\add* had come down,
\q1 the mountains would have shaken in front of you.
\q1
\v 2 Just as fire ignites twigs and boils water,
\q1 making your name known to your enemies \add will make\add* the nations tremble in front of you.
\q1
\v 3 When you did amazing things that we weren’t expecting, you came down.
\q1 The mountains shook in front of you.
\q1
\v 4 Since ancient times, no one has heard,
\q1 and people’s ears haven’t listened \add to anything like that\add*.
\q1 No eye has seen any god other than you,
\q1 →who takes action for those who wait, \add expecting his help\add*.
\q1
\v 5 You meet the one who is happy and who does what is right—
\q1 who remembers your instructions.
\q1 Listen, you were angry because we sinned over a long period
\q1 ^but now we will be saved.
\q1
\v 6 We’ve all become like \add someone\add* ‘unclean’
\q1 and all the good things we do are like a \add used\add* menstrual rag.
\q1 \add Because of\add* our acts of disobedience,
\q1 all of us have been like leaves that withered and were carried off like the wind.
\b
\q1
\v 7 There’s no one \add left\add* who calls on your name—
\q1 no one who stirs themself up to take hold of you,
\q1 because you’ve hidden yourself from us
\q1 and allowed our acts of disobedience to dissolve us away.
\q1
\v 8 But now, Yahweh, you are our father.
\q1 We are the clay, and you’re our potter,
\q1 and all of us are your handiwork.
\q1
\v 9 Don’t be excessively angry, Yahweh,
\q1 and don’t remember our disobedience forever.
\q1 Wow, please look \add here\add*—all of us are your people.
\q1
\v 10 Your sacred cities have become a wilderness:
\q1 Zion has become a wilderness.
\q1 Jerusalem became a desolation.
\q1
\v 11 Our sacred and beautiful temple, where our ancestors praised you,
\q1 has \add now\add* been burnt,
\q1 and everything that we liked there is in ruins.
\q1
\v 12 After those, will you restrain yourself, Yahweh?
\q1 Will you remain silent as you make us suffer so much?
\c 65
\s1 Judgement from Yahweh
\p
\v 1 “I let myself be consulted by \add those who\add* hadn’t asked,
\q1 I let myself be found by \add those who\add* hadn’t searched for me.
\q1 I said, ‘Here I am. Here I am.’ to a nation that wasn’t \add even\add* calling out my name.
\q1
\v 2 All day \add long,\add* I held my hands out to a rebellious people,
\q1 who do just what they want rather than walking the good path—
\q1
\v 3 a people who continually provoke me to anger.
\q1 They regularly sacrifice in gardens
\q1 and burn incense on brick \add altars\add*.
\q1
\v 4 They sit among graves and spend the night in \add such\add* places.
\q1 They eat pork, and soup made from ‘unclean’ meat,
\q1
\v 5 saying, ‘Don’t come near me because I’m more holy than you!’
\q1 Those people make me angry \add like\add* a fire burning all day \add long\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 6 “Listen, it’s written in front of me.
\q1 I won’t stay silent because I’ll repay—
\q1 I’ll repay where it hurts—
\q1
\v 7 your disobedience and that of your ancestors together.”
\q1 Yahweh says, “They burnt incense on the mountains,
\q1 and taunted me on the hills,
\q1 so I’ll measure out their wages where it hurts.”
\b
\q1
\v 8 This is what Yahweh says:
\q1 When juice is found in \add some\add* grapes \add on the vine\add*,
\q1 →then \add someone\add* will say, ‘Don’t destroy it because there’s good in it,’
\q1 so too for the sake of my servants, I won’t destroy everyone.
\q1
\v 9 I’ll bring descendants out of Jacob,
\q1 and someone to possess my mountains out of Judah,
\q1 and my chosen ones will take possession of it,
\q1 and my servants will live there.
\q1
\v 10 The Sharon \add plain\add* will become a pasture for the flocks,
\q1 and the Akor valley will be a resting place for cattle
\q1 for my people who search for me.
\b
\q1
\v 11 But you all who abandon Yahweh,
\q1 who forget my sacred mountain,
\q1 who prepare a table for Gad,
\q1 and fill mixed wine for Meni,
\q1
\v 12 I’ll appoint you all for the sword.
\q1 You’ll all bow down for slaughter
\q1 because I called and you all didn’t answer.
\q1 I spoke and you all didn’t listen,
\q1 and did what I considered to be evil
\q1 and you chose what I didn’t like.
\p
\v 13 So my master, Yahweh, says this:
\q1 Listen, my servants will eat, but you’ll all be hungry.
\q1 Look, my servants will drink, but you’ll all be thirsty.
\q1 Watch, my servants will celebrate, but you’ll all be put to shame.
\q1
\v 14 Listen, my servants will shout happily as they celebrate,
\q1 ^but you’ll all cry out from mental anguish,
\q2 and you’ll all wail because of your broken spirits.
\q1
\v 15 Your names will remain in your language as curse \add words\add* for my chosen ones,
\q1 but my master Yahweh will put you to death,
\q1 and then he will call his servants \add by\add* another name.
\q1
\v 16 Anyone who blesses themself on earth, will bless themself by the god of certainty,
\q1 and anyone who makes a vow on earth, will promise by the god of certainty,
\q1 because their former troubles will be forgotten because they’ll be hidden from my eyes.
\s1 Yahweh’s new creation
\p
\v 17 Listen, I’m about to create new skies and a new earth,
\q1 and then the previous things won’t be remembered or thought about
\q1
\v 18 because you’ll celebrate and be forever happy with what I’m about to create,
\q1 because listen, I’m about to create a happy Jerusalem with celebrating people.
\q1
\v 19 Then I’ll celebrate about Jerusalem and be happy about my people,
\q1 and the \add sounds\add* of weeping and distress will no longer be heard there.
\q1
\v 20 There won’t be any more babies \add die\add* there at a few days old,
\q1 or an elderly person who doesn’t enjoy their full life.
\q1 Someone who’s a hundred years old will \add seem like\add* a young man,
\q1 and someone who doesn’t reach one hundred will be \add considered\add* cursed.
\q1
\v 21 They’ll build houses and live in them,
\q1 and they’ll plant vineyards and \add get to\add* eat their fruit.
\q1
\v 22 They won’t build then \add end up\add* having someone else live there,
\q1 and they won’t plant then \add end up\add* with someone else eating \add the produce\add*,
\q1 because my people will live as long as trees,
\q1 and my chosen ones will fully enjoy everything they’ve worked at.
\q1
\v 23 Their work won’t be wasted, and they won’t end up discouraged,
\q1 →because they’ll be Yahweh’s children who are blessed, along with their descendants.
\q1
\v 24 Before they \add even\add* call out, I’ll answer them—
\q1 while they’re still speaking, I’ll hear them.
\q1
\v 25 The wolf and the lamb will graze together,
\q1 and the lion will eat straw like the cow.
\q1 The snake’s food will be dirt and they won’t cause any harm,
\q1 and nothing will be destroyed on my sacred mountain says Yahweh.
\c 66
\s1 Hypocrisy in worship
\p
\v 1 Yahweh says this:
\q1 The heavens are my throne and the earth is my footstool.
\q1 Where \add then,\add* could you all build a house for me,
\q1 and where could be a place for me to relax?
\q1
\v 2 My hand made all those things
\q1 and they came to be \add by\add* Yahweh’s declaration.
\q1 The people that I take notice of
\q1 are those who are humble and contrite,
\q1 and who sincerely respect and honour my message.
\b
\q1
\v 3 The same person who slaughters a bull \add (to sacrifice)\add*, also murders a person.
\q1 The one who sacrifices a lamb, breaks a dog’s neck.
\q1 Someone who brings up a grain offering, also brings pigs’ blood.
\q1 A person who offers incense as a memorial, blesses wickedness as well.
\q1 →They’ve chosen their own ways, and enjoy their detestable behaviour.
\q1
\v 4 So I will choose their punishment,
\q1 →and I’ll cause their fears to come to be.
\q1 because I called but no one answered.
\q1 I spoke and no one listened.
\q1 They did evil things as I watched,
\q1 and chose \add to do\add* what I didn’t like.
\b
\q1
\v 5 Listen to Yahweh’s message \add you\add* who respect and honour it.
\q1 Your relatives who hate you \add and\add* who exclude you all for my name’s sake, have said,
\q1 ‘Let Yahweh be honoured so we can see your happiness,’
\q1 however, they’ll be put to shame.
\q1
\v 6 There’s a sound of uproar from the city—
\q1 noise coming from the temple:
\q1 the sound of Yahweh giving his enemies what they deserve.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Before it went into labour, it \add already\add* gave birth.
\q1 Before the pain hit it, it delivered a son.
\q1
\v 8 Who’s heard anything like that \add before\add*?
\q1 Who’s seen those \add sorts of\add* things?
\q1 Can a land be given birth to in one day?
\q1 Can a nation be born in an instant,
\q1 →because Zion gave birth to her children \add so soon as\add* she went into labour?
\q1
\v 9 “Do I bring \add a baby\add* to the birth \add canal\add* and then not deliver it?” Yahweh asks.
\q1 “Or do I bring it \add to the point of delivery\add* and then hold it back?” asks your god.
\q1
\v 10 Celebrate with Jerusalem all of you who love it, and be glad.
\q1 Happily celebrate with it, all of you who mourned over it,
\q1
\v 11 because you’ll all suckle and be satisfied from her comforting breasts.
\q1 Yes, you’ll all drink deeply and delight yourselves from her gushing nipple,
\p
\v 12 because Yahweh says this:
\q1 Listen, I’m about to extend peace to Jerusalem like a river,
\q1 and the wealth of the nations to her like an overflowing stream.
\q1 →Then you’ll all suckle \add and\add* be carried on the hip,
\q1 and bounced on her knees.
\q1
\v 13 I’ll comfort you all like a mother comforts her child,
\q1 and you’ll all be comforted in Jerusalem.
\q1
\v 14 You’ll all see that and it’ll warm your heart,
\q1 and your bones will sprout like the grass.
\q1 Yahweh will reveal his power to his servants,
\q1 and he’ll be indignant at his enemies,
\b
\q1
\v 15 because listen, Yahweh will come in fire with his chariots like a windstorm,
\q1 →to bring his raging anger and to rebuke with flames of fire.
\q1
\v 16 Yes, Yahweh judges all humanity with fire and his sword,
\q1 and many people will be killed by him.
\p
\v 17 Those who consecrate themselves, and purify themselves for the gardens,
\q1 and those who eat pork, rats, and other detestable things, will all be destroyed together.
\m That is Yahweh’s declaration.
\p
\v 18 I \add know\add* their thoughts and what they’ve done. \add The time\add* is coming to gather all the nations and the language \add groups\add*, and they’ll come and see my glory.
\v 19 I’ll perform a miracle among them, and will send survivors from them to the nations: Tarshish, Put, Lud (home of archers), Tuval, Greece, and to the distant islands where they haven’t heard about me or seen my splendour. They’ll tell \add other\add* nations about my greatness.
\v 20 Then they’ll bring all your relatives back from all the nations to my sacred mountain Jerusalem, on horses, and in chariots and wagons, and on mules and camels, as an offering to Yahweh like how the Israelites bring a grain offering in a clean vessel into the house of Yahweh.
\m That’s what Yahweh says.
\p
\v 21 Then I’ll choose some of them to be priests and temple workers.
\m That’s what Yahweh says.
\p
\v 22 Just as the new skies and the new earth will stand in front of me,
\q1 so your descendants and your name will endure.
\m That’s what Yahweh declares.
\q1
\v 23 Then from month to month and week to week,
\q1 all creatures will come to bow down in front of me.
\m That’s what Yahweh says.
\p
\v 24 Then they’ll go out and see the corpses of those who rebelled against me,
\q1 because the maggots won’t die and their fire won’t be extinguished,
\q1 and all creatures will \add find them\add* abhorrent.
